window.FLOWER_SEARCH = [{"t":"Introduction","h":"Introduction","u":"index.html","d":1,"x":"Flower's code and documentation are open source under the MIT license . Flower is a replicated database of source records and reactive derived values. You write the application logic in Rust, compile it to WebAssembly, deploy it into a cluster, and call it by name over HTTP. Explore Flower’s source and examples in the GitHub repository . These docs cover the current implementation. These pages are for developers building applications on Flower: writing guest modules with flower-sdk , deploying them with cargo flower , calling them from any HTTP client or from Rust with flower-client , and running the native workers and clusters that serve them. Read them top to bottom the first time — the qu","k":"flower-sdk cargo flower flower-client"},{"t":"Introduction","h":"The shape of an application","u":"index.html#the-shape-of-an-application","d":0,"x":"A Flower application is a module : a Rust crate built as a wasm32-unknown-unknown cdylib . Each operation and record type is declared once, with everything about it, and one static App::new(&[…]) lists the module's components: Collections of typed records, with indexes and references the engine maintains for you. Queries , which read, and mutations , which read and write. Both are plain synchronous Rust functions, each declaring its own name, consistency, access and deadline. Derived values and aggregates : computations the engine caches, tracks the dependencies of, and keeps current as records change. HTTP exposure , opted into per operation: nothing answers over HTTP unless its declaration","k":"wasm32-unknown-unknown cdylib App::new(&[…]) http"},{"t":"Introduction","h":"What Flower guarantees","u":"index.html#what-flower-guarantees","d":0,"x":"Atomic mutations. A mutation's writes, the index entries they imply and the derived values that must change with them are published together, or not at all. Linearizable reads by default. A query declared Linearizable sees every write that completed before it began, whichever member serves it. Queries can opt into cheaper replica-local reads , with or without a staleness bound. Exactly-once effects for retried writes. A mutation sent again with the same request ID answers the original outcome instead of running twice — see retries and receipts . Isolation between modules. A module exports nothing unless it says so; another module can read only what it exports and can never write its collecti","k":"Linearizable"},{"t":"Introduction","h":"Where to go next","u":"index.html#where-to-go-next","d":0,"x":"Quickstart — install cargo flower , write a to-do module, serve it on one node and call it with curl . Concepts — the data model, methods, derived values, consistency, retries, watches, time, queues, modules and transactions. Guides — calling Flower from Rust, authentication, cryptography, workers, deployments, backups, operations and testing. Reference — the HTTP API , the cargo flower CLI , the SDK , and error codes and limits .","k":"cargo flower curl"},{"t":"Introduction","h":"The crates you will use","u":"index.html#the-crates","d":0,"x":"Crate Where it runs What it is for flower-sdk Your guest (Wasm) Types, schemas, collections, contexts and components for writing modules. no_std plus alloc , no runtime dependencies. cargo-flower Your machine, your servers The cargo flower command: build and inspect guests, plan and deploy them, call methods, and serve a cluster node. flower-client Native Rust An HTTP client with retries, deadlines, watches, transactions and admin calls, which calls methods type-checked through the descriptors your guest crate declares. flower-worker Native Rust Queue workers with leases, concurrency control and graceful draining, and the reconcile worker for external values. The Flower repository also carri","k":"flower-sdk no_std alloc cargo-flower cargo flower serve flower-client flower-worker reconcile examples/ goblin-pizza crypto-ticket external-transactions typed-modules-*"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"Quickstart","u":"quickstart.html","d":1,"x":"Build a to-do module in Rust, serve it on a one-node cluster, and call it over HTTP. Every command and output on this page was run against the current Flower code.","k":""},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"Prerequisites","u":"quickstart.html#prerequisites","d":0,"x":"Rust 1.96 or newer with the WebAssembly target: rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown . A C compiler , for the zstd codec the server links. On Windows on ARM64, also install LLVM ( winget install LLVM.LLVM ) and put its bin directory on PATH before building: the TLS library's assembly needs clang . A checkout of the Flower repository. The crates are not published to crates.io; your guest depends on flower-sdk by path.","k":"rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown winget install LLVM.LLVM bin PATH clang flower-sdk"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"1. Install cargo flower","u":"quickstart.html#install","d":0,"x":"From the root of the Flower checkout: This installs the cargo-flower binary, which Cargo runs as cargo flower . The same binary builds guests, deploys them, calls them and serves cluster nodes. A release build takes a few minutes the first time: it compiles the whole server, including the Wasm compiler.","k":"cargo install --path crates/cargo-flower cargo-flower cargo flower cargo install path crates"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"2. Write a module","u":"quickstart.html#write-a-module","d":0,"x":"Create a library crate next to your Flower checkout: A guest is a cdylib that depends on flower-sdk and nothing else. Building it as an rlib too lets tests and Rust clients link its types: Cargo.toml Replace src/lib.rs with the module. It declares a tasks collection indexed by whether a task is done, two mutations and one query, all three callable over HTTP: src/lib.rs A few things to notice: #[derive(FlowerType)] gives Task a schema and a codec. The wire names are the Rust field names. #[collection] turns the Tasks marker into the collection's typed handle — get , set and delete by key, and a typed BY_DONE index handle — and into the component the app lists. Keys are values of the declared ","k":"cargo new --lib tasks cd tasks cdylib flower-sdk rlib src/lib.rs tasks #[derive(FlowerType)] Task #[collection] Tasks get set delete BY_DONE Tasks::get(ctx, &id) String #[mutation] #[query] ADD_TASK AddTaskArgs ctx http #[mutation(name = \"tasks.add\", http)] App::new export!(APP) cargo package name version edition crate-type dependencies path flower crates to-do list Flower guest: collection index mutations query exposed over HTTP. extern crate alloc alloc::string::String alloc::vec::Vec flower_sdk:: FlowerType MutationCtx QueryCtx Result export fail mutation task stored JSON object title done derive Clone Debug PartialEq struct title: done: bool indexed whether they done. value by_done fields types What open_tasks returns each task. OpenTask add_task ctx: Tasks::get is_some return TASK_EXISTS with this already exists false Tasks::set complete_task Some else TASK_NOT_FOUND task.done true page Tasks::BY_DONE.prefix rows into_iter row.key row.value.title collect static APP: COMPLETE_TASK OPEN_TASKS"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"3. Build it","u":"quickstart.html#build","d":0,"x":"build compiles the crate for wasm32-unknown-unknown in release mode, then loads the result through the same Wasm backend the server uses and checks its imports, exports and manifest. Nothing is written unless that inspection passes. The manifest — collections, schemas, methods and the names callable over HTTP ( aliases ) — is embedded in the module; the .manifest.json copy is for you to read and diff.","k":"cargo flower build build wasm32-unknown-unknown aliases .manifest.json cargo flower built tasks wasm tasks.wasm manifest tasks.manifest.json sha256 f3eca4e1f4e308ced6fd1684a06156c19a6664e6364c58cd8c168bd590d2fee8 collections queries mutations derived transactions triggers http"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"4. Start a node","u":"quickstart.html#serve","d":0,"x":"In a second terminal: --dir is where the node keeps its data; --id is its member ID. --bootstrap 1 founds a new one-member cluster on first start. It is ignored once the directory is initialized, so restarting with the same command is safe. --admin-token enables the operator endpoints, deployment included. Without it every admin request is refused with 403. The node serves HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 on port 8080 and Raft on port 9000, both on loopback. It logs at info ; set RUST_LOG=warn for a quieter terminal. Check that it elected itself: Client commands talk to http://127.0.0.1:8080 unless you pass --endpoint or set FLOWER_ENDPOINT .","k":"listening raft=127.0.0.1:9000 listening http=127.0.0.1:8080 --dir --id --bootstrap 1 --admin-token info RUST_LOG=warn cargo flower status http://127.0.0.1:8080 --endpoint FLOWER_ENDPOINT cargo flower serve data bootstrap admin-token secret listening raft http status state Leader leader Some term last_log last_applied"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"5. Deploy","u":"quickstart.html#deploy","d":0,"x":"This creates the database demo and deploys the artifact into it as the module tasks . The deployment went through the Raft log (position 3); it did not move the database's revision, which counts data changes. You could deploy the same file again as another module, or into another database.","k":"revision=0 position=3 demo tasks cargo flower deploy module wasm tasks.wasm admin-token secret revision position"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"6. Call it","u":"quickstart.html#call","d":0,"x":"Mutations go to POST /v1/mutate ; the body names the database, module, method and arguments: The answer carries the method's return value, the database revision the write produced, and the log position it committed at. Send exactly the same request again: Because the request carried an idempotency_key , the server recognized the retry and answered the original outcome instead of running add_task again — note \"duplicate\":true and the unchanged revision. Without a key, the second call would run and fail with the module's own error: A method's own failure answers 422, with \"ok\":false and your code and message. Nothing it wrote was kept and the revision did not move; the log entry at position 5 ","k":"POST /v1/mutate idempotency_key add_task \"duplicate\":true \"ok\":false {\"id\":\"bread\",\"title\":\"Bake bread\"} POST /v1/query Server-Timing --mutation /v1/mutate curl http: mutate content-type: application json demo module tasks name args milk title add-milk true value done false revision position duplicate HTTP Unprocessable Entity code TASK_EXISTS message task with this already exists query open_tasks x-flower-leader-url: server-timing: cache desc miss fence admission eval total node leader x-flower-group: root bread Bake cargo flower call complete_task mutation"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"7. Watch a query","u":"quickstart.html#watch","d":0,"x":"A watch keeps a query's result current over server-sent events. POST /v1/watch takes exactly the body of /v1/query : Leave it running and, in another terminal, add a task and complete one. Each change that alters the query's result produces a new snapshot: Add \"deltas\":true to the body to receive JSON Patch events instead, whenever a patch is smaller than the value: The engine knows which index range open_tasks read, so writes that cannot change its result never wake the watch. See Watches .","k":"POST /v1/watch /v1/query \"deltas\":true open_tasks curl http: watch content-type: application json demo module tasks name args event: snapshot data: revision value bread title Bake eggs patch base path"},{"t":"Quickstart","h":"Next steps","u":"quickstart.html#next-steps","d":0,"x":"Open http://127.0.0.1:8080/admin and paste the admin token to see the node's dashboard: Raft state, databases, queues, deployments and live activity. Read Collections and indexes and Queries and mutations to go beyond this module. Call the module from Rust with flower-client , through the descriptors #[mutation] and #[query] declared, and test it in-process without building Wasm. Run three nodes instead of one: Operating a cluster . Compiled examples The module on this page, and every other example in these docs, lives in web/docs/examples of the Flower repository, where it is compiled against the current SDK and its apps are deployed into the engine by tests. web/docs/README.md has the comm","k":"http://127.0.0.1:8080/admin flower-client #[mutation] #[query] web/docs/examples web/docs/README.md"},{"t":"Databases, modules and groups","h":"Databases, modules and groups","u":"concepts/databases.html","d":1,"x":"Every call names a logical database and a module. Logical databases are the unit of atomicity; modules are namespaces of code inside them; Raft groups are where databases physically live.","k":""},{"t":"Databases, modules and groups","h":"Logical databases","u":"concepts/databases.html#logical-databases","d":0,"x":"A logical database is an atomic application-state domain. It holds source records, indexes and derived values, the deployed code of its modules, its timers, queues and retry receipts, its catalog of managed keys , and one revision counter that moves with each committed change. Every HTTP call carries its name in db ; every cargo flower command that touches application state takes --db . You do not create databases explicitly. A database comes into being the first time you deploy a module into it. Names are yours to choose: one database per application, per environment, or per tenant are all reasonable. A mutation is atomic within its database. Work spanning several databases goes through a t","k":"db cargo flower --db"},{"t":"Databases, modules and groups","h":"Modules","u":"concepts/databases.html#modules","d":0,"x":"A module is one deployed Wasm artifact inside a database, under a name you choose at deploy time. The same artifact can be deployed under several names, or into several databases. Each module has its own collections, methods, derived values and HTTP names; a collection called tasks in module crm is unrelated to one called tasks in module jobs . Modules share their database's revision and commit atomically with each other: a mutation in one module that calls an exported mutation in another commits both modules' writes in one entry. They are capability boundaries, not consensus domains. A module exports nothing unless its declarations say so ( export ); other modules reach it only through what","k":"tasks crm jobs export http \"\" CollectionRef::local(\"tasks\") CollectionRef::new(\"\", \"tasks\")"},{"t":"Databases, modules and groups","h":"Named partitions and groups","u":"concepts/databases.html#named-partitions-and-groups","d":0,"x":"A server process can host several Raft groups . Each group has one ordered log and one leader at a time. The root group exists from bootstrap; it spans every server process and holds the partition catalog. Every database the catalog does not place lives in root . An operator can create more groups on a subset of the servers, and place a database on one of them, which makes it a named partition . A partition is still addressed by its database name: clients never name groups. The server that receives a call looks the database up in the catalog and routes the call to the group that owns it, forwarding to that group's leader when needed. Responses for placed databases carry x-flower-group and x-","k":"root x-flower-group x-flower-partition-epoch acme cargo flower group create cargo flower partition create 503 partition_moving tenant_id"},{"t":"Databases, modules and groups","h":"Replicas, leaders and followers","u":"concepts/databases.html#replicas","d":0,"x":"Each member of a group keeps a full copy of the group's databases. The leader runs mutations and appends their accepted consequences to the log; followers apply those consequences without running guest code. Any member answers queries, according to the consistency each query declares (see Revisions and consistency ), and any member accepts writes, forwarding them to the leader.","k":""},{"t":"Databases, modules and groups","h":"Identities worth knowing","u":"concepts/databases.html#identities","d":0,"x":"Identity What it identifies Where you meet it Database name The owner of application state and the target of every call. db in every request. Revision A per-database counter that moves with each committed change. Not comparable across databases. Mutation results, watch events. Position The log index of the entry in the owning group's log. Restarts after a restore. Mutation and transaction results. History incarnation One unbroken history of a database. A restore to an earlier point starts a new one. Scoped retry IDs, wrong_history refusals. Placement epoch Which group currently owns a partition. x-flower-partition-epoch . Code identity The SHA-256 content identity of a module's Wasm and mani","k":"db wrong_history x-flower-partition-epoch cargo flower build plan modules"},{"t":"Collections and indexes","h":"Collections and indexes","u":"concepts/collections.html","d":1,"x":"Source records live in collections: typed rows under unique keys. The engine validates every write against the collection's schemas and maintains its indexes and references in the same commit.","k":""},{"t":"Collections and indexes","h":"Values and typed contracts","u":"concepts/collections.html#values-and-types","d":0,"x":"Everything that crosses the guest boundary — arguments, results, stored rows, keys — is a JSON-compatible Value : null, booleans, finite numbers, strings, arrays and objects with unique keys, at most 128 levels deep. In Rust you rarely touch Value directly. Instead you derive FlowerType , which gives a type a schema (used for validation and for module contracts), an encoder and a decoder: The rules the derive follows: Wire names are field names , or their camelCase forms when the struct says #[flower(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] , for clients speaking JavaScript conventions. A raw identifier is plain on the wire ( r#type is type ), and two fields answering to one wire name are a compile error.","k":"Value FlowerType camelCase #[flower(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] r#type type INVALID_VALUE Option<T> null Option #[flower(default)] Default::default() #[flower(default = path)] #[flower(skip)] Default {\"tag\": \"Variant\", \"value\": …} i64 u64 typed::DecimalI64 DecimalU64 () bool String f64 Vec Box TYPE_MISMATCH MISSING_FIELD SCHEMA_MISMATCH INVALID_ARGUMENT details.path [\"items\", 1, \"count\"] project. Wire names Rust field names. derive Clone Debug PartialEq struct Project name: unit enum closed string enum: normal high Copy Priority Normal High Task project: owner: title: priority: time epoch milliseconds. due: visible value: member must present. notes: Absent members decode Vec::new flower default tags:"},{"t":"Collections and indexes","h":"Declaring a collection","u":"concepts/collections.html#declaring-a-collection","d":0,"x":"The #[collection] attribute declares a collection on a marker struct, with its key type, row type, indexes and references. The marker is both the typed handle and the component the app lists: For each marker the macro generates: Item What it is Tasks::NAME The collection name, \"tasks\" . Tasks::get(ctx, &id) Read one row by key, decoded: Result<Option<Task>> . Works in any context. Tasks::set(ctx, &id, &task) Encode and upsert. Mutations only. Tasks::delete(ctx, &id) Delete one row. Mutations only. Tasks::page(ctx, limit, after) One page of the whole collection in host order. Tasks::SOURCE A typed Source<K, V> read handle: get , page , and in_module to read another module's exported collectio","k":"#[collection] Tasks::NAME \"tasks\" Tasks::get(ctx, &id) Result<Option<Task>> Tasks::set(ctx, &id, &task) Tasks::delete(ctx, &id) Tasks::page(ctx, limit, after) Tasks::SOURCE Source<K, V> get page in_module Tasks::BY_OWNER_DUE Index Tasks::raw() Collection App::new(&[&Tasks, ...]) export access = policy collection name projects String value Project struct Projects tasks Task index by_owner_due fields owner types field reference needs whose start with own. by_project project target on_delete cascade Tasks"},{"t":"Collections and indexes","h":"Keys","u":"concepts/collections.html#keys","d":0,"x":"A key is a value of the collection's key type: a String , a number, or a tuple for a compound key. The handles take it as it is: On the wire and in storage a key is its canonical JSON rendering — the string key milk is \"milk\" , the tuple key (\"acme\", 7) is [\"acme\",7] — so every key has exactly one form, and keys of every type order consistently in scans. The SDK renders keys for you; the raw context operations take keys as Value s (anything FlowerType encodes to one) and render them the same way. Scan and range results hand keys back as decoded values ( row.key is a String above), so you never parse key text. Host order Pages come back in the host's storage order: shorter key text first, the","k":"String milk \"milk\" (\"acme\", 7) [\"acme\",7] Value FlowerType row.key \"bread\" mutation rename_task ctx: MutationCtx title: Result bool Some task Tasks::get else return false task.title title Tasks::set true"},{"t":"Collections and indexes","h":"Indexes and range queries","u":"concepts/collections.html#indexes-and-ranges","d":0,"x":"An index names one or more fields of the row; the engine maintains its entries atomically with every write. types(...) gives the typed handle the fields' Rust types, so prefixes and bounds are type-checked; without it the components are Value s. A range fixes a prefix of the indexed fields, then optionally bounds the next field from below ( gt / gte ) and above ( lt / lte ), and returns a page of at most limit rows with a continuation cursor: prefix(()) — or Tasks::BY_OWNER_DUE.query() — ranges over the whole index; a prefix covering every field takes no bounds and returns the rows equal to it. .reverse() walks the range descending; .after(cursor) continues a previous page. The host checks t","k":"types(...) Value gt gte lt lte limit prefix(()) Tasks::BY_OWNER_DUE.query() .reverse() .after(cursor) INVALID_VALUE DecimalI64 DecimalU64 derive FlowerType Clone Debug PartialEq struct DuePage titles: String Pass back after next page null last. next: Option query due_between ctx: QueryCtx owner: since: until: after: Result range Tasks::BY_OWNER_DUE.prefix owner since until Some cursor range.after range.page page.rows.into_iter row.value.title collect page.cursor project_names names Vec::new None loop Projects::page after.as_deref names.extend row.value.name match return"},{"t":"Collections and indexes","h":"References","u":"concepts/collections.html#references","d":0,"x":"A reference declares a foreign key from this collection to another. The engine checks it on every write and acts on it when the target row is deleted: on_delete Deleting a referenced row… restrict (default) fails while any row still refers to it. cascade deletes the referring rows too, in the same commit. set_null sets the referring fields to null. The reference can sit in named fields ( fields(project) ), in the whole key ( key ) or in a key prefix ( key_prefix ). A field reference can be polymorphic: targets(Posts, Comments) with a discriminator field naming which collection each row points to. Targets name the marker structs of collections in scope. Cascading deletes and triggers abort wi","k":"reference on_delete restrict cascade set_null fields(project) key key_prefix targets(Posts, Comments) discriminator cascade_too_deep by_project #[collection] Collection"},{"t":"Collections and indexes","h":"Writes in detail","u":"concepts/collections.html#writes","d":0,"x":"Inside a mutation, reads see the mutation's own staged writes. Nothing is visible to anyone else until the mutation returns successfully and the engine commits. Besides set and delete , the mutation context offers: delete_if_match(collection, &key, &expected) — delete only when the stored row equals expected ; returns whether it did. delete_permanently(collection, &key) — delete without running retention hooks , for the row and the deletes it cascades into. set_clearing(collection, &key, value, &[\"field\"]) — upsert and remove named fields. A caller whose access policy hides fields from it otherwise keeps their stored values when it writes the row back. These are raw operations: collection is","k":"set delete delete_if_match(collection, &key, &expected) expected delete_permanently(collection, &key) set_clearing(collection, &key, value, &[\"field\"]) collection CollectionRef CollectionRef::local(Tasks::NAME) Value &Value::String(id) id.encode()? null None"},{"t":"Collections and indexes","h":"Raw, untyped collections","u":"concepts/collections.html#raw-collections","d":0,"x":"The typed layer sits on a raw one you can use directly for deliberately dynamic code. Collection::new(\"orders\").indexes(&[IndexDef::new(\"by_shop\", &[\"shop\"])]) declares a collection without schemas — list the static among the app's components like a marker — and the context's get , set , delete , scan and range work on Value keys and rows addressed by CollectionRef::local(\"orders\") . Host enforcement — schemas, references, access rules — is the same whichever layer you use.","k":"get set delete scan range Value CollectionRef::local(\"orders\")"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"Queries and mutations","u":"concepts/methods.html","d":1,"x":"Methods are plain synchronous Rust functions. Queries read one snapshot; mutations read and write, and commit atomically. Each is declared once, with everything about it — its name, whether clients may call it over HTTP, who may call it, its consistency and its deadline — and an App lists them.","k":"App"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"Queries","u":"concepts/methods.html#queries","d":0,"x":"#[query] adapts a function whose first parameter is ctx: &mut QueryCtx<'_, '_> . The remaining parameters become the fields of the method's JSON arguments object, and the return type becomes its result contract: The macro keeps your function as it is — you can still call balance(ctx, account) from Rust — and adds two items: a public argument struct named after the function ( BalanceArgs , one field per parameter after ctx ), which derives FlowerType : callers in Rust build it instead of writing JSON; a static named after the function in upper case ( BALANCE ), an Op<BalanceArgs, Option<u64>, Failure> . It is the component the app lists, and it carries the method's name, kind, argument, resul","k":"#[query] ctx: &mut QueryCtx<'_, '_> balance(ctx, account) BalanceArgs ctx FlowerType BALANCE Op<BalanceArgs, Option<u64>, Failure> INVALID_ARGUMENT details.path Option<T> self BALANCE_NEARBY .named(..) infallible query: return type result contract. query http greet ctx: QueryCtx name: String alloc::format Hello name fallible returning Result with deadline. timeout balance account: Option account Accounts::get account.map account.balance same callback under another served from replica state most seconds stale. static BALANCE_NEARBY: named balance_nearby consistency Consistency::ReplicaLocalWithin _000"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"Mutations","u":"concepts/methods.html#mutations","d":0,"x":"#[mutation] works the same way with a MutationCtx . A mutation can do everything a query can, plus write the module's own collections, call other modules' exported mutations, enqueue jobs, schedule timers and draw random bytes. Everything it does is staged; when it returns Ok , the engine validates the staged writes, applies their consequences (indexes, references, triggers, derived values) and commits the lot as one log entry. When it returns Err , or traps, or exceeds a budget, nothing it wrote is kept. Mutations run on the group's leader, which prepares several at a time speculatively and commits them in order. A mutation whose reads were invalidated by an earlier one in the same batch is","k":"#[mutation] MutationCtx Ok Err"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"Options","u":"concepts/methods.html#options","d":0,"x":"Everything about an operation goes in its attribute, for queries and mutations alike: #[query(http, timeout = \"500ms\")] . All options are optional: Option Meaning name = \"accounts.balance\" The definition's name, which is also its HTTP name. Default: the function's name. http Callers may reach it over HTTP under its name. Without it, no client can call it over HTTP : timer and queue handlers, trigger targets and methods written for other modules need no exposure, and none is ever public by accident. consistency = replica_local , max_staleness_ms = 2000 Queries only: how fresh a read must be. Linearizable by default. See Revisions and consistency . access = public , authenticated or a check fu","k":"#[query(http, timeout = \"500ms\")] name = \"accounts.balance\" http consistency = replica_local max_staleness_ms = 2000 access = public authenticated default export callers(\"shop\", ...) timeout = \"500ms\" ms s m rename_all = \"camelCase\" camelCase lifetimeSeconds lifetime_seconds bare_arg ctx"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"Declared errors","u":"concepts/methods.html#declared-errors","d":0,"x":"A method that can fail returns Result<T, E> where E is either the SDK's Failure (via the flower_sdk::Result<T> alias, as in the quickstart) or an enum deriving FlowerError . Every variant names a stable code ; a single payload travels as the failure's details : Over HTTP, a method's own failure answers 422 , for queries and mutations alike, with \"ok\":false , the code, the variant's message (its name when it sets none) and the payload as details . A mutation's answer also carries the revision and log position its failed outcome was recorded at: Arguments the method's schema refuses never reach your code. They answer 422 too, with the path of the member at fault: Things to know about errors: C","k":"Result<T, E> E Failure flower_sdk::Result<T> FlowerError code details \"ok\":false UPPER_SNAKE_CASE deadline_exceeded overloaded #[flower(other)] ? From<Failure> map_err #[flower(retryable)] is_retryable() failures transfer declares. Codes discriminate variants payload travels failure derive Clone Debug PartialEq enum TransferError flower ACCOUNT_NOT_FOUND message account this NotFound String INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS balance InsufficientFunds ACCOUNT_LOCKED retryable Locked Every other codec host converts into Host mutation http ctx: MutationCtx from: amount: core::result::Result source Accounts::get from ok_or TransferError::NotFound from.clone target to.clone source.balance amount return TransferError::InsufficientFunds target.balance Accounts::set HTTP Unprocessable Entity false revision position duplicate INVALID_ARGUMENT path arguments invalid account: expected string"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"The App assembly","u":"concepts/methods.html#the-app","d":0,"x":"Nothing is discovered automatically. A module is exactly what its App static lists, in one flat list of components: Component Listed as Queries, mutations and derived values The statics their attributes declare ( &BALANCE ), twins ( &BALANCE_NEARBY ) and raw Method s. Collections The #[collection] marker itself ( &Accounts ). Aggregates, triggers, maintenance tasks, retention hooks Their Derived , Trigger , Task and Retention statics. Queues A TypedQueue static: it declares a queue the module uses. The authorization component An Authorization static, at most one per module. Managed keys Each KeyDeclaration . Imports and interface providers An interface handle ( InventoryApi::import(\"$invento","k":"App &BALANCE &BALANCE_NEARBY Method #[collection] &Accounts Derived Trigger Task Retention TypedQueue Authorization KeyDeclaration InventoryApi::import(\"$inventory\") COMPONENT external! App::new(..).init(f) fn() -> Result<()> flower_sdk::export!(APP); static APP: App::new Accounts GREET BALANCE BALANCE_NEARBY TRANSFER"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"Calling other modules","u":"concepts/methods.html#calling-other-modules","d":0,"x":"A method can call another module's exported methods in the same database. The call runs in the same transaction and snapshot, with the callee's module identity; the callee sees your module as its caller . call (queries) and call_mutation (mutations) are ordinary calls: if the callee fails, your whole invocation fails, even if you handle the Err . The engine poisons the invocation so a half-done callee can never commit. try_call and try_mutation run the callee under a savepoint . A recoverable failure rolls back only the callee's writes and comes back as CallOutcome::Failure ; typed::decode_outcome turns it into your declared error type. Exhausted budgets and other fatal failures still fail t","k":"caller call call_mutation Err try_call try_mutation CallOutcome::Failure typed::decode_outcome CallError::Host typed::call_typed MODULE_PRIVATE through bank module exported transfer turning declared failures into answer instead failing whole call. TransferArgs argument struct mutation derived ctx: MutationCtx from: String amount: Result args from String::from merchant amount outcome match ctx.try_mutation args.encode decode_outcome:: TransferError failure return CallOutcome::Success left alloc::format paid TransferError::InsufficientFunds balance declined: other other: Read another query. here fails invocation even ignores query merchant_balance QueryCtx Value alloc::collections::BTreeMap::new args.insert account Value::String ctx.call Value::Object"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"Who is calling","u":"concepts/methods.html#who-is-calling","d":0,"x":"Query and mutation contexts implement MethodCtx , which adds two reads: ctx.principal() — the Principal the call runs as ( subject , optional tenant , optional claims ), or None for an anonymous call. It is decided by the module's authorization hook . ctx.caller() — the calling module's name for a nested call, or None when the call came from outside.","k":"MethodCtx ctx.principal() Principal subject tenant claims None ctx.caller()"},{"t":"Queries and mutations","h":"Deadlines and limits","u":"concepts/methods.html#deadlines","d":0,"x":"A method's budget is a deadline, stated where it is meant. Each call runs under the method's own timeout — or the operator's default, 5 seconds — capped by the operator's ceiling (60 seconds by default). A caller can only shorten it, by sending x-flower-timeout-ms (the Rust client always does). Everything the call nests shares that one deadline. The engine derives the call's internal instruction and host-operation budget from the method's declaration (or a fixed default) alone, so whether a call fits does not depend on which member runs it or how that member is configured. Running out fails the call with deadline_exceeded (or budget_exhausted for the derived budget): the call's own failure, ","k":"timeout x-flower-timeout-ms deadline_exceeded budget_exhausted caller_deadline_exceeded out_of_memory stack_overflow nesting_too_deep"},{"t":"Derived values and aggregates","h":"Derived values and aggregates","u":"concepts/derived.html","d":1,"x":"A derived value is a computation the engine caches and keeps correct. It records exactly what each evaluation read, and drops the cached result when any of it changes — present rows, absent keys and empty ranges included.","k":""},{"t":"Derived values and aggregates","h":"Derived values","u":"concepts/derived.html#derived-values","d":0,"x":"#[derived] adapts a function taking a DerivedCtx . A derived context can read collections, indexes, other derived values and the clock — nothing else: no writes, no calls to methods, no identity. An instance is identified by the definition name plus its canonical arguments: Like #[query] , the attribute keeps the function and adds an argument struct ( UnshippedArgs ) and a static named after it in upper case ( UNSHIPPED , a Derived ), which you list among the app's components. Its options are name , export (other modules may read it) and materialize (below); a derived value is never called over HTTP directly — queries read it. Read it from any context with ctx.get_derived(module, name, &args","k":"#[derived] DerivedCtx #[query] UnshippedArgs UNSHIPPED Derived name export materialize ctx.get_derived(module, name, &args) \"\" unshipped many customer orders have shipped yet. engine records index range this reads caches result drops when order that changes. derived ctx: customer: String Result page Orders::BY_CUSTOMER.prefix _000 page.rows.iter filter row.value.shipped count Read from query another value same module with argument struct generated. http customer_summary QueryCtx args encode u64::decode ctx.get_derived UNSHIPPED.name"},{"t":"Derived values and aggregates","h":"Materialization","u":"concepts/derived.html#materialization","d":0,"x":"By default an instance is computed on demand and kept in cache while there is room ( Materialize::Never ). A materialized instance is a pinned root: when its dependencies change it is re-evaluated eagerly, at commit, so reads never pay for it. Set the policy in the attribute or on the descriptor: #[derived(materialize = always)] , or .materialize(Materialize::Always) on a Derived static, pins every evaluated instance. #[derived(materialize = each(Orders))] , or .materialize(Materialize::Each(Orders::raw())) , keeps one root per row of a collection of the same module, at arguments {\"key\": <row key>} : inserting a row pins its root, deleting it unpins it. ctx.materialize(module, name, &args) a","k":"Materialize::Never #[derived(materialize = always)] .materialize(Materialize::Always) Derived #[derived(materialize = each(Orders))] .materialize(Materialize::Each(Orders::raw())) {\"key\": <row key>} ctx.materialize(module, name, &args) ctx.unmaterialize(…)"},{"t":"Derived values and aggregates","h":"Aggregates","u":"concepts/derived.html#aggregates","d":0,"x":"An aggregate keeps one accumulator per group of an index, and maintains it by deltas : a write folds the changed row's old image out and its new image in, without reading the group's other rows again. Implement index::TypedAggregate : On the node from the quickstart, with two orders for ada and then one of them reassigned to bob : Group is a full prefix of the index fields: the aggregate groups by every field of the index you name. Declare an index with exactly the fields you want to group by. The first read of a group builds it by folding its rows through add in index order, a page ( PAGE , 256 rows) at a time. After that, writes update it incrementally, in the writing transaction. remove m","k":"index::TypedAggregate ada bob Group add PAGE remove Ok(None) aggregate_context_forbidden VERSION TypedCollection Value CustomerTotals::get(ctx, &(customer,)) totals NAME {\"group\": [<field values>]} DERIVED derive FlowerType Clone Debug PartialEq struct Totals orders: cents: Order count value customer kept deltas: write folds changed order image without scanning other orders. CustomerTotals impl TypedAggregate type Source Orders Fields String Accumulator const NAME: static customer_totals INDEX: by_customer Keep cached groups across deployments that keep this version. VERSION: Option Some initial _customer: Result totals: _key: order: totals.orders totals.cents order.cents Every group stays maintained read ever build one. CUSTOMER_TOTALS: Derived CustomerTotals::DERIVED.materialize Materialize::Always query http ctx: QueryCtx customer: CustomerTotals::get true cents orders after move"},{"t":"Derived values and aggregates","h":"Triggers and retention hooks","u":"concepts/derived.html#triggers-and-retention","d":0,"x":"A trigger runs inside the mutation that changed its source collection, once per net change of a row, with the row's key and its image before and after: Triggers fire when the mutation settles — at an explicit ctx.settle() or at commit — exactly once per (collection, key, after-image) . The before-image is the committed row, unaffected by the mutation's own staged writes. Each firing runs under its own savepoint with the module's rights. A failing trigger rolls back its own writes and fails the mutation. Triggers and reference cascades that trigger each other abort after 16 rounds with cascade_too_deep . The engine fires a trigger as a mutation of the trigger's name, which is why cargo flower","k":"ctx.settle() (collection, key, after-image) cascade_too_deep cargo flower build features::TypedRetention RETENTION delete_permanently Records shipment whenever order flips shipped inside same mutation write that flipped struct RecordShipments impl TypedTrigger type Source Orders const NAME: static record_shipments ctx: MutationCtx key: String before: Option Order after: Result before.is_some_and order.shipped after.is_some_and ctx.clock Shipment order: key.clone Shipments::set RECORD_SHIPMENTS: Trigger RecordShipments::TRIGGER"},{"t":"Derived values and aggregates","h":"Maintenance tasks","u":"concepts/derived.html#maintenance-tasks","d":0,"x":"A maintenance task is periodic work driven by data. due says, as a query, when the task next has work; while that moment is past, the leader runs run , one bounded unit at a time, as a replicated mutation: due must depend only on data and time. Before each run the engine evaluates it again in the run's own transaction, so a unit of work never runs twice. The leader checks tasks after writes, at the earliest declared due time, on leadership changes and at least every --timer-sweep-ms (one second by default). Followers never run tasks; a new leader carries on from replicated state. A failed run rolls back and the task backs off — one second, doubling up to a minute — unless it sets HANDLES_FAI","k":"due run --timer-sweep-ms HANDLES_FAILURES on_error cargo flower modules Deletes shipment records once they page run. struct ForgetShipments const DAY_MS: _400_000.0 impl TypedTask NAME: static forget_shipments earliest moment there work: after oldest this never. ctx: QueryCtx Result Option Shipments::page None rows iter row.value.at DAY_MS reduce f64::min bounded unit work task stays while past. MutationCtx Value ctx.clock forgotten page.rows Shipments::delete row.key Value::Number FORGET_SHIPMENTS: Task ForgetShipments::TASK"},{"t":"Derived values and aggregates","h":"Registering them","u":"concepts/derived.html#registering","d":0,"x":"Derived values, aggregates, triggers and tasks are components like collections and methods. A module is exactly what its App lists:","k":"App Derived values aggregates triggers tasks components like rest. static APP: App::new Orders Shipments PUT_ORDER UNSHIPPED CUSTOMER_TOTALS RECORD_SHIPMENTS FORGET_SHIPMENTS CUSTOMER_SUMMARY TOTALS"},{"t":"Revisions and consistency","h":"Revisions and consistency","u":"concepts/consistency.html","d":1,"x":"Each database has one revision that moves with every committed change. Each query declares how fresh its answer must be — linearizable, or replica-local with or without a bound — and every member serves it accordingly.","k":""},{"t":"Revisions and consistency","h":"Revisions and positions","u":"concepts/consistency.html#revisions","d":0,"x":"A revision is a per-database counter that moves each time a mutation, transaction step, timer firing, task run or queue operation commits a change to that database. It identifies a state of one database; revisions of different databases are unrelated. A few things do not move it: deployments, business failures (which only record their outcome) and caches the engine fills while reading. A position is the index of the log entry a change committed in, in the log of the group that owns the database. Positions are what operators see in backups and Raft metrics; revisions are what applications usually care about. Where you see them: Answer Revision information POST /v1/mutate revision and position","k":"POST /v1/mutate revision position POST /v1/transaction revisions positions POST /v1/watch POST /v1/query {\"ok\":true,\"value\":…}"},{"t":"Revisions and consistency","h":"Optimistic concurrency","u":"concepts/consistency.html#optimistic-concurrency","d":0,"x":"A mutation request may carry expected_revision . If the database has moved past it by the time the mutation commits, the call fails with stale_revision (HTTP 422, with the revision the database is at) and changes nothing: The check is database-wide: any change to the database moves its revision. Learn a revision from a previous mutation answer or a watch event. Most applications do not need this — a mutation's reads are validated at commit anyway — but it is useful for \"save only if nobody changed anything since I looked\" flows across several requests.","k":"expected_revision stale_revision false revision position code message expected database duplicate"},{"t":"Revisions and consistency","h":"Query consistency","u":"concepts/consistency.html#query-consistency","d":0,"x":"Consistency is part of a query's declaration: To serve one callback under two consistencies, declare a twin: BALANCE.named(\"balance_nearby\").consistency(Consistency::ReplicaLocalWithin(2_000)) (see Queries ). Consistency Guarantee How a member serves it Linearizable (the default) Reflects every write that completed before the query began, whichever members served the write and the query. The leader answers under its read lease or after a quorum round; a follower asks the leader for the commit index, waits until it has applied it, then reads its own copy. ReplicaLocalWithin(ms) At most ms milliseconds stale. From its own state while within the bound; otherwise it proves a fresh read first (an","k":"Linearizable ReplicaLocalWithin(ms) ms 503 stale_replica ReplicaLocal x-flower-staleness-ms 0 unknown x-flower-max-staleness-ms RequestOptions::max_staleness x-flower-max-staleness-ms: 0 query http linearizable balance ctx: QueryCtx account: String Result Option max_staleness_ms ReplicaLocalWithin _000 leaderboard Score consistency replica_local catalog Item"},{"t":"Revisions and consistency","h":"Read-your-writes","u":"concepts/consistency.html#read-your-writes","d":0,"x":"Linearizable queries always see the caller's completed writes, on any member. Replica-local queries may not: a follower that has not yet applied your write answers from before it. Use replica-local queries for reads that can lag — dashboards, browsing, leaderboards — and keep checks that guard money or stock inside mutations, which always run on the leader against the latest state. Pattern Goblin Pizza ( examples/goblin-pizza ) declares pizza.shop as a linearizable query and pizza.shop.local as its replica-local twin over the same callback. Clients browse with the local one and buy with mutations.","k":"examples/goblin-pizza pizza.shop pizza.shop.local"},{"t":"Revisions and consistency","h":"Leader leases and read leases","u":"concepts/consistency.html#leases","d":0,"x":"Proving a read linearizable normally takes a round trip to a quorum. Flower avoids most of them with a read lease : after a quorum round, the leader may answer linearizable reads on its own for a short time (by default 270 ms, nine tenths of the 300 ms leader lease), renewing the lease in the background. A leadership transfer or a membership change ends the lease. The lease relies on member clocks advancing at roughly the same rate and never stopping while the process runs. A host that suspends a running member (to RAM, say) can make it serve one stale read when it wakes; set --raft-read-lease-ms 0 on such hosts to make every fresh read take a quorum round. The timing flags are described in ","k":"--raft-read-lease-ms 0"},{"t":"Revisions and consistency","h":"Routing and failover","u":"concepts/consistency.html#routing","d":0,"x":"Any member accepts any call. Queries run where they arrive, as declared. Writes, transactions and other leader-only requests are forwarded by a follower to the leader and the leader's answer is relayed verbatim; every answer names the leader's URL in x-flower-leader-url , so clients can go there directly next time (the Rust client does this with leader_routing(true) ). When a leader fails, a new one is elected in about half a second with the default timing. Writes sent to a follower meanwhile are held and forwarded when the new leader appears. A few answers mean the outcome of a write is unknown — the write may or may not have committed: 503 leader_isolated : the leader lost its quorum for l","k":"x-flower-leader-url leader_routing(true) 503 leader_isolated 503 forward_failed"},{"t":"Retries and receipts","h":"Retries and receipts","u":"concepts/retries.html","d":1,"x":"Networks fail after a write has committed but before its answer arrives. Give each write a request ID and retry with the same one: Flower answers the original outcome instead of running the write again.","k":""},{"t":"Retries and receipts","h":"Request IDs","u":"concepts/retries.html#request-ids","d":0,"x":"A mutation or transaction request may carry an idempotency_key (in the Rust client, MutationOptions::request_id ; in the CLI, --request-id ). When the write commits, the database stores a receipt : the outcome under that key, with its value, revision and position. A later request with the same key does not run again; it is answered from the receipt, with \"duplicate\": true : That holds whichever member the retry reaches, and after any number of later writes. A business failure is an outcome too: a retry of a request that failed with TASK_EXISTS gets TASK_EXISTS again. If the original is still committing when the retry arrives, the retry waits for it (up to five seconds). Retries are authorize","k":"idempotency_key MutationOptions::request_id --request-id \"duplicate\": true TASK_EXISTS identity_conflict true value done false title milk revision position duplicate"},{"t":"Retries and receipts","h":"When to retry","u":"concepts/retries.html#unknown-outcomes","d":0,"x":"Retry with the same key whenever you cannot tell whether a write committed: a timeout, a dropped connection, a cancelled request, 503 leader_isolated , 503 forward_failed . Retry, with or without the key, after refusals that guarantee nothing ran: 503 overloaded (honour Retry-After ), 503 quorum , 503 partition_moving and the other 503s. Never treat an HTTP 422 with \"ok\":false as transient: that is your method's own answer, recorded like a success. The client guide lists every case, and flower-client implements this policy for you.","k":"503 leader_isolated 503 forward_failed 503 overloaded Retry-After 503 quorum 503 partition_moving \"ok\":false flower-client"},{"t":"Retries and receipts","h":"How long receipts last","u":"concepts/retries.html#how-long","d":0,"x":"Every database served by cargo flower serve gets a retry window , by default 24 hours ( --retry-window-ms ). Receipts are grouped into epochs of one eighth of the window, and nine epochs are kept, so a write stays retryable for at least the window and at most one eighth longer — 24 to 27 hours by default — before the leader collects its receipt. Operators can change a database's retention with cargo flower retention : the epoch length, how many epochs to keep, and a byte budget beyond which new writes are refused with receipt_budget_exceeded rather than evicting anything. A retry with a key whose receipt has been collected runs again as a new write. Design for the window: a client that might","k":"cargo flower serve --retry-window-ms cargo flower retention receipt_budget_exceeded"},{"t":"Retries and receipts","h":"Scoped request IDs","u":"concepts/retries.html#scoped-ids","d":0,"x":"Two kinds of ID give stronger guarantees than a plain key. Both are minted from the database's current retry identity , which GET /v1/retry?db=… returns and cargo flower retry --db … prints: f1: IDs are derived from a business intent: f1:<incarnation>:<epoch>:<sha-256 of the intent> . Their receipt pins the module, alias, canonical arguments and expected revision, so the same ID with different content is refused with identity_conflict . Mint one with cargo flower retry --db D id --intent order:42 or client.retry_identity(db) and RetryIdentity::request_id(intent) . f2: IDs belong to a retry session : a numbered sequence of writes a client opens, acknowledges as it learns their outcomes, and c","k":"GET /v1/retry?db=… cargo flower retry --db … f1: f1:<incarnation>:<epoch>:<sha-256 of the intent> identity_conflict cargo flower retry --db D id --intent order:42 client.retry_identity(db) RetryIdentity::request_id(intent) f2: already_acknowledged wrong_history"},{"t":"Retries and receipts","h":"Refusals a receipt can decide","u":"concepts/retries.html#refusals","d":0,"x":"Code Meaning identity_conflict The key belongs to another principal, or a scoped ID was reused for different content. epoch_retired / epoch_not_admitted The scoped ID's epoch is older than the oldest kept, or from the future. wrong_history The scoped ID was minted in another history of the database (before a restore). receipt_released The receipt was released by a retry session acknowledgement. retry_session_unknown , retry_session_closed , retry_session_forbidden , already_acknowledged , retry_ack_gap Retry-session states. receipt_budget_exceeded The database's receipt budget is full. These are answered like a call's own failure — HTTP 422 with \"ok\":false — and are not duplicates:","k":"identity_conflict epoch_retired epoch_not_admitted wrong_history receipt_released retry_session_unknown retry_session_closed retry_session_forbidden already_acknowledged retry_ack_gap receipt_budget_exceeded \"ok\":false false revision position code message retry identity reused with different intent duplicate"},{"t":"Watches","h":"Watches","u":"concepts/watches.html","d":1,"x":"A watch keeps one query's result up to date. You subscribe to a query and its arguments; the server sends the current value, then a new value — or a patch — whenever a commit changes what the query returns.","k":""},{"t":"Watches","h":"Subscribing","u":"concepts/watches.html#subscribing","d":0,"x":"A watch is a POST /v1/watch whose body is exactly a query's — db , module , name , args , and optionally credentials — answered as server-sent events: The first event is always a snapshot of the current value. Then each change to the result produces one event carrying the revision it was computed at, and an id naming the value. With \"deltas\":true in the body, a change may arrive as a patch : an RFC 6902 JSON Patch ( add , remove , replace ) against the value whose id is base — always the last one delivered. Patches are sent only when smaller than the whole value: A watch that cannot start answers like the query would, with an ordinary error response: 404 UNKNOWN_METHOD for a name the module ","k":"POST /v1/watch db module name args credentials snapshot revision id \"deltas\":true patch add remove replace base UNKNOWN_METHOD {} error {\"code\",\"message\"} details unauthorized : watching Authorization: Bearer {\"bearer\": token} x-flower-timeout-ms timestamp_ms flower-client curl http: watch content-type: application json demo tasks open_tasks event: data: value bread title Bake eggs path"},{"t":"Watches","h":"When a watch wakes","u":"concepts/watches.html#when-a-watch-wakes","d":0,"x":"Each evaluation of the query produces a dependency certificate : the records it read (present or absent), the scan and index ranges it covered (so an insertion into an empty range counts), and the derived values it read, expanded transitively. A commit wakes a watch only if it touched something in that certificate, or changed the module's code. Unrelated writes cost one check against the change journal. A woken watch re-evaluates its query and publishes only if the result's bytes changed. Values never go back in revision; several commits in quick succession may be coalesced into one event. Time also wakes watches: at an instant the query declared with changes_at , or every 250 ms for a query","k":"changes_at now()"},{"t":"Watches","h":"Reauthorization","u":"concepts/watches.html#reauthorization","d":0,"x":"Every delivery is authorized at the revision it shows. Before publishing a refresh, the server re-runs each subscriber's authorization against the same view it evaluates the query on. It also rechecks when a commit touches what the decision read, at the decision's declared expiry, when a managed key the decision used changes, and at least every 30 seconds. A subscriber who is no longer allowed gets an error event with code unauthorized , and the stream ends; one whose principal changed gets watch_scope_changed and should reconnect.","k":"error unauthorized watch_scope_changed"},{"t":"Watches","h":"Consistency and failover","u":"concepts/watches.html#consistency-and-failover","d":0,"x":"A watch's first snapshot reads as the query declares. Watches take no staleness bound from the caller. A follower cut off from its leader keeps its watches open but cannot refresh them, except replica-local watches with a bound, which it ends once they are past it. When a stream ends — leader change, member shutdown ( shutdown ), partition move, redeployment removing the alias ( UNKNOWN_METHOD ) — reconnect and you get a fresh snapshot. Not an event log A watch delivers the current value of a query, not the history of changes. Reconnecting does not replay what you missed; there is no Last-Event-ID . If you need every change, write changes to a collection (a trigger is a good place) and watch","k":"shutdown UNKNOWN_METHOD Last-Event-ID"},{"t":"Watches","h":"Limits","u":"concepts/watches.html#limits","d":0,"x":"Each subscriber has a 32-event queue. A subscriber that falls that far behind is dropped and its stream closes without an error event; reconnect. A query that reads more than 4,096 dependencies gets a broad certificate: any change to its database wakes it. Keep watched queries focused. Each watch is an HTTP/2 stream. The Rust client opens another connection per 100 watches. A member holds at most 64 MiB of watches' arguments and credentials. Past that, a new watch is refused as an overload (503, retryable) until others end.","k":""},{"t":"Time","h":"Time","u":"concepts/time.html","d":1,"x":"Each invocation sees one fixed clock reading. Results that depend on time say so — either by polling the clock or by declaring the instant they will change — and caches, derived values and watches follow those declarations.","k":""},{"t":"Time","h":"The invocation clock","u":"concepts/time.html#the-invocation-clock","d":0,"x":"ctx.now() and ctx.clock() return the same value: the invocation's timestamp in milliseconds since the Unix epoch, as an f64 . It does not advance while your code runs, and nested calls see the same reading. They differ only in what they tell the engine: now() says \"my result depends on the current time\". The result polls the clock: it is never served from cache at a later instant, and a watch on it re-evaluates every 250 ms. clock() reads the time without that implication. Pair it with changes_at when your result changes at a known instant.","k":"ctx.now() ctx.clock() f64 now() clock() changes_at"},{"t":"Time","h":"Declaring when a result changes","u":"concepts/time.html#changes-at","d":0,"x":"ctx.changes_at(Some(t)) declares that the result may change at instant t even if no data changes. The result is valid from its evaluation until then: caches serve it, and at t it is evaluated again and watches refresh. The earliest declaration wins . Declarations from everything an invocation reads — derived values, other modules' methods, the authorization decision — combine, and a later instant never lifts an earlier one. That is what you want: a session that lasts an hour does not extend a token check that expires in a minute. changes_at(None) declares nothing. A declared instant at or before the evaluation's own clock counts as polling. When the value changes continuously, poll instead:","k":"ctx.changes_at(Some(t)) t changes_at(None) discount coupon gives right now. answer change expiry without write query says when: caches watches hold result until then re-evaluate that instant. http ctx: QueryCtx code: String Result Some Coupons::get code else return ctx.clock coupon.expires_at ctx.changes_at coupon.percent Seconds left coupon. This changes every instant reads never cached watch re-evaluates poll. seconds_left ctx.now floor"},{"t":"Time","h":"Derived values and time","u":"concepts/time.html#derived-values-and-time","d":0,"x":"A derived value keeps the time behaviour of its evaluation with its cached outcome, including what it inherited from the values it read. One that polled is served only at the instant it was computed; one that declared an instant is served until that instant, then evaluated again (keeping its pin if it is materialized). Every reader inherits both.","k":""},{"t":"Time","h":"Where the clock comes from","u":"concepts/time.html#where-the-clock-comes-from","d":0,"x":"Mutations take the leader's clock when the leader prepares them. A batch of mutations prepared together shares one reading, and a leader's writer never goes backwards. Node clocks are not synchronized distributed time: after a failover, the new leader's clock is what counts. Keep clocks synchronized with NTP. Queries take the clock of the member that evaluates them. Timers and queue leases are due times on the leader's clock, at least as late as requested; they are never exact. A trusted gateway (a request carrying the admin token) may pass timestamp_ms to evaluate a call at a chosen time, for testing and replays. A watch uses it for its first snapshot only: its later refreshes read the serv","k":"timestamp_ms"},{"t":"Time","h":"Authorization decisions and time","u":"concepts/time.html#authorization-and-time","d":0,"x":"An authorization hook's decision can expire too: a typed authenticator like JwtBearer declares the token's expiry, and a hook can return until or call changes_at . The server keeps a decision only until then, and an open watch is re-authorized at that instant — so a watch never outlives the token that opened it. See Authentication and authorization .","k":"JwtBearer until changes_at"},{"t":"Queues and timers","h":"Queues and timers","u":"concepts/queues-timers.html","d":1,"x":"Guests never touch the outside world. Work that must — sending email, calling a payment provider — becomes a durable job that a native worker claims under a fenced lease. Work that must happen later becomes a timer that fires a mutation.","k":""},{"t":"Queues and timers","h":"Queues","u":"concepts/queues-timers.html#queues","d":0,"x":"A mutation enqueues a job: a JSON payload in a named queue. The enqueue is part of the mutation, so the job exists exactly when the mutation commits and never if it rolls back. Workers then claim jobs, do the work, and report the outcome. A TypedQueue<P, R> static is the queue's declaration, its contract and its handle at once: listed among the app's components, it declares the queue, and the server checks every payload against P and every kept result against R : Calling invite twice for the same address shows the named job at work. The second call is the mutation's own failure, HTTP 422: Guest code uses only the queues its own module declares; naming any other fails with queue_undeclared . ","k":"TypedQueue<P, R> P R invite queue_undeclared queue_purging Queue::enqueue(ctx, \"mail\", payload, &options) Value QueueDef derive FlowerType Clone Debug PartialEq struct Email String subject: Sent message_id: queue contract handle declaration. Listed among components declares queue: host refuses other payload from guests native workers alike checks kept results too. static MAIL: TypedQueue TypedQueue::new mail Queue invitation tenant scope. exists exactly when this mutation commits naming makes second same address fail JOB_EXISTS instead queueing twice. http ctx: MutationCtx tenant: Result email to.clone String::from invited options EnqueueOptions::default scope named format invite: max_attempts MAIL.enqueue Whether went result worker kept. query invitation_sent QueryCtx bool name Job::Named scope: name: MAIL.result is_some true value ff7d39fc385f5fe1d53b0e5a4ca7e32 revision position duplicate false code message invite:ada example.com already"},{"t":"Queues and timers","h":"Enqueue options","u":"concepts/queues-timers.html#enqueue-options","d":0,"x":"Option Default Meaning scope(s) \"\" A logical partition of the queue — per tenant, per region. Workers claim from one scope at a time. Scopes separate work; they do not authenticate anyone. priority(p) 0 Lower values are claimed first. group(g) none Within a priority, each ready group's next job goes before any group's second: fair turns between, say, customers. named(n) random ID A name unique within the scope. A second enqueue of a pending or claimed name fails JOB_EXISTS . replace() off With a name: replace a completed or dead job of that name instead of failing. delay_ms(d) / at_ms(t) now Not claimable before then; at most 30 days ahead. Use one or the other. max_attempts(n) 5 Claims befo","k":"scope(s) \"\" priority(p) 0 group(g) named(n) JOB_EXISTS replace() delay_ms(d) at_ms(t) max_attempts(n) 5"},{"t":"Queues and timers","h":"Claims, leases and fencing","u":"concepts/queues-timers.html#claims-and-leases","d":0,"x":"A claim takes up to 100 claimable jobs of one scope — due pending jobs, and claimed jobs whose lease lapsed — lowest priority first, then group turns, then the job that waited longest. Each claim: uses one of the job's attempts; holds a lease (30 seconds by default, at most an hour) that the worker renews while it works; carries a secret token that every renewal and report must present; carries a fencing token , larger than every one issued before it in that queue and scope. Pass it to the downstream system so it can refuse a write from a worker whose lease lapsed and was taken over. The worker then reports one of: complete (keep the job with a result), finish (delete it), fail (pending agai","k":""},{"t":"Queues and timers","h":"Who claims","u":"concepts/queues-timers.html#who-claims","d":0,"x":"Usually a native worker built with flower-worker , over the worker HTTP endpoints ( /v1/queue/poll , /complete , …) with the server's worker token. Native workers may name any queue, but never enqueue: jobs come only from mutations. Guest mutations can claim and report too, through the typed handle's claim , complete , renew and friends, which is useful for work that stays inside the database. Goblin Pizza's pizza.deliver renews a delivery drone's lease inside the paying mutation, so a drone whose lease lapsed cannot collect payment. Queries can watch a queue: stats and ready (on the typed handle, or Queue::stats and Queue::ready by name) return counts and whether a claim would succeed, from","k":"flower-worker /v1/queue/poll /complete claim complete renew pizza.deliver stats ready Queue::stats Queue::ready"},{"t":"Queues and timers","h":"Timers","u":"concepts/queues-timers.html#timers","d":0,"x":"A mutation schedules a timer by a stable ID: at a due time, the leader runs a mutation of the same module (the handler ) with the arguments you gave. The handler is the mutation's own static and the arguments its own argument struct, so a mistake is a compile error; the arguments are also checked against its contract when the timer is scheduled. Scheduling an existing ID replaces the timer, which makes debouncing trivial. The handler is a mutation of the same module. It needs no http , and an internal handler should have none: then no client can call it, as the 404 UNKNOWN_METHOD for end_trial shows. Timers::after(ctx, id, delay, &HANDLER, &args) takes a core::time::Duration ; Timers::at an ","k":"http UNKNOWN_METHOD end_trial Timers::after(ctx, id, delay, &HANDLER, &args) core::time::Duration Timers::at Timers::schedule ScheduleOptions --timer-sweep-ms max_attempts dead cargo flower timer Timers::get scan retry const TRIAL: Duration Duration::from_secs Timer text bytes without control characters. trial_timer account: String format trial: account mutation start_trial ctx: MutationCtx Result Trials::set Trial active: true handler descriptor arguments argument struct checked now. Scheduling existing replaces that timer. args EndTrialArgs account.clone Timers::after TRIAL END_TRIAL timer handler: ordinary this module. Without nothing outside module call false convert bool Trials::delete Timers::cancel cargo flower shop list attempts due_ms trial:ada state pending"},{"t":"Queues and timers","h":"Registering them","u":"concepts/queues-timers.html#registering","d":0,"x":"The queue is a component; the timer's handler is an ordinary mutation:","k":"static APP: App::new MAIL Trials INVITE INVITATION_SENT START_TRIAL END_TRIAL CONVERT"},{"t":"Queues and timers","h":"Queues, timers or tasks?","u":"concepts/queues-timers.html#choosing","d":0,"x":"Use When A queue The work happens outside Flower, may fail and be retried, and needs a worker process. A timer A specific thing must happen inside Flower at or after a specific time: expire a trial, finish baking a pizza. A maintenance task Periodic housekeeping whose need you can compute from the data: purge expired sessions, compact old rows.","k":""},{"t":"Modules and interfaces","h":"Modules and interfaces","u":"concepts/modules.html","d":1,"x":"Several modules can share a database and commit together, while each keeps its data private. A module exposes exactly what it exports; other modules reach it through contracts that deployment checks for compatibility.","k":""},{"t":"Modules and interfaces","h":"Private by default","u":"concepts/modules.html#private-by-default","d":0,"x":"A module's collections, derived values and methods are invisible to other modules unless their declarations export them. Reading or calling something not exported fails with MODULE_PRIVATE . Exported collections are read-only to other modules ( MODULE_READ_ONLY ): changes always go through the owner's own mutations, so its checks, triggers and references always run.","k":"MODULE_PRIVATE MODULE_READ_ONLY"},{"t":"Modules and interfaces","h":"Exports","u":"concepts/modules.html#exports","d":0,"x":"An export is a flag on the declaration it concerns: export on #[collection] , #[query] , #[mutation] and #[derived] , or callers(\"shop\", ...) on a method to admit only the modules it names: A method with a callers allowlist refuses every other module with MODULE_PRIVATE . Allowlists name actual module names, never slots. Raw descriptors say the same with .export() and .export_to(&[\"shop\"]) ; an empty allowlist admits no module. Exports change nothing inside the module itself, and HTTP exposure is separate: http governs clients, export governs other modules. Other modules read exported collections through typed handles pointed at the provider: Items::SOURCE.in_module(\"warehouse\") or an index ","k":"export #[collection] #[query] #[mutation] #[derived] callers(\"shop\", ...) callers MODULE_PRIVATE .export() .export_to(&[\"shop\"]) http Items::SOURCE.in_module(\"warehouse\") in_module ctx.call try_call call_mutation try_mutation Without interface exports flags declarations: other modules read this collection... collection name prices String value struct Prices only shop reports call query alone would admit every module price ctx: QueryCtx sku: Result Prices::get unwrap_or"},{"t":"Modules and interfaces","h":"Interface crates","u":"concepts/modules.html#interface-crates","d":0,"x":"Modules are separate Wasm artifacts that share no code at run time — only contracts. The tidy way to share a contract is an interface crate : a plain library both modules depend on, holding the types and a trait annotated with #[interface] : From that one trait the macro generates both sides: For the provider , InventoryProvider<P> : implement the trait on any type P , then list InventoryProvider::<P>::COMPONENT among the app's components. It brings the interface's methods, exported to every module, and nothing else. They stay off HTTP unless you list COMPONENT.http() instead; to give them access rules, name them in the authorization component's methods table. For consumers , the handle Inve","k":"#[interface] InventoryProvider<P> P InventoryProvider::<P>::COMPONENT COMPONENT.http() methods InventoryApi InventoryApi::import(\"$inventory\") item reserve try_item try_reserve examples/typed-modules-contract -provider -consumer derive FlowerType Clone Debug PartialEq struct Item name: String stock: FlowerError enum StockError flower code UNKNOWN_ITEM Unknown OUT_OF_STOCK OutOfStock other Host Failure contract versioned incompatible change gets interface rather than breaking deployed consumers. name inventory.v1 handle trait Inventory query ctx: QueryCtx sku: flower_sdk::Result Option mutation MutationCtx quantity: core::result::Result collection items value Items Warehouse impl Items::get ok_or StockError::Unknown item.stock quantity return StockError::OutOfStock Items::set provider component brings exported every module. http callers reach them over HTTP too. static PROVIDER_APP: App::new InventoryProvider:: COMPONENT Wherever this deployment inventory binding points: typed call import declaring must meet. INVENTORY: InventoryApi::import checkout Result match INVENTORY.try_reserve CallOutcome::Success left alloc::format reserved CallOutcome::Failure only fail CHECKOUT_FAILED other: CallError::Host failure CONSUMER_APP: INVENTORY CHECKOUT"},{"t":"Modules and interfaces","h":"Slots and bindings","u":"concepts/modules.html#slots-and-bindings","d":0,"x":"The consumer above imports \"$inventory\" , a slot , rather than a concrete module name. Each deployment binds the slot to a module, so the same binary can run against different providers in different databases: Deploying without the binding is refused before anything is sent: Bindings become part of the deployed manifest and of its content identity. Every imported slot must be bound and every binding used; module names never start with $ . To point a consumer at another provider, deploy the same binary again with another binding. --bindings FILE takes the same map as a JSON object.","k":"\"$inventory\" $ --bindings FILE cargo flower deploy store module warehouse wasm provider.wasm admin-token secret shop consumer.wasm bind inventory cargo-flower: invalid_module preflight nothing sent slot bound: with"},{"t":"Modules and interfaces","h":"Compatibility checks","u":"concepts/modules.html#compatibility","d":0,"x":"When you deploy a module, the server checks its imports against the providers deployed in the database, and checks every deployed importer against what the module is about to export. A provider must accept every argument the importer may send, and may only return results and declared errors the importer accepts; exported collections must agree on keys and fit the importer's row type. A change that would break a link is refused with 409 MODULE_CONFLICT , naming what would break. You can run the same check offline before deploying: At run time the host still holds every answer to the provider's declared contract: a result or error outside it fails with MODULE_CONTRACT_VIOLATION , as the callee","k":"409 MODULE_CONFLICT MODULE_CONTRACT_VIOLATION inventory.v2 cargo flower plan wasm consumer.wasm module shop with warehouse provider.wasm bind inventory"},{"t":"Modules and interfaces","h":"Removing a module","u":"concepts/modules.html#removing-modules","d":0,"x":"cargo flower modules --db D remove --module M removes a module's code, HTTP names, hooks and derived values through the log. Its records stay, unreachable, so deploying it again brings them back; add --purge to delete its data too, in the background, page by page. A module another module imports from cannot be removed ( MODULE_CONFLICT ). See Deployments .","k":"cargo flower modules --db D remove --module M --purge MODULE_CONFLICT"},{"t":"Transactions and partitions","h":"Transactions and partitions","u":"concepts/transactions.html","d":1,"x":"A mutation is atomic within one database. A transaction runs mutations in several databases — in one group or across groups — and commits all of them or none.","k":""},{"t":"Transactions and partitions","h":"Transactions are lists of steps","u":"concepts/transactions.html#steps","d":0,"x":"A transaction is a list of steps , each naming a database, a module, a mutation the module exposes over HTTP ( method ) and its arguments. Each step must target a different database. Send it to POST /v1/transaction : value holds each step's result in order, and revisions the revision each database reached. If any step fails, nothing commits anywhere, and the answer is that step's failure, with HTTP 422 like any call's own failure: The same transaction from the CLI and from Rust:","k":"method POST /v1/transaction value revisions curl http: transaction content-type: application json steps demo module tasks complete_task args eggs archive add_task title idempotency_key archive-eggs true done false revision position duplicate code TASK_EXISTS message task with this already exists cargo flower async client: FlowerClient Result TransactionResult FlowerError TransactionStep to_string module: method: args: archived options TransactionOptions request_id: Some format archive: TransactionOptions::new client.transaction await"},{"t":"Transactions and partitions","h":"What a transaction guarantees","u":"concepts/transactions.html#semantics","d":0,"x":"All or nothing. Every step commits, or none does. Steps are isolated from each other. Each step prepares in its own session against its own database; a step never sees another step's writes. Pass whatever a later step needs in its arguments. Each database stays consistent. Steps are validated against the latest state of their database at commit, just like mutations. Transactions take no expected_revision . Retries are safe. An idempotency_key works as for mutations: a retry answers the recorded outcome with \"duplicate\": true . Transactions accept only plain keys, not scoped f1: / f2: IDs. Steps call what each module exposes over HTTP ( http on the mutation; anything else answers 404 UNKNOWN_","k":"expected_revision idempotency_key \"duplicate\": true f1: f2: http UNKNOWN_METHOD x-flower-timeout-ms --max-transaction-bytes 413 transaction_too_large"},{"t":"Transactions and partitions","h":"Within one group","u":"concepts/transactions.html#within-a-group","d":0,"x":"When every database lives in the same Raft group — the usual case, since unplaced databases all live in root — the transaction commits as one log entry at one position. Watchers of each database see the change at that database's new revision.","k":"root"},{"t":"Transactions and partitions","h":"Across groups","u":"concepts/transactions.html#across-groups","d":0,"x":"When the databases live in different groups, the first step's group coordinates a two-phase commit: it records the transaction durably, asks each participant group to prepare, decides, and has every participant finish. A participant persists its read and write footprint, including the transitive source dependencies behind cached derived values. Every replica checks that same footprint, even when its own derived cache differs. Independent writes can continue, including writes that store retry receipts or maintain unrelated derived values. Conflicting writes wait within their deadlines. Timer and queue reads protect the rows and ranges they actually inspected; they do not block unrelated write","k":"transaction_conflict positions cargo flower transactions Transaction::new(name, planner).args(schema) Plan .http() POST /v1/mutate"},{"t":"Transactions and partitions","h":"Partitions","u":"concepts/transactions.html#partitions","d":0,"x":"Placing a database in its own group — making it a named partition — changes where it runs, not how you call it. Clients keep naming the database; the server routes each call to the owning group. Reasons to partition: Isolation. A busy tenant's writes queue on its group's leader, not on everyone's. Scale. Each group has its own leader and log, so write throughput grows with groups placed on different servers. Placement. A group's members can be the servers in one region. The cost is that work spanning partitions in different groups becomes a cross-group transaction, with the locking described above. Design partitions so that most mutations touch one database. Operators create groups and place","k":"cargo flower group cargo flower partition 503 partition_moving Retry-After: 1 partition_moved"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Calling Flower from Rust","u":"guides/rust-client.html","d":1,"x":"flower-client is an async HTTP client for Flower: plain and typed calls, request IDs and retries, deadlines and cancellation, reconnecting watches, transactions, queue and admin calls.","k":"flower-client"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Setup","u":"guides/rust-client.html#setup","d":0,"x":"Add the crate by path from your Flower checkout, with Tokio and serde: Cargo.toml The guest crate builds natively as an rlib (the quickstart 's crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"rlib\"] ), so the client can use its declarations for typed calls . The default remote client uses reqwest with rustls and does not link the engine or Wasmtime. Enable the replica feature for local sync-down and offline intent queues. A shared interface crate supplies native contract descriptors without linking the provider implementation. On Windows on ARM64, building it needs LLVM's clang on PATH , as for the server.","k":"rlib crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"rlib\"] replica clang PATH dependencies flower-client path flower crates flower-sdk typed calls tasks your guest crate descriptors serde version features derive serde_json tokio macros rt-multi-thread"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Connecting","u":"guides/rust-client.html#connecting","d":0,"x":"The client talks to one base URL. Any member works: followers forward writes. With leader_routing(true) , the client remembers the leader each answer names ( x-flower-leader-url , per database) and sends writes there directly, falling back to the base URL when the leader stops answering. Credentials::token sends Authorization: Bearer … . A token that is not one of the server's own tokens reaches the module's authorization hook as {\"bearer\": token} ; the server's admin token makes the client a trusted gateway. Credentials::from_fn consults a closure on every attempt, for tokens that refresh. Over http:// the client speaks HTTP/2 without TLS (h2c) and multiplexes every call over one connection","k":"leader_routing(true) x-flower-leader-url Credentials::token Authorization: Bearer … {\"bearer\": token} Credentials::from_fn http:// TransportOptions::new().protocol(Protocol::Http1) https:// .ca_file(path) .retry(Retry::Default) FlowerClient connect token: Result FlowerError FlowerClient::builder http: credentials token Retry transient failures with backoff ride overload. retry Retry::Default Send writes straight leader each reply names. leader_routing true Each attempt timeout default Duration::from_secs build"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Queries and mutations","u":"guides/rust-client.html#calls","d":0,"x":"query and mutate take any serde-serializable arguments and deserialize the result; query_value and mutate_value work with serde_json::Value . Every call names the database, the module and the method's HTTP name, and the caller picks query or mutation. These calls need nothing from the guest, so they suit scripts and other teams' services; with the guest crate at hand, prefer typed calls : A MutationResult carries the request ID, optional read token, method's value , the revision and position it committed at, and duplicate — true when a stored receipt answered a retry. A QueryResult carries the value and, for replica-local queries, the reported staleness ; RequestOptions::max_staleness tighte","k":"query mutate query_value mutate_value serde_json::Value MutationResult value revision position duplicate QueryResult staleness RequestOptions::max_staleness request_id derive Serialize struct AddTask title: Deserialize Debug Task String done: bool OpenTask async add_and_list client: FlowerClient Result FlowerError request chosen intent: retrying never twice. options MutationOptions::new add-milk args milk added: client demo tasks add_task await println duplicate: added.value added.revision added.duplicate open: open_tasks json RequestOptions::new open.value"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Scoped handles and persisted commands","u":"guides/rust-client.html#durable-commands","d":0,"x":"Bind the database and module once with client.database(\"demo\").module(\"tasks\") . Query descriptors can be queried or watched; mutation descriptors can be prepared as serializable commands. Shared #[interface] contracts emit native descriptors on their handles and public argument types, without requiring the provider implementation. Add client = TasksClient to generate named query methods and prepare_* mutation methods. Bind the facade with TasksApi::client(module) ; its public remote backend executes persisted commands. The shared contract still needs only the SDK, including when compiled for Wasm. Scoped query builders take QueryOptions ; prepared commands and typed transactions take Comman","k":"client.database(\"demo\").module(\"tasks\") #[interface] client = TasksClient prepare_* TasksApi::client(module) remote QueryOptions CommandOptions into() CommandOptions::try_from(legacy) prepare_with_identity CommandResult CommandError scope.query(&QUERY, &args).at_least(token).await command.if_unchanged(&token) client.protocol(options).await?.require(&[\"read_tokens\"]) TransportOptions::direct() proxy() flower_sdk::interface name tasks.v1 handle TasksApi client TasksClient trait TasksContract query get_task ctx: flower_sdk::QueryCtx String flower_sdk::Result Option tasks::Task mutation add_task flower_sdk::MutationCtx title: async prepare_with_generated_client client: FlowerClient Result flower_client::PreparedCommand FlowerError tasks TasksApi::client client.database demo module current TasksContractGetTaskArgs milk into await map_err error error.source clone println value: current.value Persist returned command before tasks.remote.execute prepare_add_task TasksContractAddTaskArgs Prepare once durably store serialized first execution. prepare_task operation ADD_TASK as_mutation failure FlowerError::Decode failure.message database prepare AddTaskArgs title recovered retains identity returns commit metadata. execute_task command: flower_client::CommandResult flower_client::CommandError flower_sdk::Failure execute CommandOptions::retrying"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Generated workflow builders","u":"guides/rust-client.html#workflow-builders","d":0,"x":"Generated mutation methods also return a builder: tasks.add_task(&args).prepare().await prepares an unsent command, while .enqueue(&outbox).await persists it before returning a handle. Deliver the stored command with handle.execute(&options).await . Existing prepare_* methods remain available. Custom backends opt in through the SDK's RemoteCommands trait. A query builder supports .watch().await for a single connection and .subscribe() for reconnecting snapshots. Credentials, connect timeout and cancellation carry into the stream. Query causal bounds, staleness, whole-call deadlines and retry overrides are rejected before opening a stream; use the explicit subscription API for reconnect optio","k":"tasks.add_task(&args).prepare().await .enqueue(&outbox).await handle.execute(&options).await prepare_* RemoteCommands .watch().await .subscribe() RemoteError CommandError TransactionError declared() problem() execution() async enqueue_with_generated_client client: FlowerClient outbox: flower_client::CommandOutbox args: TasksContractAddTaskArgs Result flower_client::CommandHandle tasks::Task flower_client::OutboxError flower_sdk::Failure tasks TasksApi::client client.database demo module Returns only after persistence. Execute this handle deliver mutation. tasks.add_task args enqueue outbox await subscribe_with_generated_client String flower_client::DeclaredSubscription Option tasks.get_task TasksContractGetTaskArgs subscribe"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Typed calls and declared errors","u":"guides/rust-client.html#typed-calls","d":0,"x":"Every #[query] and #[mutation] declares a static descriptor, an Op<Args, T, E> , and a public argument struct. With the guest crate as a dependency, client.call(db, module, &OP, &OpArgs { .. }) takes the method's name and kind from the descriptor, encodes the arguments with the SDK's own codec, decodes the result as the method's return type and a failure as the method's error type. A renamed method, a changed argument or a changed result type is then a compile error in the client, not a 404 or a decode failure in production: call returns Result<T, RemoteError<E>> . A 422 failure the method's error type accepts comes back as RemoteError::Declared { error, source } , both boxed so the error st","k":"#[query] #[mutation] Op<Args, T, E> client.call(db, module, &OP, &OpArgs { .. }) call Result<T, RemoteError<E>> RemoteError::Declared { error, source } RemoteError::Other source() FlowerError #[flower(other)] Declared call_with MutationOptions pay_once base duplicate typed_mutate typed_query FlowerType RemoteTarget::new(module, name) *_at doc_snippets::methods:: TRANSFER TransferArgs TransferError tasks:: ADD_TASK AddTaskArgs OPEN_TASKS OpenTasksArgs guest crate dependency built rlib each query mutation static carries name kind argument struct result type declared errors. async add_milk client: FlowerClient Result tasks::OpenTask args milk to_string title: client demo tasks await map_err error error.source clone failure method declares comes back typed. from: amount: String from.to_string merchant amount match client.call bank left format paid RemoteError::Declared source TransferError::InsufficientFunds balance declined: other other.source sends under fresh request takes options. Here names payment sending again answers first attempt outcome instead paying twice. RemoteError options MutationOptions::retrying request_id pay: from"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Deadlines and cancellation","u":"guides/rust-client.html#deadlines","d":0,"x":"Every attempt tells the server its remaining budget in x-flower-timeout-ms , so a request still waiting for admission when its caller has given up is dropped rather than run. A cancelled or timed-out mutation may still have committed: retry it with the same request ID.","k":"x-flower-timeout-ms async open_tasks_soon client: FlowerClient cancel: CancellationToken Result serde_json::Value FlowerError options RequestOptions::retrying attempt starts after this each what left server sheds work queued past deadline Instant::now Duration::from_secs cancel open client query_value demo tasks open_tasks json await open.value"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Durable command storage and recovery","u":"guides/rust-client.html#durable-outbox","d":0,"x":"CommandOutbox verifies the complete contract and persists a command before allowing delivery. Its pluggable CommandStore supports atomic insert and compare-and-swap generations. FileCommandStore uses a checksummed, synced journal with OS file locking; it repairs a torn final entry and refuses complete-entry corruption. It replays the journal per operation and limits entries to 16 MiB. Use a database backend for larger histories; MemoryCommandStore is an ephemeral test backend. Call handle.execute(&CommandOptions::retrying()).await to submit saved work. status() exposes Pending, Sending, Succeeded, Rejected, Unknown and Unresolved; updates() coalesces local lifecycle snapshots. An abandoned S","k":"CommandOutbox CommandStore FileCommandStore MemoryCommandStore handle.execute(&CommandOptions::retrying()).await status() updates() retry_uncertain resolve async persist_task client: FlowerClient journal: std::path::Path args: TasksContractAddTaskArgs Result flower_client::CommandHandle tasks::Task std::error::Error outbox flower_client::CommandOutbox::new Arc::new flower_client::FileCommandStore::open journal module client.database demo tasks command module.prepare TasksApi::ADD_TASK args await mutation been sent. returned handle owns durable workflow. enqueue recover_task request_id: Checks current complete contract keeps original request resume request_id"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Causal sessions","u":"guides/rust-client.html#causal-sessions","d":0,"x":"client.causal_session() opts into a shared unary causal context. Modules and generated clients retain tokens per database and automatically bound later reads by earlier observations. Clones share state. Operations within a database serialize; different databases remain independent. Deadlines and cancellation cover lock waits too. Missing token support or history mismatch causes an error instead of a weaker read. observe(token) imports a commit or watch token; token(db) exports current state. Proved reads and scoped writes can carry history across a move. reset(db) deliberately starts a new context after reconciliation. Tokens stay in memory unless the application persists them. Session-bound","k":"client.causal_session() observe(token) token(db) reset(db) async causal_roundtrip client: FlowerClient args: TasksContractAddTaskArgs Result QueryResult Option tasks::Task std::error::Error session client.causal_session tasks TasksApi::client session.database demo module command tasks.prepare_add_task args await remote execute CommandOptions::retrying automatically bounds this query committed write token. get_task TasksContractGetTaskArgs args.id.clone options flower_client::QueryOptions::new deadline Instant::now Duration::from_secs"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Protocol compatibility and custom transports","u":"guides/rust-client.html#compatibility","d":0,"x":"Typed requirements produce structured reports with missing capabilities, unknown extensions and peer/client versions. inspect_protocol inspects newer peers without accepting their format; a wider requested version range never enables an unimplemented protocol. Enable conformance to check a transport against the reusable HTTP/1.1 and h2c loopback harness. It verifies credentials, bytes, deadlines, causal headers, response metadata, retry hints, incremental streaming and response release on drop. This server dependency is absent from the default client.","k":"inspect_protocol conformance async require_causal_peer client: FlowerClient Result flower_client::protocol::ProtocolInfo flower_client::protocol::NegotiationError flower_client::protocol:: Capability ProtocolRequirements client negotiate ProtocolRequirements::requiring Capability::ReadTokens Capability::ScopedCommands RequestOptions::new await tokio::test transport_passes_the_probe_suite std::error::Error flower_client:: HttpTransport TransportOptions testing::conformance::ProbeServer server ProbeServer::start transport HttpTransport::with_options server.base_url TransportOptions::direct Substitute your Transport here same checks apply. server.check await.assert_passed server.shutdown"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Watches","u":"guides/rust-client.html#watches","d":0,"x":"Scoped subscriptions accept query descriptors and retain history identity, reset markers and read tokens. Monotonic suppression restarts when history changes or cannot be proved after reconnect. Terminal errors end the stream; transient errors reconnect with the retry hint. WatchOptions::cancel releases the connection and into_stream() adapts typed watches and subscriptions to futures streams. Scoped watches and subscriptions also preserve the query's declared error type: both opening and terminal failures return RemoteError<E> . Inspect error.declared() for a domain failure and error.source() for the original structured failure. Authorization and host failures remain separate. The existing ","k":"WatchOptions::cancel into_stream() RemoteError<E> error.declared() error.source() typed_watch typed_subscribe FlowerError::Http Business code() problem() POST /v1/watch subscribe Update { revision, value, reset } wait_until reset: true SubscribeOptions retry max_reconnects stall_timeout monotonic watch watch_deltas FlowerType async follow_open_tasks client: FlowerClient Result FlowerError subscription client.subscribe:: OpenTask demo tasks open_tasks json SubscribeOptions::default while Some update subscription.next await reset marks first value after connecting. println revision open update.revision update.value.len update.reset fresh snapshot else until_all_done done client open: open.is_empty done.revision"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Transactions","u":"guides/rust-client.html#transactions","d":0,"x":"TypedTransaction::new().step(db, module, &mutation, &args)? builds a typed result tuple. Persist the serialized batch before executing; redelivery reuses its request ID. Each database appears once. Compose multiple same-database actions inside a guest mutation. Failures retain the failing step and database, with the callee's details under details.cause . The builder also retains a tuple of declared error types. A failure's declared() exposes StepError::Step0(error) through Step7(error) , using the same zero-based index as error.step . When restoring a batch, specify both tuples, such as TypedTransaction<(Invoice, Task), (BillingError, TaskError)> . The legacy one-parameter form keeps dynamic","k":"TypedTransaction::new().step(db, module, &mutation, &args)? details.cause declared() StepError::Step0(error) Step7(error) error.step TypedTransaction<(Invoice, Task), (BillingError, TaskError)> async archive client: FlowerClient Result TransactionResult FlowerError steps TransactionStep demo to_string module: tasks method: complete_task args: json add_task title archived options TransactionOptions request_id: Some format archive: TransactionOptions::new client.transaction await"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Restoring typed transactions","u":"guides/rust-client.html#transaction-restoration","d":0,"x":"Typed transaction snapshots use local format 2 with per-step argument, result and declared-error fingerprints. Build a current typed template and call template.restore(saved) : it checks participant scopes, contracts and saved argument decoding while retaining the saved request ID and arguments. Execution also checks result/error fingerprints. Direct deserialization cannot check the original argument contract against current operation descriptors; use template restoration for recovery. Format-one snapshots require reconciliation or explicit migration using the original contract. Never prepare a new identity to recover an uncertain transaction. These local format checks do not change the serv","k":"template.restore(saved) restore_archive_batch saved: Result flower_client::TypedTransaction tasks::Task flower_sdk::Failure FlowerError freshly built template supplies current operation argument decoder. placeholder arguments fresh replace saved work. flower_client::TypedTransaction::new step archive tasks TasksApi::ADD_TASK TasksContractAddTaskArgs String::new title: template.restore"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Queues, timers and admin calls","u":"guides/rust-client.html#queues-timers-and-admin","d":0,"x":"The same client speaks the worker protocol — claim_queue , heartbeat_queue , report_queue , complete_queue_with , queue_readiness , get_queue_job and more — though you will usually let flower-worker drive it. Jobs are only ever enqueued by guest mutations. With the admin token it also deploys and stages modules, changes membership, lists and inspects timers, reads metrics and drives backups; these are the calls cargo flower itself makes.","k":"claim_queue heartbeat_queue report_queue complete_queue_with queue_readiness get_queue_job flower-worker cargo flower"},{"t":"Calling Flower from Rust","h":"Offline replicas","u":"guides/rust-client.html#offline-replicas","d":0,"x":"For devices, the client includes a sync-down replica ( SyncClient ) that follows a database's log into a local engine and serves reads offline, and an intent queue ( IntentQueue ) that stores mutations made offline and submits them, in order and under stable request IDs, when the device reconnects. These need the server's sync token.","k":"SyncClient IntentQueue"},{"t":"SQL queries","h":"SQL queries","u":"guides/sql.html","d":1,"x":"Run bounded, read-only SQL through ordinary module queries. Declare a typed plan for a fixed statement, or a scoped endpoint for statements supplied at request time.","k":""},{"t":"SQL queries","h":"Request-time SQL","u":"guides/sql.html#endpoint","d":0,"x":"A SqlEndpoint fixes the local collections a statement may read and its resource ceilings. A wrapping query chooses the public name and access rules. The endpoint itself creates no HTTP route. SQL reads the invocation's pinned snapshot, sees staged writes, and applies the enclosing authority's row and field policies. This application exposes sql , sql_explain , a typed orders_by_owner query and a mutation for inserting orders. The #[application] macro assembles its lexical collections and methods; #[component] registers the SQL declarations. Export shop::APP from your guest crate's root. For manual composition, use App::new and list the collection, endpoint, named plan and method descriptors ","k":"SqlEndpoint sql sql_explain orders_by_owner #[application] #[component] shop::APP App::new alloc:: string::String vec::Vec flower_sdk::typed::DecimalI64 flower_sdk:: SqlBudget SqlQuery Value prelude:: flower_sdk::application shop super:: derive FlowerType struct Order owner: String total: DecimalI64 collection name orders value index by_owner fields owner Orders component static SQL_ENDPOINT: SqlEndpoint::new orders.sql Orders::NAME SqlBudget::new _000 _048_576 _000_000 query http ctx: QueryCtx statement: params: Result SQL_ENDPOINT.run statement params SQL_ENDPOINT.explain mutation put_order MutationCtx order: Orders::set order OrdersParams limit: OrderRow BY_OWNER: SqlQuery::new orders.by_owner SELECT _key total FROM WHERE ORDER DESC LIMIT limit require_index BY_OWNER.run guest crate root: flower_sdk::export"},{"t":"SQL queries","h":"Build, deploy and execute","u":"guides/sql.html#execute","d":0,"x":"The repository includes a complete sql-queries guest. With a server running, set FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN for deployment and FLOWER_TOKEN for application calls as required by your server and module. Contents of order.json for the repository example: Contents of query.json : --args-file - reads standard input. Add --contract .flower/sql-queries.manifest.json to validate the public method and argument shape locally. SQL syntax and binding are checked by the host when it executes the module query. Over HTTP, use the same arguments with POST /v1/query ; there is no separate administrative SQL endpoint.","k":"sql-queries FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN FLOWER_TOKEN order.json query.json --args-file - --contract .flower/sql-queries.manifest.json POST /v1/query cargo flower build package profile deploy demo module shop wasm sql-queries.wasm call name put_order mutation args-file sql_explain order owner status open total statement SELECT _key FROM orders WHERE ORDER DESC LIMIT limit params"},{"t":"SQL queries","h":"Parameters and result contracts","u":"guides/sql.html#contracts","d":0,"x":"SqlQuery<P, R> binds its statement and parameter/result schemas at deployment. run(ctx, &params) returns Vec<R> . Each plan is a component and must be registered. SqlEndpoint::run accepts any FlowerType parameter object, including Value , and returns projected JSON objects. run_as<R> decodes rows into a chosen result contract. Supply exactly the named parameters the statement uses, with an empty object or null when none are needed. The dynamic binder infers types from values and column comparisons. Decimal columns require exact decimal strings; a JSON number cannot substitute for one. Explicit null and absent data remain distinct. Redacted or absent projected fields are omitted; typed respon","k":"SqlQuery<P, R> run(ctx, &params) Vec<R> SqlEndpoint::run FlowerType Value run_as<R> Omittable<T> IS NULL"},{"t":"SQL queries","h":"Supported dialect","u":"guides/sql.html#dialect","d":0,"x":"Statements select from one local collection, using * or projections, aliases, named parameters, comparisons, IS NULL , IS NOT NULL , AND , OR , NOT , ordering and a mandatory LIMIT . Access the primary key with _key , tuple keys with _key[n] , and declared nested fields through paths. LIMIT must be between 1 and 10,000. Ordering follows logical values, including exact decimal integers, with a deterministic key tie-breaker. Joins, aggregates, arbitrary functions, writes and continuation cursors are not implemented. Results are complete for the statement's limit at one snapshot.","k":"* IS NULL IS NOT NULL AND OR NOT LIMIT _key _key[n]"},{"t":"SQL queries","h":"Access paths and limits","u":"guides/sql.html#budgets","d":0,"x":"The planner can use primary-key equality, a complete secondary-index equality bucket, or a bounded collection scan. require_index() rejects a named plan at deployment, or a dynamic statement at execution, when no supported index path is proven. Legacy index ordering does not support SQL range pushdown or partial-prefix optimization. SqlBudget::new(candidates, bytes, work) configures positive ceilings. scan remains an equivalent constructor. Maxima are 10,000 candidates, 64 MiB and 10,000,000 work units. Hidden rows still count; LIMIT bounds returned rows and does not bound scan input. Dynamic parsing and binding consume the endpoint's budget. Statements are limited to 16 KiB and named parame","k":"require_index() SqlBudget::new(candidates, bytes, work) scan"},{"t":"SQL queries","h":"Explain and diagnostics","u":"guides/sql.html#explain","d":0,"x":"Both handles offer explain returning Value and explain_typed returning SqlExplain . The typed view includes the access path, collection, proven input bound and resource ceilings, and retains extension fields in raw . Dynamic explain binds the supplied parameters without reading rows; its execution ceilings reflect the remaining budget after binding. The CLI command explains a named plan offline. For a request-time statement, call the module's sql_explain query. SQL_INVALID covers syntax, binding and unsupported access paths. Parser diagnostics include zero-based UTF-8 start_byte and exclusive end_byte ; end-of-input failures have an empty span. SQL_BUDGET reports exhaustion; evaluator failur","k":"explain Value explain_typed SqlExplain raw sql_explain SQL_INVALID start_byte end_byte SQL_BUDGET details.budget INVALID_ARGUMENT ACCESS_DENIED cargo flower sql-explain manifest sql-queries.manifest.json plan orders.open"},{"t":"SQL queries","h":"Compatibility","u":"guides/sql.html#compatibility","d":0,"x":"SQL host operations and decimal schemas require state-machine compatibility version 10. The stored value format remains 4. Existing store-contract checks require backup/restore into a fresh directory across a contract change; see the upgrade procedure . Value-ordered indexes, snapshot-retaining pagination and cross-group recovery changes remain future work.","k":""},{"t":"Errors and retries in clients","h":"Errors and retries in clients","u":"guides/errors-retries.html","d":1,"x":"Every failed call falls into one of three cases: the application said no, nothing ran, or you cannot tell. Retrying is only right in the last two, and in the last one only with the same request ID.","k":""},{"t":"Errors and retries in clients","h":"Three cases","u":"guides/errors-retries.html#three-cases","d":0,"x":"Case What you see over HTTP What to do The application said no 422 with \"ok\":false : the call's own failure, for queries, mutations and transactions alike. Your method's code , message and details ; INVALID_ARGUMENT with details.path for arguments that do not match; stale_revision ; deadline_exceeded when the call ran past its own declared deadline. Also 403 (authorization), 404 UNKNOWN_METHOD , 400 bad_request (malformed body), 413 (too large) and 415 (no JSON content type). Never retry blindly: the same request gets the same answer. Show it, or fix the call. Nothing ran 503 with overloaded (and Retry-After ), caller_deadline_exceeded (your own deadline passed first), quorum , forward , rec","k":"\"ok\":false code message details INVALID_ARGUMENT details.path stale_revision deadline_exceeded UNKNOWN_METHOD bad_request overloaded Retry-After caller_deadline_exceeded quorum forward recovering rejoining stale_replica storage_failed leadership_transfer partition_moving instantiation_failed module_unavailable 503 leader_isolated 503 forward_failed \"duplicate\": true false revision position INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS balance duplicate path amount arguments transfer invalid amount: integer below minimum expected database methods nope callable over HTTP declared http bank module nomod nowhere here Failed parse request body JSON: while parsing value line column caller deadline passed"},{"t":"Errors and retries in clients","h":"What flower-client does for you","u":"guides/errors-retries.html#flower-client","d":0,"x":"With retries on ( .retry(Retry::Default) on the builder, or RequestOptions::retrying() / MutationOptions::retrying() per call), the client: retries transport errors, attempt timeouts, 429, 502, 504 and every 503 except forward , up to 8 attempts with equal-jitter exponential backoff from 250 ms to 30 s; retries overload refusals on their own budget — 60 seconds by default, not counted against the attempts — waiting at least what Retry-After asks; sends the same request ID, and the same body, on every attempt of a mutation; never retries a 200 or a 422 (so never a business failure), a 401 or 403, or any other status; stops at the call's deadline and when its CancellationToken fires. RetryPoli","k":".retry(Retry::Default) RequestOptions::retrying() MutationOptions::retrying() forward Retry-After deadline CancellationToken RetryPolicy attempts delays(initial, max) attempt_timeout until(instant) through_overload overload_budget client.overloads()"},{"t":"Errors and retries in clients","h":"The error type","u":"guides/errors-retries.html#the-error-type","d":0,"x":"Every call returns Result<_, flower_client::FlowerError> : Variant Meaning Business { code, message, revision, position, details } The call's own failure, and only that: a 422 answer carrying the \"ok\":false envelope, from a query, a mutation or a transaction. Server { code, message } Any other non-2xx answer: UNKNOWN_METHOD , bad_request , a failure of the server itself. A body that is not JSON (a proxy's page, the 413 body limit) gives code: \"status 413\" and the text. Auth(_) 401 or 403. The server's body ( unauthorized , ACCESS_DENIED …) is not kept. RetryExhausted(_) Retryable refusals until the policy gave up — or the first one when retries are off. Forward { leader } The member could no","k":"Result<_, flower_client::FlowerError> Business { code, message, revision, position, details } \"ok\":false Server { code, message } UNKNOWN_METHOD bad_request code: \"status 413\" Auth(_) unauthorized ACCESS_DENIED RetryExhausted(_) Forward { leader } Timeout Transport(_) Cancelled Decode(_) HistoryChanged Engine(_) client.call *_declared #[flower(retryable)] true is_retryable() What failed call means caller. advice error: FlowerError static error.is_auth return credentials refused error.execution flower_client::Execution::Unknown outcome unknown: reuse persisted command request error.is_transient retry with same identity after server hint match error failure HTTP declared arguments INVALID_ARGUMENT deadline passing deadline_exceeded Final show FlowerError::Business application said hook access rule FlowerError::Auth write have committed. FlowerError::Timeout FlowerError::Transport FlowerError::Cancelled unknown outcome: Legacy transport errors structured retain certainty separately. FlowerError::RetryExhausted FlowerError::Forward again later alias malformed body large failure: misconfiguration transient state. FlowerError::Server FlowerError::Decode"},{"t":"Errors and retries in clients","h":"Request IDs across restarts","u":"guides/errors-retries.html#request-ids-across-restarts","d":0,"x":"The client's retries happen within one call. If your process can crash between sending a write and learning its outcome, the request ID must outlive the process: derive it from the intent, store it with the intent, and send it again on restart. A scoped ID from retry_identity adds a server-side check that a reused ID carries the same request: Receipts last for the database's retry window — by default 24 to 27 hours. A retry after that runs as a new write.","k":"retry_identity async checkout client: FlowerClient cart: Result MutationResult serde_json::Value FlowerError identity client shop RequestOptions::new await same intent gives within retry window store with cart before sending resend after crash. identity.request_id format checkout: as_bytes options MutationOptions::retrying request_id mutate_value json"},{"t":"Errors and retries in clients","h":"Stale revisions","u":"guides/errors-retries.html#optimistic-concurrency","d":0,"x":"A mutation sent with expected_revision fails with Business { code: \"stale_revision\", revision, .. } when the database has moved on; the error's revision is the current one. Retrying the same request can never succeed: re-read, decide again, and send a new request.","k":"expected_revision Business { code: \"stale_revision\", revision, .. } revision"},{"t":"Errors and retries in clients","h":"From other languages","u":"guides/errors-retries.html#from-other-languages","d":0,"x":"Any HTTP client works the same way. Send Content-Type: application/json , put a stable idempotency_key in every write's body, and set x-flower-timeout-ms to the time budget. Inspect the structured problem's origin, execution certainty and retry advice alongside status and code. A 422 can report a declared application error, SQL validation or budget failure, or another host refusal; status alone does not identify a declared error. The request-body limit's 413 can answer in plain text. The HTTP reference lists the endpoints and response fields.","k":"Content-Type: application/json idempotency_key x-flower-timeout-ms"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Authentication and authorization","u":"guides/auth.html","d":1,"x":"The server does not know your users. Each module decides who a caller is with an authorization component, each method declares who may call it, and collections can protect rows and fields with declarative access policies the engine enforces on every read and write.","k":""},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"How a call is authorized","u":"guides/auth.html#the-pipeline","d":0,"x":"The server resolves the name. Only methods declared http answer; any other name answers 404 UNKNOWN_METHOD , whether or not the module defines it. The server turns the request's credentials into a JSON value: the body's credentials member if present, otherwise {\"bearer\": \"<token>\"} from an Authorization: Bearer header — unless the token is one of the server's own tokens, which are never shown to guest code — otherwise null . Watches are requests like the others: the same body, the same header. If the module lists an Authorization component, its hook runs on the call's own snapshot. It authenticates the caller into a principal and checks the method's access, or refuses. A refusal answers 403 ","k":"http 404 UNKNOWN_METHOD credentials {\"bearer\": \"<token>\"} Authorization: Bearer null Authorization 403 unauthorized UNAUTHENTICATED: authentication required FORBIDDEN: access denied ctx.principal()"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Bearer JWTs with JwtBearer","u":"guides/auth.html#jwt-bearer","d":0,"x":"The usual setup verifies JSON Web Tokens from an identity provider. JwtBearer is a ready-made typed authenticator: Keys. BearerKey::Pem(pem, &[algorithms]) takes a PEM public key; BearerKey::Bytes(bytes, KeyFormat::Raw | Der, &[algorithms]) an HS256 secret or a DER public key; BearerKey::Managed(&KEY) a managed key from the cluster's catalog (for tokens your own module issued with that key). Algorithms are Hs256 , Rs256 , Es256 and EdDsa . There is no JWKS fetching: guests have no network, so embed the provider's keys or store them in a collection and use a callback hook. Claims. exp is required. issuer makes iss required and exact. The audience check is strict both ways: with an audience li","k":"JwtBearer BearerKey::Pem(pem, &[algorithms]) BearerKey::Bytes(bytes, KeyFormat::Raw | Der, &[algorithms]) BearerKey::Managed(&KEY) Hs256 Rs256 Es256 EdDsa exp issuer iss audience aud alg typ kid claims_principal sub tenant claims principal fn(&Value) -> Result<Option<Principal>> {\"bearer\": t} {\"token\": t} null UNAUTHENTICATED BearerKey::Managed ES256 public from your identity provider PEM. const IDP_PUBLIC_KEY: BEGIN PUBLIC KEY----- n... n-----END static BEARER: issuer: Some https: auth.example.com audience: notes-api clock_tolerance_seconds: JwtBearer::new BearerKey::Pem IDP_PUBLIC_KEY Algorithm::Es256 List among components most module. AUTHORIZATION: Authorization authenticate: Authentication::Typed BEARER Methods that declare access need signed-in caller... default: Access::Authenticated retry sessions. sessions: Access what other macros external operations interface providers name. methods: method declares where differs default. query http health ctx: QueryCtx String String::from"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Method access","u":"guides/auth.html#access-levels","d":0,"x":"The Authorization component says how callers authenticate and what access applies where a method declares none: Field Meaning authenticate Authentication::Typed(&authenticator) , Callback(fn) , Gateway (trust the principal a gateway holding the admin token asserts) or Anonymous . default Access for every method that declares none. sessions Access for opening, reading, acknowledging and closing retry sessions . methods Access for definitions declared where no access option reaches, by name: an external! value's operations, an interface provider's methods. Usually empty. A method states its own access in its declaration, where it differs from the default: #[query(access = public)] , access = a","k":"Authorization authenticate Authentication::Typed(&authenticator) Callback(fn) Gateway Anonymous default sessions methods access external! #[query(access = public)] access = authenticated access = path::to::check .access(Access::..) Transaction::new(..).access(..) Access::Public Access::Authenticated UNAUTHENTICATED Access::Check(fn) FORBIDDEN MethodCtx static APP: App::new AUTHORIZATION Notes HEALTH WHOAMI NOTE RETITLE FORGET_PRIVATE derive FlowerType Clone Debug PartialEq struct subject: Option String tenant: query http authenticated whoami ctx: QueryCtx Result principal ctx.principal principal.as_ref p.subject.clone principal.and_then p.tenant"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Custom hooks","u":"guides/auth.html#callback-hooks","d":0,"x":"When credentials are not JWTs — opaque session tokens, API keys, signed requests — write the hook yourself. It is a query: it can read collections (with the module's own rights), read the clock and use guest crypto, but it cannot write. Here opaque session tokens are looked up in a sessions collection, and methods are open only to callers of the tenant they name: Over HTTP, a missing or unknown token, or a caller of another tenant, is refused before the method runs: State-changing checks — consuming a one-time code, counting attempts — belong in a mutation, not the hook: the hook commits nothing. Tenants and partitions The hook is not told which database it serves. If tenants live in their o","k":"sessions same_tenant Opaque session tokens: decides caller until expires changes. by_session ctx: QueryCtx credentials: Value _request: AuthRequest Result Option Principal token match credentials Value::Null return None Value::Object fields fields.get bearer Some Value::String token.clone fail UNAUTHENTICATED expected Sessions::get else unknown clock decision then kept expiry. ctx.clock session.expires_at expired ctx.changes_at Principal::new session.user session.tenant Only callers tenant arguments name. _ctx: principal: args: bool named args principal.and_then p.tenant.as_deref mine false Every method checks unless declares otherwise whoami does. static SESSION_AUTHORIZATION: Authorization authenticate: Authentication::Callback default: Access::Check sessions: Access::Authenticated methods: code unauthorized message UNAUTHENTICATED: authentication required true value subject acme FORBIDDEN: access denied globex"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Decision reuse and expiry","u":"guides/auth.html#decision-expiry","d":0,"x":"Running the hook on every call would be wasteful, so the engine keeps its decisions in a memo (4 MiB by default, --decision-memo-bytes ). A kept decision answers a later call with the same credentials while all of these hold: the module's code has not changed; nothing the hook read has changed — deleting the session row ends every decision that read it; the decision has not expired: JwtBearer declares the token's exp (plus tolerance), and a callback hook declares with ctx.changes_at . The earliest declaration wins, so a session expiring in an hour cannot extend a token expiring in a minute; for a hook that reads the arguments (callbacks and Check predicates), the arguments are the same. Deni","k":"--decision-memo-bytes JwtBearer exp ctx.changes_at Check now() clock() changes_at by_session"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Watches","u":"guides/auth.html#watches","d":0,"x":"A watch presents credentials as a query does, in its body or its Authorization header. Every watch delivery is authorized at the revision it shows. The server rechecks each subscriber when something the decision read changes, when the decision expires, when a managed key it used changes, and at least every 30 seconds. A subscriber who is no longer allowed gets an error event with code unauthorized and the stream ends; one whose principal changed (new claims, another tenant) gets watch_scope_changed and should reconnect.","k":"Authorization error unauthorized watch_scope_changed"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Access policies","u":"guides/auth.html#access-policies","d":0,"x":"Method access decides who may call. Access policies decide which rows and fields each caller may read and write, whatever method they call. A policy is a set of rules over operands — the stored row ( row ), the row being written ( next ), the key, the principal ( subject , tenant , claim ), the time ( now ) — attached to a collection with its access option, which names a fn() -> Policy : A raw Collection takes the same function with .access(notes_policy) . Rule Checked on Sees read gets, scans, index ranges, history the stored row, key, principal, time insert writing a new key the written row ( next ) update replacing an existing row both row and next delete deleting the stored row write sho","k":"row next subject tenant claim now access fn() -> Policy Collection .access(notes_policy) read insert update delete write field(name, FieldAccess) Policy::new() row.owner == subject Rule::stored(collection, key) Rule::readable(collection, key) Derived::new(…).access(fn() -> Rule) row.f == subject f derive FlowerType Clone Debug PartialEq struct Note owner: String title: Hidden from everyone absent when redacted. private: Field collection with policy. name notes value index by_owner fields owner types notes_policy Notes Policy mine Operand::row Operand::subject admin Operand::claim role Operand::literal Policy::new Rule::authenticated Operand::next mine.clone field private FieldAccess::new"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Hidden rows and redacted fields","u":"guides/auth.html#redaction","d":0,"x":"A row the caller may not read is invisible: get returns None , and scans and ranges drop it before applying limits and cursors. A visible row loses the fields the caller may not read — the member is absent, not null — and a typed row decodes it as Field::Redacted : Writing back a row with hidden fields keeps their stored values, so a read-modify-write by a caller who cannot see everything loses nothing. A Field::Redacted cannot be encoded ( REDACTED_VALUE ), so write the visible fields, as retitle does. To remove a field, including one the caller cannot see, use set_clearing : it takes the field's write rule. A refused write fails with ACCESS_DENIED . In a mutation that is the call's own fai","k":"get None Field::Redacted REDACTED_VALUE retitle set_clearing ACCESS_DENIED derive FlowerType Clone Debug PartialEq struct NoteView title: String private: Option query http note ctx: QueryCtx Result Rows caller read come back fields Notes::get note.title match note.private Field::Visible text Some mutation MutationCtx else return fail NOT_FOUND such redacted field cannot encoded write engine keeps stored values ones not. alloc::collections::BTreeMap::new row.insert String::from owner Value::String note.owner title private ctx.set CollectionRef::local Notes::NAME Value::Object forget_private Remove outright even this that takes rule. ctx.set_clearing"},{"t":"Authentication and authorization","h":"Gateways and server tokens","u":"guides/auth.html#gateways","d":0,"x":"The server has three tokens of its own, configured on cargo flower serve : the admin token (operator endpoints), the worker token (queue endpoints) and the sync token (replica sync). None of them is ever passed to a hook. A request bearing the admin token is a trusted gateway : it may assert the principal a call runs as (body principal : {\"subject\", \"tenant\"?, \"claims\"?} ) and the time a mutation commits at ( timestamp_ms ), in call and watch bodies alike; from anyone else both fields are ignored. A hook decides what to make of an asserted principal — Authentication::Gateway trusts it outright, while JwtBearer still wants a token. Use this for a backend that authenticates users itself and ca","k":"cargo flower serve principal {\"subject\", \"tenant\"?, \"claims\"?} timestamp_ms Authentication::Gateway JwtBearer"},{"t":"Guest cryptography","h":"Guest cryptography","u":"guides/crypto.html","d":1,"x":"Guests hash, sign, verify, encrypt and run passkey ceremonies through the host: no primitive runs in Wasm, and managed secret keys never enter guest memory. Each database keeps its keys in a replicated catalog that operators provision and rotate.","k":""},{"t":"Guest cryptography","h":"The crypto host","u":"guides/crypto.html#setup","d":0,"x":"Every server runs guest crypto: hashing, randomness, JWTs and JWEs with keys the guest supplies, and WebAuthn need no configuration. Every operation goes through ctx.crypto() , which query, mutation and derived contexts all offer; it borrows the context, so create one, use it and drop it. Bytes are plain Rust slices; hex_encode / hex_decode and webauthn::base64url_encode / base64url_decode convert for the wire. Crypto failures are ordinary failures you can map to your own codes, not traps. Managed keys are the one part that needs an operator. Each database's catalog is sealed by a 32-byte wrapping key that every member mounts: A member started without one still applies the catalogs it replic","k":"ctx.crypto() hex_encode hex_decode webauthn::base64url_encode base64url_decode KEYS_LOCKED head urandom wrapping.key keep secret back cargo flower serve data bootstrap admin-token key-wrapping-file"},{"t":"Guest cryptography","h":"Hashing and randomness","u":"guides/crypto.html#determinism","d":0,"x":"Everything except randomness is deterministic, so queries, derived values and authorization hooks can hash, verify and decrypt. random_bytes (at most 1,024 bytes a call) and anything that draws randomness — key pairs, WebAuthn challenges, JWE without an explicit nonce — works only in mutations; elsewhere it fails with INVALID_INVOCATION . A mutation's random output is decided once on the leader and replicated, like the rest of its effects.","k":"random_bytes INVALID_INVOCATION Hashing deterministic queries query http fingerprint ctx: QueryCtx text: String Result digest ctx.crypto sha256 text.as_bytes hex_encode Entropy mutations only: their outcome decided once leader replicated. mutation new_api_key MutationCtx bytes base64url_encode"},{"t":"Guest cryptography","h":"Managed keys","u":"guides/crypto.html#managed-keys","d":0,"x":"A module declares the keys it uses by an alias, an algorithm and the usages it needs. An operator generates (or imports) a key in the database's catalog and binds it to that alias, granting usages. Both sides must agree: an undeclared alias fails FORBIDDEN_USAGE , an unbound one UNKNOWN_KEY ( managed key \"tickets\" is not bound ). Each declaration is a component; list it with the rest: The operator side, with the admin token, names the database whose catalog it changes ( --db , before or after the subcommand): Each change prints its request ID and the catalog's new revision, then the catalog; the listing alone is: A change is replicated through the database's group and runs under a request ID","k":"FORBIDDEN_USAGE UNKNOWN_KEY managed key \"tickets\" is not bound --db --request-id keys import keys --key-cache-bytes box_after box_open_after derive encrypt decrypt Ed25519 P256 Rsa Hs256 A256Gcm dir A256GCM XSalsa20Poly1305 X25519 keys rotate retire revoke destroy kid flower.<key id>.<version> app this module uses alias operator binds. Each component: list among components. declaration grants nothing itself: binding must exist grant same usages. static TICKETS: KeyDeclaration KeyDeclaration::new tickets Algorithm::Ed25519 Usage::Sign Usage::Verify Usage::PublicKey VAULT: vault Algorithm::A256Gcm Usage::Encrypt Usage::Decrypt APP: App::new TICKETS VAULT Challenges Passkeys FINGERPRINT NEW_API_KEY ISSUE_TICKET TICKET_HOLDER TICKET_KEY SEAL UNSEAL BEGIN_REGISTRATION FINISH_REGISTRATION BEGIN_SIGN_IN FINISH_SIGN_IN export FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN secret cargo flower generate name ticket-signing algorithm vault-2026 bind usages sign verify publicKey active versions live seal key-file private.key wrapping-file wrapping.key sealed.json shop request-id import-boxes import boxes sealed-file"},{"t":"Guest cryptography","h":"Signed tokens (JWT)","u":"guides/crypto.html#jwt","d":0,"x":"A ticket's protected header names the binding's algorithm and the version that signed it: With a managed key, the binding fixes the algorithm and the kid . With raw keys — JwtKey::Bytes(secret, KeyFormat::Raw) for HS256 (at least 32 bytes), KeyFormat::Der or JwtKey::Pem for public keys — signing needs SignOptions::algorithm and verifying a non-empty VerifyOptions::algorithms allowlist: the token's own alg never decides. Verification checks exp (required unless require_expiration: Some(false) ), nbf , and, when you ask, iss , sub and typ . The audience must be validated explicitly: a token carrying aud fails unless you set audience . Check custom claims yourself. Verification does not poll th","k":"kid JwtKey::Bytes(secret, KeyFormat::Raw) KeyFormat::Der JwtKey::Pem SignOptions::algorithm VerifyOptions::algorithms alg exp require_expiration: Some(false) nbf iss sub typ aud audience CRYPTO_ERROR JWT expired ticket_holder Issue signed ticket under tickets active version. mutation http issue_ticket ctx: MutationCtx guest: String Result ctx.clock claims BTreeMap::new claims.insert String::from Value::String ISSUER guest Value::Number floor TICKETS.key binding fixes algorithm EdDSA header. ctx.crypto sign Value::Object SignOptions::default Check ticket: token picks version issued before rotation keep verifying until that revoked. answer cached expires. query QueryCtx options VerifyOptions validation: ValidationOptions issuer: Some ValidationOptions::default VerifyOptions::default verified crypto verify map_err failure fail INVALID_TICKET failure.message match verified.claims claims.get subject subject.clone names derive FlowerType Clone Debug PartialEq struct PublicKey kid: Ed25519: byte base64url. Publish verification others check with JOSE library wrap JWKS document they expect ticket_key crypto.managed_version public crypto.managed_public_key ManagedKeyRef::Current base64url_encode flower.kVmQNyYF7VeHo0uqfYzyIQ.1 true value sOCdOyVfLhz-zEL-F7eeTRE2geKUoQQXNi9hjGUlbAE"},{"t":"Guest cryptography","h":"Encrypted tokens (JWE)","u":"guides/crypto.html#jwe","d":0,"x":"Only compact dir + A256GCM is supported, with a managed A256Gcm key or a raw 32-byte key. Pass an explicit 12-byte nonce to encrypt outside a mutation, and never reuse one under the same key. decrypt validates standard claims like verify .","k":"dir A256GCM A256Gcm decrypt verify Seal value later readable only through this module. mutation http seal ctx: MutationCtx secret: String Result ctx.clock claims BTreeMap::new claims.insert String::from secret Value::String Value::Number floor _400.0 VAULT.key None fresh random nonce which mutation. ctx.crypto encrypt Value::Object Default::default query unseal QueryCtx token: Value opened crypto token ValidationOptions::default opened.claims"},{"t":"Guest cryptography","h":"Passkeys (WebAuthn)","u":"guides/crypto.html#webauthn","d":0,"x":"A WebAuthn ceremony takes two mutations: one creates options with a fresh challenge and stores it, the other consumes the challenge and verifies the browser's response. begin_registration returns PublicKeyCredentialCreationOptionsJSON for the browser: The option builders default to discoverable credentials, required user verification and no attestation, offering EdDSA, ES256 and RS256. RegistrationOptions::new defaults to preferred user verification, so set it to match the options, as above. The host checks the challenge, the exact origin, the RP ID hash, user presence and verification, the credential algorithm and the signature. Attestation statements other than none are not evaluated. Stor","k":"begin_registration PublicKeyCredentialCreationOptionsJSON RegistrationOptions::new none sign_count WEBAUTHN_COUNTER flower_sdk::crypto::webauthn::Authentication flower_sdk::auth::Authentication const RP_ID: example.com ORIGIN: https: Step options navigator.credentials.create challenge random this mutation stored step http ctx: MutationCtx user_handle: String name: Result Value init RegistrationInit RelyingParty String::from RP_ID Example user: RegistrationUser opaque base64url handle bytes never email. user_handle.clone name display_name: None RegistrationInit::default ctx.crypto webauthn registration_options expires_at ctx.clock _000.0 Challenge value: options.challenge.clone Challenges::set user_handle options.to_value verify browser PublicKeyCredential.toJSON text consuming store credential. finish_registration response: Some Challenges::get else return fail NO_CHALLENGE start registration again Challenges::delete challenge.expires_at CHALLENGE_EXPIRED RegistrationOptions Verify under policy asked for. user_verification: UserVerification::Required challenge.value ORIGIN crypto verify_registration response credential registration.credential passkey Passkey public_key: credential.public_key.clone sign_count: credential.sign_count Passkeys::set credential.id Sign-in stores from authentication_options above check assertion keep signature counter. finish_sign_in credential_id: challenge: Passkeys::get credential_id UNKNOWN_PASSKEY such AuthenticationOptions origins: alloc::vec "},{"t":"Guest cryptography","h":"Other primitives","u":"guides/crypto.html#other-primitives","d":0,"x":"NaCl ( crypto.nacl() ): secretbox, box, Ed25519 signing and detached signatures with explicit 24-byte nonces, SHA-512, and constant-time comparison, in NaCl's byte layouts. Raw managed operations : managed_sign , managed_verify , managed_public_key and managed_version on any declared key, plus typed shortcuts for managed Ed25519 keys: sign , verify and public_key (which also reads an X25519 key). There is no HMAC with a raw key (use a managed Hs256 key) and no JWKS fetching (guests have no network). Error codes: CRYPTO_ERROR , UNKNOWN_KEY , UNKNOWN_VERSION , FORBIDDEN_USAGE , KEYS_LOCKED (the member has no wrapping key mounted), KEY_REVOKED , KEY_DESTROYED , WEBAUTHN_COUNTER ; see Error code","k":"crypto.nacl() managed_sign managed_verify managed_public_key managed_version sign verify public_key Hs256 CRYPTO_ERROR UNKNOWN_KEY UNKNOWN_VERSION FORBIDDEN_USAGE KEYS_LOCKED KEY_REVOKED KEY_DESTROYED WEBAUTHN_COUNTER"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Worker queues","u":"guides/workers.html","d":1,"x":"flower-worker runs queue workers in your own Rust processes: it claims jobs, runs your handler, renews leases while it works, reports outcomes, adapts its concurrency to load, and drains cleanly when told to stop. Jobs come from guest mutations, into queues their modules declare (see Queues and timers ). This guide is about the other end. A native worker may name any queue of the database; it claims, renews and reports, but never enqueues.","k":"flower-worker"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Setup","u":"guides/workers.html#setup","d":0,"x":"Cargo.toml Queue endpoints need the server's worker token ( serve --worker-token / FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN ) or the admin token. Give workers the worker token: it can claim and report jobs and nothing else.","k":"serve --worker-token FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN dependencies flower-client path flower crates flower-worker serde version features derive serde_json tokio macros rt-multi-thread signal"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"A worker","u":"guides/workers.html#a-worker","d":0,"x":"The handler gets a Claim — payload , attempts (this claim included), the fence token, the job's scope , priority , name and group — and returns: Handler returns Report Effect Ok(()) complete The job is done and deleted. Err(WorkError::Retry(Some(delay))) fail Pending again after delay , or dead once its attempts are spent. Err(WorkError::Retry(None)) fail As above, after the default backoff: 2 s after the first attempt, doubling, capped at 60 s ( .retry(base, max) ). Err(WorkError::Terminal(reason)) kill Dead now, whatever attempts remain. The reason stays in your process. a panic fail As Retry(None) . run_queue_worker returns a WorkerSummary counting completed , failed , killed , lost (leas","k":"Claim payload attempts fence scope priority name group Ok(()) Err(WorkError::Retry(Some(delay))) delay Err(WorkError::Retry(None)) .retry(base, max) Err(WorkError::Terminal(reason)) Retry(None) run_queue_worker WorkerSummary completed failed killed lost drained client.complete_queue_with(…) claim.fence derive Deserialize struct Email String subject: Stand-in your mail provider client. async send_email fence: Result println subject run_mailer FlowerError token std::env::var FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN unwrap_or_default client FlowerClient::builder http: credentials Credentials::token build options QueueWorkerOptions::new acme owner mailer-1 Between handlers once following demand load. adaptive lease Duration::from_secs Keep place line instead polling. wait Each report claims next jobs same commit. chain stop give then fail what left. drain Arc::new Notify::new on_signal stop.clone tokio::spawn move tokio::signal::ctrl_c await on_signal.notify_one claim email: serde_json::from_value claim.payload map_err error WorkError::Terminal error.to_string email.to email.subject Fail comes back after default backoff. WorkError::Retry None"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Leases","u":"guides/workers.html#leases","d":0,"x":"Each claim holds a lease ( .lease(…) , 30 seconds by default, at most an hour). The worker renews it every third of the lease while your handler runs. If a renewal is refused — the job was settled or taken over elsewhere — or none lands before the lease would end, the worker drops your handler's future and counts the job lost ; it will be delivered again. Choose a lease comfortably longer than a renewal round trip, not longer than the time you are willing to wait for a crashed worker's jobs to come back.","k":".lease(…) lost"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Concurrency and backpressure","u":"guides/workers.html#concurrency","d":0,"x":".concurrency(n) runs at most n handlers (4 by default). .adaptive(min, initial, max) adjusts the limit: it grows while work keeps coming and the process is healthy, and cuts back when the process is busy (Tokio runtime saturation, memory on Linux) or Flower sheds load. Supply your own load signal with .health(…) , and observe changes with .on_event(…) . Whenever the server refuses work for load ( 503 overloaded ), the worker pauses claiming for a second and halves an adaptive limit. The worker claims only what it can start now. Once claims come back full, up to .claimers(n) (4) claims run in parallel; a short claim falls back to one per .poll_interval(…) (1 s).","k":".concurrency(n) n .adaptive(min, initial, max) .health(…) .on_event(…) 503 overloaded .claimers(n) .poll_interval(…)"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Lines and chained claims","u":"guides/workers.html#lines-and-chains","d":0,"x":"Polling an empty queue wastes requests and adds latency. Two options remove most of it: .wait(d) joins the scope's line . A claim that comes back short keeps the worker's place; the worker then reads readiness (a cheap query that wakes when work arrives) instead of claiming, and the first worker in line gets new work to itself for the queue's turn (one second by default; the declaring module sets it with TypedQueue::new(\"mail\").turn_ms(..) ). One process wakes per job instead of all of them. Stopping gives the place up. .chain() makes every report also claim the next jobs, in the same commit: one round trip per job instead of two.","k":".wait(d) TypedQueue::new(\"mail\").turn_ms(..) .chain()"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Idle jobs and throttling","u":"guides/workers.html#job-control","d":0,"x":"run_queue_worker_with passes a JobControl to the handler as well: job.idle() says the job will mostly wait from now on (on a slow remote service, a human): it stops counting against the concurrency limit, so the worker takes other jobs, while its lease keeps being renewed. job.throttle(pause, reason) says the service behind the worker is overloaded: claiming pauses for pause and an adaptive limit halves.","k":"run_queue_worker_with JobControl job.idle() job.throttle(pause, reason) pause async run_renderer client: FlowerClient stop: Notify Result WorkerSummary FlowerError options QueueWorkerOptions::new media render concurrency client stop claim job: move claim.payload slow json true Mostly waiting another service from here: counting against limit worker takes other jobs. job.idle match await RenderError::Busy behind overloaded: claiming. job.throttle Duration::from_secs renderer busy WorkError::Retry Some RenderError::Invalid WorkError::Terminal enum RenderError Busy Invalid String serde_json::Value job.get source None to_string"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Shutdown and drain deadlines","u":"guides/workers.html#shutdown","d":0,"x":"The worker runs until the Notify you passed is notified (use notify_one ). It then stops claiming, hands back anything it claimed but has not started, gives up its place in line, and waits for running handlers. Without .drain(…) it waits for every handler, however long. With .drain(d) , handlers still running after d are dropped at their next .await and their jobs are failed with the default backoff — or released to run again at once with .release_drained() . They are never reported complete. Handlers stuck outside an .await five seconds later are abandoned and come back when their lease lapses. Pair drain with your platform's termination grace period: if Kubernetes waits 30 seconds after SI","k":"Notify notify_one .drain(…) .drain(d) d .await .release_drained() drain"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"When the worker gives up","u":"guides/workers.html#errors","d":0,"x":"Transient failures — network errors, timeouts, overload, no leader — never end a worker; it waits and tries again. A refusal the same request would meet again ends it with that error: revoked credentials ( Auth ), an unknown database, invalid options ( Server { code: \"invalid_options\" } ). queue_purging does not: when the last module declaring a queue is removed and purged, the queue refuses every operation until the purge has deleted it, and the worker waits that out like a transient failure ( FlowerError::is_transient says so), while the module listing ( cargo flower modules ) shows the module purging. Run workers under a supervisor that restarts them and alerts on repeated exits.","k":"Auth Server { code: \"invalid_options\" } queue_purging FlowerError::is_transient cargo flower modules"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"External values with reconcile","u":"guides/workers.html#external-values","d":0,"x":"Some values are computed outside Flower — a word count from a service, a price from a supplier — but should behave like derived values. A guest declares an external value with flower_sdk::external! : an input computed from the database, and a contract for the result. Listed among the app's components, it brings its collections, its trigger and the operations workers use; the http option ( external!(.., http) ) is what lets a worker reach those operations over HTTP, and the authorization component's methods table gives them access other than the default ( (\"words.publish\", Access::Check(worker)) ). The reconcile worker watches for inputs that changed, calls your function with each, and publis","k":"flower_sdk::external! http external!(.., http) methods (\"words.publish\", Access::Check(worker)) reconcile .args(…) .leased() examples/external-transactions crates/flower-worker/tests/reconcile.rs"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Testing workers","u":"guides/workers.html#testing","d":0,"x":"With the testing feature, flower_worker::testing::FakeQueue is an in-memory queue with the server's claiming rules on Tokio's clock, so worker tests run in paused time without a server. See Testing applications .","k":"testing flower_worker::testing::FakeQueue"},{"t":"Worker queues","h":"Typed payloads and lease handles","u":"guides/workers.html#typed-workers","d":0,"x":"run_typed_queue_worker accepts a TypedQueue<Payload, Result> descriptor and passes a decoded TypedJob<Payload> to the handler. The worker renews and settles the lease automatically; incompatible payloads are dead-lettered. Manual workers use claim_typed and its lease handle for heartbeat, typed completion, failure and release, with the database, queue, claim token and fence already bound. Manual claims return one ClaimOutcome<Payload, Result> per item, so an incompatible payload never discards other claims. A decoding error retains the raw payload and its lease. Terminal operations consume the lease; a SettlementError returns it via into_lease() for explicit retry under the same claim identi","k":"run_typed_queue_worker TypedQueue<Payload, Result> TypedJob<Payload> claim_typed ClaimOutcome<Payload, Result> SettlementError into_lease() async settle_claim outcome: flower_client::ClaimOutcome String options: RequestOptions Result flower_client::SettlementError match outcome claim lease complete_with processed into options await payloads retain their value explicit handling. bad.lease kill retry_completion error: original_result: error into_lease original_result"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"Deployments","u":"guides/deployments.html","d":1,"x":"A deployment replaces a module's code through the Raft log, atomically on every member. Build and plan offline, deploy in one call, or stage a large change and activate it when its indexes and derived values are ready.","k":""},{"t":"Deployments","h":"Build","u":"guides/deployments.html#build","d":0,"x":"build compiles the package for wasm32-unknown-unknown (release profile by default), loads the module through the server's own Wasm backend, checks its imports and initialization, and reads the manifest it declares. Only then does it write <package>.wasm and <package>.manifest.json to .flower/ (or --out-dir ) and print the artifact's SHA-256. The manifest — collections, schemas, definitions, HTTP names, access, exports, imports, queues, keys — is generated from the module's declarations and embedded in the Wasm; the JSON copy is for reading, diffing and review.","k":"build wasm32-unknown-unknown <package>.wasm <package>.manifest.json .flower/ --out-dir cargo flower cdylib workspace package tasks name features auth out-dir dist"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"Plan","u":"guides/deployments.html#plan","d":0,"x":"plan checks a deployment offline, without a cluster, and exits non-zero when it would break something. It prints what the module declares and the content identity the deployment will have, which covers the code and its bindings (it is not the file's SHA-256): --current takes the deployed manifest (or the deployed Wasm) and diffs the contracts: removed collections or definitions, changed key types and changed kinds are incompatible. --with MODULE=FILE (repeatable) adds the database's other modules, so the cross-module link check runs offline; --module names the module being deployed. --bind SLOT=MODULE / --bindings FILE bind import slots; bindings change the content identity, which plan print","k":"plan --current --with MODULE=FILE --module --bind SLOT=MODULE --bindings FILE cargo flower contract inspect --manifest M contract diff --from A --to B ! cargo flower wasm tasks.wasm current deployed.manifest.json collections queries mutations derived transactions triggers http bindings: none identity f009ff3a4a23231886633bc7afd20700d1d00cf253c0cba80b0dff5f4047a884 compatible true"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"Deploy","u":"guides/deployments.html#deploy","d":0,"x":"The CLI runs the same preflight as plan and sends nothing if it fails. The leader then validates the module by building its runtime, checks links with the database's other modules ( 409 MODULE_CONFLICT ), builds any new indexes over existing rows, and proposes the activation as one log entry. Every member switches at the same position. Deploying identical content again is a no-op, so retrying a deploy is safe. Two deploys of the same module at once: the second gets 409 deployment_in_progress . Bodies are limited to 64 MiB ( --deploy-max-bytes ).","k":"plan 409 MODULE_CONFLICT 409 deployment_in_progress --deploy-max-bytes export FLOWER_ENDPOINT https: flower.internal:8080 every command default server FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN from your secret store cargo flower deploy shop module wasm shop.wasm bind inventory warehouse"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"What happens to traffic","u":"guides/deployments.html#what-happens-to-traffic","d":0,"x":"Queries read either the old code with the old data or the new code with the new data, never a mix. Mutations prepared under the old code but not yet committed are prepared again under the new code, transparently. Derived values computed by the old code are re-evaluated on their next read (pinned ones eagerly); aggregates with an unchanged VERSION keep their groups. Watches re-evaluate; a watch whose method the new code removed, or no longer declares http , ends with UNKNOWN_METHOD . Timers fire with whatever code is current when they come due.","k":"VERSION http UNKNOWN_METHOD"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"Changing collections and indexes","u":"guides/deployments.html#changing-schemas","d":0,"x":"Adding collections, indexes and methods, or exposing a method over HTTP, is always fine. New indexes are built over existing rows before activation. Changing a collection's value schema is accepted: existing rows stay as they are and stay readable, and new writes validate against the new schema. Migrating old rows is application work — a maintenance task that rewrites a page at a time is a good fit. Make new fields #[flower(default)] so old rows still decode. An index's fields are fixed by its name. To index different fields, add an index under a new name and stop using the old one. ( plan does not catch a changed index; the server refuses it.) Removing collections or indexes retires them; t","k":"#[flower(default)] plan cargo flower deployment … collect"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"Staged deployments","u":"guides/deployments.html#staged-deployments","d":0,"x":"When the new code needs large indexes built or many derived values recomputed, stage it first. The old code keeps serving, writes go to both old and new indexes while the staging builds, and you activate when it is ready: stage , advance and status print the staging's progress; activate prints the revision and log position it switched at, after which status reports none: stage validates the module like a deploy (it requires --manifest , and skips the CLI's local preflight). advance builds a bounded page of index backfill (up to 65,536 records), then rebuilds derived roots under the new code (up to 1,024 a step). Each step is a log entry, so a new leader continues where the old one stopped. a","k":"stage advance status activate --manifest deploy /admin cargo flower deployment shop module wasm shop.wasm manifest shop.manifest.json max-records repeat until ready true cancel collect later: delete what code left behind position retired identity a849945 dc7f1 scanned graph rebuilt revision staging none"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"Bindings","u":"guides/deployments.html#bindings","d":0,"x":"A module that imports a slot ( $inventory ) must be deployed with a binding for it: --bind inventory=warehouse . Bindings are part of the deployed manifest and its identity; to rebind, redeploy the same artifact with another binding. See Slots and bindings .","k":"$inventory --bind inventory=warehouse"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"Removing a module","u":"guides/deployments.html#removing-a-module","d":0,"x":"Removal takes away the module's code, HTTP names, hooks and derived values through the log. Its records, indexes, queues and timers stay, unreachable — its due timers are held rather than spending their attempts — and come back if you deploy the module again. A module that another module imports from cannot be removed. --purge deletes the data too, in the background, a page at a time: records and indexes, timers, maintenance-task state, and the queues the module declared that no other module declares, with their jobs. Until the purge ends, modules lists the module as purging, the module cannot be deployed again, no module declaring one of those queues can be deployed, and every operation on ","k":"--purge modules queue_purging cargo flower shop list remove module legacy purge"},{"t":"Deployments","h":"From CI","u":"guides/deployments.html#from-ci","d":0,"x":"A typical pipeline builds with --locked , runs plan --current against the manifest stored from the last release (failing the job on exit 1), deploys with FLOWER_ENDPOINT and FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN from the CI secret store, and stores the new manifest for next time. cargo flower modules shows each module's deployed identity, which matches the identity plan printed. Module code, not Flower itself Deploying a module never restarts anything. Upgrading the Flower binary is a different operation, with its own rule: a member refuses to open files written under another compatibility contract. See Upgrading Flower .","k":"--locked plan --current FLOWER_ENDPOINT FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN cargo flower modules identity plan"},{"t":"Backups and restore","h":"Backups and restore","u":"guides/backups.html","d":1,"x":"Every member can archive the log continuously into an encrypted backup store — an S3 bucket or a directory. Restore offline to the latest backup, to a named backup, to an exact log position, or to a point in time.","k":""},{"t":"Backups and restore","h":"How it works","u":"guides/backups.html#how-it-works","d":0,"x":"The group's leader periodically takes a base (a full snapshot) and continuously archives the log entries applied after it, in segments. A restore loads a base and replays archived entries. Every object is compressed (zstd) and sealed with AES-256-GCM under a backup key kept in a keystore file, itself sealed by a 32-byte key-encryption key (KEK) you provide. Object names are opaque. Every member runs the backup loop with the same configuration. Members hold log entries they would otherwise purge until the archive has them (the purge hold ), so a new leader continues the same archive without starting over. Retention is by time: every point of the last 30 days stays restorable ( --backup-retent","k":"--backup-retention-ms"},{"t":"Backups and restore","h":"Configure continuous backup","u":"guides/backups.html#configure","d":0,"x":"Give every member the same three settings: where backups go, a keystore file and the KEK file. In S3: S3 reads its settings from FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_REGION , FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT (for another S3 implementation), FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ADDRESSING ( path or virtual ) and FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID , …_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY and …_SESSION_TOKEN ; each falls back to its AWS_ name, and the credentials to AWS's usual chain. Large objects go up as multipart uploads. To back up to a directory instead — shared storage, or one you copy elsewhere — pass --backup-dir /backups/flower (or --backup-url file:///backups/flower ). Flag Environment Default Meaning --backup-url FLOWER_BACKUP_URL off s3://BUCKET/PREF","k":"FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_REGION FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ADDRESSING path virtual FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID …_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY …_SESSION_TOKEN AWS_ --backup-dir /backups/flower --backup-url file:///backups/flower --backup-url FLOWER_BACKUP_URL s3://BUCKET/PREFIX file:///PATH --backup-dir FLOWER_BACKUP_DIR --keystore FLOWER_BACKUP_KEYSTORE --kek-file FLOWER_BACKUP_KEK_FILE --backup-archive-ms FLOWER_BACKUP_ARCHIVE_MS --backup-base-ms FLOWER_BACKUP_BASE_MS --backup-base-bytes FLOWER_BACKUP_BASE_BYTES --backup-retention-ms FLOWER_BACKUP_RETENTION_MS --backup-retention-interval-ms FLOWER_BACKUP_RETENTION_INTERVAL_MS --backup-part-bytes FLOWER_BACKUP_PART_BYTES --backup-hold-max-bytes FLOWER_BACKUP_HOLD_MAX_BYTES --backup-hold-max-entries FLOWER_BACKUP_HOLD_MAX_ENTRIES root groups/<name> <keystore>.<name> head urandom backup.kek keep outside backup store export eu-central-2 else AWS_REGION cargo flower serve data bootstrap admin-token secret backup-url acme-backups keystore backup.keystore kek-file"},{"t":"Backups and restore","h":"Check on backups","u":"guides/backups.html#operate","d":0,"x":"list works offline and prints, oldest first, each backup's ID, manifest, history, the log index it covers and how far a restore from it reaches through the archive ( restorable_through , also as a time): The admin dashboard shows the same, and /v1/metrics reports the last backup.","k":"list restorable_through /v1/metrics export FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN secret FLOWER_BACKUP_URL acme-backups flower FLOWER_BACKUP_KEYSTORE backup.keystore FLOWER_BACKUP_KEK_FILE backup.kek cargo backup status this member loop JSON take full through FLOWER_ENDPOINT offline: reads store itself backup_id d004740955c0e17b282e95e7a5f9a94d objects covered_index c3fbf2d67cb414fb82287ab23b05ac manifest flower-backup e7f1392eb984c241b6264e9f9e0484 created_ms history f5af2bdc0a20ad75 restorable_through_ms bytes ac91a3ee68e13e9630bbb00e1b1c8bf6"},{"t":"Backups and restore","h":"Restore","u":"guides/backups.html#restore","d":0,"x":"Restore is offline: stop the member, restore into its directory, start it again with --bootstrap . To restore a whole cluster, stop every member, restore the same target on each, and start them together. Without a target, the newest state the backups hold: the latest base, then every entry archived after it. --manifest restores one backup exactly as it was taken. --at-ms replays every write stamped at or before that time: the result is at or just before the target, never after it. --index counts in one history; pass --history (as list prints it) to pick another than the latest backup's. Restore registers every module again, so pass the same --guest-* limits the cluster runs with. Each databa","k":"--bootstrap --manifest --at-ms --index --history list --guest-* --key-wrapping-file KEYS_LOCKED with FLOWER_BACKUP_URL FLOWER_BACKUP_KEYSTORE FLOWER_BACKUP_KEK_FILE above cargo flower backup restore data newest state backups hold at-ms time index exactly through manifest taken serve bootstrap admin-token secret key-wrapping-file wrapping.key restored backup_id d004740955c0e17b282e95e7a5f9a94d flower-backup ac91a3ee68e13e9630bbb00e1b1c8bf6 history f5af2bdc0a20ad75 replayed reached_ms start node"},{"t":"Backups and restore","h":"After a restore","u":"guides/backups.html#after-a-restore","d":0,"x":"A restore to an earlier point starts a new history of each database, with a fresh incarnation: Scoped request IDs ( f1: / f2: ), retry sessions and queue claims minted in the old history are refused with wrong_history ; plain idempotency keys replay from restored receipts. Log positions restart; synced device replicas see HistoryChanged and must rebuild. Everything after the target is gone — including work your workers already did in the outside world. Fence the old deployment (it keeps running if you restored elsewhere), and reconcile external effects. Groups restore independently: a restore is not atomic across groups. Upgrades across a compatibility contract A member refuses to open data ","k":"f1: f2: wrong_history HistoryChanged"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Operating a cluster","u":"guides/operations.html","d":1,"x":"One binary serves every member: cargo flower serve . This guide covers running three of them, the credentials they need, changing membership, watching health and load, upgrading Flower, and the knobs worth knowing.","k":"cargo flower serve"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"A three-node cluster","u":"guides/operations.html#a-three-node-cluster","d":0,"x":"Three voters tolerate the loss of one. Give each member its own directory, ID, HTTP address and Raft address, and the same tokens. Only the founding member gets --bootstrap , naming every member's Raft address: On one machine, with loopback addresses and ports 18091–18093, the same three commands give: A write sent to a follower is forwarded to the leader; its Server-Timing shows the hop and which member led: --bootstrap takes effect only on an empty directory. Restarting any member with the same command line is always safe. --advertise-http is the URL clients should use to reach this member; it appears in x-flower-leader-url . It defaults to the listen address, except when listening on all ","k":"--bootstrap Server-Timing --advertise-http x-flower-leader-url --shutdown-timeout-ms export FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN FLOWER_PEER_TOKEN host cargo flower serve http raft advertise-http https: a.flower.internal:8080 bootstrap b.flower.internal:8080 c.flower.internal:8080 state Leader leader Some term last_log last_applied Follower server-timing: forward admission queue eval commit append replicate apply total node desc x-flower-leader-url: http:"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Credentials and TLS","u":"guides/operations.html#credentials-and-tls","d":0,"x":"Flag Environment Grants --admin-token FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN Operator endpoints (deploy, membership, keys, backups, metrics…), the worker and sync endpoints, and trusted-gateway calls. Without it, operator endpoints always answer 403. --worker-token FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN The queue endpoints, for workers. --sync-token FLOWER_SYNC_TOKEN Replica sync, for devices. --peer-token FLOWER_PEER_TOKEN Raft between members. Defaults to the admin token; required whenever --raft is not loopback. --key-wrapping-file FLOWER_KEY_WRAPPING_FILE Not a token: the 32-byte key sealing every database's managed-key catalog . Mount the same file on every member of every group. A member without it serves no catalog key ( K","k":"--admin-token FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN --worker-token FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN --sync-token FLOWER_SYNC_TOKEN --peer-token FLOWER_PEER_TOKEN --raft --key-wrapping-file FLOWER_KEY_WRAPPING_FILE KEYS_LOCKED --key-wrapping-previous cargo flower keys --db … rewrap --endpoint FLOWER_ENDPOINT http://127.0.0.1:8080 --tls-cert --tls-key --raft-tls-cert --raft-tls-key --raft-tls-ca"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Changing membership","u":"guides/operations.html#membership","d":0,"x":"cargo flower membership without --members prints the member's view: leader, voter sets and addresses. With --members , it sends the leader the complete desired voter set: The leader checks every member's identity and software compatibility, refuses a new member that already holds Raft state, waits for new members to catch up, and switches through joint consensus. A change is bounded to 15 seconds; a refused or uncertain one exits non-zero (409 membership ) and may have partly happened, so inspect before retrying, and pass --expected-log-id to make a change conditional on the membership you inspected. Add a member by starting it with an empty directory and no --bootstrap , then including it i","k":"cargo flower membership --members membership --expected-log-id --bootstrap --retain cargo flower leadership --to 2 503 leadership_transfer export FLOWER_ENDPOINT https: a.flower.internal:8080 cargo flower members"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Health, readiness and status","u":"guides/operations.html#health","d":0,"x":"Endpoint Use GET /health/live Liveness: 200 {\"ok\":true} while the process serves. GET /health/ready Readiness: 200 when the member knows a leader, has applied what it knows is committed, is not rejoining and has no storage failure; 503 with a reason otherwise. A process hosting several groups is ready only when all are. GET /v1/status Raft state: {id, state, leader, term, last_log, last_applied} ( cargo flower status ). Route load balancer traffic by readiness. A member that lost its data, or restarted after an unsynced crash, comes back rejoining : unready, casting no votes and refusing replica-local reads until it has caught up with its leader.","k":"GET /health/live 200 {\"ok\":true} GET /health/ready 200 503 reason GET /v1/status {id, state, leader, term, last_log, last_applied} cargo flower status applied committed leader true null rejoining false storage"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Metrics, timing and tracing","u":"guides/operations.html#observability","d":0,"x":"Server-Timing on every query, mutation and transaction answer breaks the request into phases: forward , cache , fence , join , admission , queue , eval , commit , append , replicate , apply , total , plus the answering node and the leader . Always on, and visible in browser dev tools. GET /v1/metrics (admin; cargo flower metrics ): per-database revisions, admission and shedding, reads (lease reads, quorum rounds, forwards), the writer, retention, deployments, snapshots, compaction, query cache, authorization decisions, Wasm pools and per-method costs. OpenTelemetry : set FLOWER_OTEL_ENABLED=1 and the standard OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_* variables (HTTP protobuf or JSON; no gRPC). Spans join an inco","k":"Server-Timing forward cache fence join admission queue eval commit append replicate apply total node leader GET /v1/metrics cargo flower metrics FLOWER_OTEL_ENABLED=1 OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_* traceparent flower.http.* flower.query.* flower.writer.* flower.evaluator.* flower.storage.* flower.raft.* flower.watch.* RUST_LOG info"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"The admin dashboard","u":"guides/operations.html#dashboard","d":0,"x":"Every member serves a dashboard at /admin on its API port: Raft and readiness, replication, databases and modules, queues, deployments and stagings, backups, retention and live activity. It asks for the admin token, keeps it for the tab only, and talks only to its own origin. Actions that change things — taking a backup, transferring leadership, advancing or activating a staging — ask for confirmation. Turn it off with --admin-ui false . The same data streams as server-sent events from GET /v1/admin/stream .","k":"/admin --admin-ui false GET /v1/admin/stream"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Overload","u":"guides/operations.html#overload","d":0,"x":"Flower refuses work it cannot do in time rather than queueing without bound. A refused request answers 503 overloaded with Retry-After ; nothing ran, so clients retry after the delay (the Rust client does). Requests carrying x-flower-timeout-ms are shed as soon as they could no longer finish within it ( 503 caller_deadline_exceeded ), and a write still queued at its deadline never runs. Writes never queue behind reads, and each database gets a fair share of each queue. Flag Default Meaning --query-workers CPU count Concurrent query and watch evaluations. --queued-input-bytes 64 MiB Request bytes queued or running. --admission-max-wait-ms 10,000 Longest wait for admission before refusing. --a","k":"503 overloaded Retry-After x-flower-timeout-ms 503 caller_deadline_exceeded --query-workers --queued-input-bytes --admission-max-wait-ms --admission-target-ms --admission-interval-ms --writer-preparation-workers --guest-timeout-ms timeout --guest-max-timeout-ms --guest-memory-bytes --max-transaction-bytes 413 transaction_too_large --raft-max-frame-bytes"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Durability and storage","u":"guides/operations.html#durability","d":0,"x":"--fsync on (the default) makes every acknowledged write durable on a quorum. off survives process crashes but not power loss. With three or more voters, the leader defers its own log flush while followers alone form a majority ( --leader-flush lazy , the default): one fsync per write on the critical path. Snapshots are taken every 10,000 entries, after 16 MiB applied or after 5 minutes; data files compact themselves online. The defaults suit most deployments. If storage fails, the member stops accepting writes and linearizable reads ( 503 storage_failed ), reports unready, and once the disk accepts writes again exits non-zero so its supervisor restarts it. Acknowledged writes are not lost: t","k":"--fsync on off --leader-flush lazy 503 storage_failed"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Upgrading Flower","u":"guides/operations.html#upgrades","d":0,"x":"Every member carries a compatibility contract : how entries apply, how snapshots and values encode, how peers frame messages, and the Wasm engine. cargo flower membership prints each member's: Same contract, new build (a bugfix release): roll the cluster one member at a time — move leadership away, stop the member, start the new binary on the same directory, wait for it to be ready. Members of one contract join each other whatever their build. Another contract : members of different contracts refuse each other's connections, and a member refuses at start to open files another contract wrote ( incompatible_store , naming both contracts). There is no in-place upgrade across a contract change: ","k":"cargo flower membership incompatible_store compatibility wire7-state12-snapshot1-value4-wasmtime-49.0.0 build"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Raft timing and read leases","u":"guides/operations.html#timing","d":0,"x":"The timing flags derive from --raft-rtt-ms , the round trip between the farthest members (0 on a LAN). With the defaults — 50 ms heartbeats, a 300 ms leader lease, 100–200 ms election timeouts and a 270 ms read lease — a failed leader is replaced in about half a second. Across regions, set --raft-rtt-ms to the measured round trip and let the rest follow. --raft-read-lease-ms 0 disables read leases, making every linearizable read take a quorum round (see Leader leases ). Use identical timing on every member.","k":"--raft-rtt-ms --raft-read-lease-ms 0"},{"t":"Operating a cluster","h":"Groups and partitions","u":"guides/operations.html#groups-and-partitions","d":0,"x":"A group runs on a subset of the root group's members; every server process is a root member. partition create places a database on a group — adopting it in place if it already exists in root . Clients keep using the database name. partition move copies the database while it serves, freezes it briefly, and cuts over; calls during the freeze answer 503 partition_moving . A move that cannot freeze within --partition-freeze-max-ms (30 s) aborts on its own; partition abort stops one by hand. The database's managed-key catalog moves with it. partition resize balances partition counts over the named active groups. It persists the plan and starts durable moves; inspect them with partition . Retrying","k":"partition create root partition move 503 partition_moving --partition-freeze-max-ms partition abort partition resize partition --op group prefer-leader --node serve --leader-placement off cargo flower group create east members acme move west wait resize groups balance-2026-11 prefer-leader node west-on-3 list transactions cross-group flight"},{"t":"Testing applications","h":"Testing applications","u":"guides/testing.html","d":1,"x":"Test guest logic natively against the real engine, without building Wasm or starting a server; test clients against a scripted transport; test workers against an in-memory queue; and keep a few end-to-end tests against a real node.","k":""},{"t":"Testing applications","h":"Guests in process","u":"guides/testing.html#guests-in-process","d":0,"x":"With the SDK's native-host feature, the engine can run your App directly as native Rust: same dispatch, same codecs, same host operations, same storage — no Wasm. Build the guest crate as an rlib too, as the quickstart does, so its tests can link it, and add two dev-dependencies: Cargo.toml Then deploy the module into an engine over a temporary file and call it, as the server would. This test runs the quickstart's module, building its calls from the argument structs and descriptors the attributes declared: tests/tasks.rs NativeRuntime::new(&APP) runs the app in-process; .manifest() is the manifest the Wasm build would embed. Add .with_crypto(…) for guests that use crypto . invoke_mutation an","k":"native-host App rlib NativeRuntime::new(&APP) .manifest() .with_crypto(…) invoke_mutation invoke_query name encode() http external: true CallOptions MutationOptions call credentials principal deadline timestamp_ms add remove crate-type cdylib dependencies flower-sdk path flower crates dev-dependencies flower-engine features flower_engine::engine:: Engine flower_engine::runtime::NativeRuntime flower_engine::storage::GroupStore flower_sdk:: FlowerType Value tasks:: ADD_TASK AddTaskArgs COMPLETE_TASK CompleteTaskArgs OPEN_TASKS OpenTask open engine: args Value::Object Default::default engine demo tasks OPEN_TASKS.name CallOptions::default unwrap Vec::decode test completing_a_task_closes_it std::env::temp_dir join format tasks- redb std::process::id std::fs::remove_file Engine::new GroupStore::create runtime NativeRuntime::new tasks::APP manifest runtime.manifest deploy Box::new argument structs mutation derived encode calls. milk to_string title: options MutationOptions::default ADD_TASK.name assert_eq again engine.invoke_mutation again.unwrap_err code TASK_EXISTS done COMPLETE_TASK.name assert is_empty drop"},{"t":"Testing applications","h":"Clients with MockTransport","u":"guides/testing.html#clients","d":0,"x":"flower_client::testing::MockTransport answers scripted replies in order and records every request: MockReply covers successes ( ok ), business failures ( failure ), HTTP errors ( error ), overload ( overloaded ), missing leaders ( forward ), server-sent event streams ( events , event_stream ) and hangs ( hang , after the script runs out). Recorded requests expose their path, JSON body, headers and credentials, so you can assert on request IDs and deadlines.","k":"flower_client::testing::MockTransport MockReply ok failure error overloaded forward events event_stream hang async counts_with_a_mock flower_client::testing:: MockTransport mock MockTransport::scripted MockReply::ok json client mock.client count query_value RequestOptions::new await unwrap assert_eq count.value mock.requests path query"},{"t":"Testing applications","h":"Workers with FakeQueue","u":"guides/testing.html#workers","d":0,"x":"With flower-worker 's testing feature, FakeQueue implements the queue protocol in memory, with the server's claiming rules — due order, scopes, priorities, fencing, leases, the waiter line and chained claims — on Tokio's clock, so tests can run in paused time: It can also refuse or fail polls and delay answers, to test backpressure and error paths. It does not model job names, groups or kept results.","k":"flower-worker testing FakeQueue async worker_against_a_fake_queue flower_worker::testing::FakeQueue queue FakeQueue::new queue.enqueue mail json example.com stop Arc::new Notify::new worker tokio::spawn run_queue_worker queue.clone QueueWorkerOptions::new concurrency poll_interval Duration::from_millis stop.clone _claim move tokio::time::sleep await stop.notify_one summary worker.await.unwrap unwrap assert_eq summary.completed queue.stats pending"},{"t":"Testing applications","h":"End to end","u":"guides/testing.html#end-to-end","d":0,"x":"For a few tests against the real thing, start a node in a temporary directory on free ports — --http 127.0.0.1:0 --raft 127.0.0.1:0 — and read the addresses it prints: Wait until /health/ready answers 200, deploy the built Wasm with cargo flower deploy or FlowerClient::deploy , and call it. Pass --timer-sweep-ms 100 to make timers fire quickly. Flower's own CLI tests ( crates/cargo-flower/tests/cli.rs ) work this way. Every example in these docs is tested The guest snippets compile for wasm32 , the client snippets compile natively, and the tests on this page run, from web/docs/examples in the repository.","k":"--http 127.0.0.1:0 --raft 127.0.0.1:0 /health/ready cargo flower deploy FlowerClient::deploy --timer-sweep-ms 100 crates/cargo-flower/tests/cli.rs wasm32 web/docs/examples listening raft http"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"HTTP API","u":"reference/http.html","d":1,"x":"Every member serves the same API on one port, over HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 (cleartext h2c, or h2 over TLS). Request bodies are JSON with Content-Type: application/json ; answers are JSON unless noted. Every exchange on this page was recorded against a release node.","k":"Content-Type: application/json"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Endpoints","u":"reference/http.html#overview","d":0,"x":"Endpoint Credential Purpose POST /v1/query none¹ Run a query. POST /v1/mutate none¹ Run a mutation. POST /v1/transaction none¹ Run mutations in several databases atomically. POST /v1/watch none¹ Watch a query (server-sent events). GET /v1/retry none The database's retry identity, for scoped request IDs. POST /v1/retry/session none¹ Open, inspect, acknowledge and close retry sessions. GET /v1/status , /health/live , /health/ready none Raft status, liveness, readiness. POST /v1/queue/… worker Claim, renew and report queue jobs. GET /v1/timer/… admin Inspect and maintain timers. POST /v1/deploy , /v1/admin/… , /v1/metrics admin Operator endpoints. GET /v1/sync sync Log sync for device replicas ","k":"/v1/query /v1/mutate /v1/transaction /v1/watch /v1/retry /v1/retry/session /v1/status /health/live /health/ready /v1/queue/… /v1/timer/… /v1/deploy /v1/admin/… /v1/metrics /v1/sync /admin #[query(http)] Method::http() Transaction::http() COMPONENT.http() external!(…, http) 404 UNKNOWN_METHOD"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Credentials","u":"reference/http.html#credentials","d":0,"x":"Versioned machine-readable contracts are available as JSON Schema 2020-12 , with valid and invalid golden fixtures . Select the appropriate named $defs schema for tokens, errors, discovery, watch frames and sync batches. The root validates call envelopes. Runtime checks additionally enforce causal, history and sync-coverage relationships. Credentials travel as Authorization: Bearer <token> (exactly that capitalization). The server compares the token with its own three tokens: The admin token unlocks operator endpoints and every worker and sync endpoint. On call endpoints it makes the caller a trusted gateway : the body may then carry principal ( {\"subject\", \"tenant\"?, \"claims\"?} ) and timest","k":"$defs Authorization: Bearer <token> principal {\"subject\", \"tenant\"?, \"claims\"?} timestamp_ms /v1/queue/… /v1/sync {\"bearer\": \"<token>\"} credentials"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Request headers","u":"reference/http.html#request-headers","d":0,"x":"Header Applies to Meaning Authorization all Bearer <token> ; see above. Content-Type JSON bodies Must be application/json : 415 unsupported_media_type otherwise. x-flower-timeout-ms all The caller's deadline for the whole request, in whole milliseconds (capped at one day). It can only shorten a call's own deadline: a call cut off by it answers 503 caller_deadline_exceeded , requests still waiting for admission at it are refused with 503 overloaded , and a write still queued past it never runs. Followers forward what is left of it. For a watch it bounds the first snapshot only. Malformed: 400 bad_request . x-flower-max-staleness-ms /v1/query Tightens a replica-local query's staleness bound (0","k":"Authorization Bearer <token> Content-Type application/json 415 unsupported_media_type x-flower-timeout-ms 503 caller_deadline_exceeded 503 overloaded 400 bad_request x-flower-max-staleness-ms /v1/query 0 x-flower-group ?group= 404 unknown_group traceparent idempotency_key Accept"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Response headers","u":"reference/http.html#response-headers","d":0,"x":"Header When Meaning x-flower-leader-url calls, watches, queue, timer and admin answers, when the member knows the leader The leader's --advertise-http URL — for a placed database, the owning group's leader. x-flower-staleness-ms replica-local query answers Estimated staleness in ms, 0 after a proven-fresh read, or unknown . x-flower-group every answer The group that served the request ( root for unplaced databases). x-flower-partition-epoch calls on placed databases The partition's ownership epoch. Server-Timing JSON answers of query, mutate and transaction, failures included See below. Retry-After 503 overloaded , partition_moving , partition_moved , 410 partition_retired Seconds to wait be","k":"x-flower-leader-url --advertise-http x-flower-staleness-ms 0 unknown x-flower-group root x-flower-partition-epoch Server-Timing Retry-After 503 overloaded partition_moving partition_moved 410 partition_retired retry_after_ms"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Server-Timing","u":"reference/http.html#server-timing","d":0,"x":"Durations in milliseconds, each present only when its phase ran, in this order: Metric Phase forward A follower's round trip to the leader. Phases after it are the leader's. cache;desc=hit|miss Query result cache lookup. fence Proving a read fresh (lease check, quorum round or leader fence). join Waiting for an identical query already in flight. admission Waiting for an evaluation slot. queue A write's wait in the leader's writer queue. eval Running the guest: the query, or preparing the write. commit , append , replicate Raft commit; local append and replication to a quorum overlap inside it. apply Applying the committed entry. total The whole request on the answering member. node;desc=<id>","k":"forward cache;desc=hit|miss fence join admission queue eval commit append replicate apply total node;desc=<id> leader;desc=<id> snapshot;desc=building server-timing: node desc leader"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Version and capabilities","u":"reference/http.html#protocol","d":0,"x":"GET /v1/protocol returns {\"version\":1,\"capabilities\":[\"read_tokens\",\"scoped_commands\",\"watch_history\",\"sync_since\"]} . Responses report x-flower-protocol-version . Optional capabilities are checked explicitly; unknown required versions are refused. Queries accept x-flower-at-least , an opaque scope-qualified read token. Successful queries report x-flower-read-token and x-flower-consistency ; successful mutation envelopes may include read_token . Wrong-history read tokens answer 409 and malformed tokens 400. Token counters are canonical decimal strings. Never compare a database revision with a group log position. Error bodies and terminal watch errors include a problem extension with status ,","k":"GET /v1/protocol x-flower-protocol-version x-flower-at-least x-flower-read-token x-flower-consistency read_token problem status code message details? origin execution retryable retry_after_ms? history version since"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"POST /v1/query","u":"reference/http.html#query","d":0,"x":"Modules can expose request-time SQL through a normal query backed by SqlEndpoint . Its arguments typically contain statement and params ; the module fixes the allowed collections, budgets and access rules. This uses the same query envelope and authentication as other methods. There is no generic /v1/sql route. See SQL queries . Field Type db string Database. module string Module. name string The query's HTTP name. args any Required; the method's arguments object ( {} for none, or the bare value of a bare_arg method). credentials any Optional; for the authorization component. principal , timestamp_ms Trusted gateway only. Success is 200 {\"ok\":true,\"value\":<result>} , with no revision. A failu","k":"SqlEndpoint statement params /v1/sql db module name args {} bare_arg credentials principal timestamp_ms 200 {\"ok\":true,\"value\":<result>} {\"ok\":false,\"code\",\"message\",\"details\"?} 422 INVALID_INVOCATION http 404 UNKNOWN_METHOD true value done false title milk code TASK_NOT_FOUND details nope message task this INVALID_ARGUMENT path arguments invalid expected string"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"POST /v1/mutate","u":"reference/http.html#mutate","d":0,"x":"The query fields, plus: Field Type idempotency_key string The request ID. A retry with the same key answers the recorded outcome. f1: / f2: keys are scoped IDs and must be well-formed (400 otherwise). See Retries . expected_revision integer Fail with stale_revision if the database has moved past it. Every outcome the mutation records answers with revision , position and duplicate : success with 200, and every failure — the method's own codes, INVALID_ARGUMENT , receipt refusals, access-policy refusals, stale_revision , a guest-side refusal such as queue_undeclared — with 422 , the message and any details . Nothing the failed call wrote was kept; duplicate is true when a stored receipt answer","k":"idempotency_key f1: f2: expected_revision stale_revision revision position duplicate INVALID_ARGUMENT queue_undeclared details --max-transaction-bytes 413 transaction_too_large 422 INVALID_INVOCATION true value done false title milk code TASK_NOT_FOUND message task this nope expected database"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"POST /v1/transaction","u":"reference/http.html#transaction","d":0,"x":"At least one step, each on a different database ( 422 INVALID_VALUE otherwise); method is a mutation's HTTP name. Also takes credentials and the gateway fields. The steps share one deadline (60 s, shortened by x-flower-timeout-ms ), and their committed effects are bounded by --max-transaction-bytes ( 413 transaction_too_large ). The answer is the mutation shape with value as an array of step results, plus revisions (per database) and, across groups, positions (per group): A failing step answers 422 with that step's failure; nothing committed. See Transactions .","k":"422 INVALID_VALUE method credentials x-flower-timeout-ms --max-transaction-bytes 413 transaction_too_large value revisions positions steps demo module tasks complete_task args milk archive add_task title idempotency_key archive-milk true done false revision position duplicate code TASK_NOT_FOUND message task this details"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"POST /v1/watch","u":"reference/http.html#watch","d":0,"x":"The body is exactly a query's — db , module , name , args , credentials ?, principal ? (trusted gateway) — plus \"deltas\": true to accept patch events: A failure before the first snapshot answers an ordinary JSON error, with the query's status ( 422 for its own failure). Then text/event-stream : snapshot carries a whole value; it is always first. patch (with deltas ) applies RFC 6902 add / remove / replace operations to the value whose id is base , whenever the patch is smaller than the value. error ends the stream: unauthorized , watch_scope_changed , shutdown , forward , overloaded , the query's own failure (with its details , if any), UNKNOWN_METHOD after a redeploy, partition_moving , and","k":"db module name args credentials principal \"deltas\": true patch 422 text/event-stream snapshot deltas add remove replace id base error unauthorized watch_scope_changed shutdown forward overloaded details UNKNOWN_METHOD partition_moving : watching id: 503 overloaded curl http: watch content-type: application json demo tasks open_tasks true event: data: revision value bread title Bake path eggs code message watching"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"GET /v1/retry, POST /v1/retry/session","u":"reference/http.html#retry","d":0,"x":"GET /v1/retry?db=D answers the database's retry identity — incarnation , epoch , min_epoch , epoch_ms , keep_epochs , initialized and counters — from which clients mint f1: and f2: request IDs. POST /v1/retry/session takes {\"db\",\"module\",\"session\":\"<32 hex>\",\"operation\":\"open\"|\"status\"|\"ack\"|\"close\",\"through\"?,\"abandon\"?,\"limit\"?,\"credentials\"?} and answers {\"ok\":true,\"session\":{session,incarnation,epoch,acknowledged,closed}} . The module's authorization component decides the session's owner under its sessions access.","k":"GET /v1/retry?db=D incarnation epoch min_epoch epoch_ms keep_epochs initialized f1: f2: POST /v1/retry/session sessions"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Status and health","u":"reference/http.html#health","d":0,"x":"/health/ready answers 503 with a reason ( no known leader , applying committed entries , rejoining: … , a storage failure) when the member should not take traffic; ?group= asks about one group.","k":"/health/ready reason no known leader applying committed entries rejoining: … ?group= status state Leader leader term last_log last_applied health live true ready applied committed null rejoining false storage"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Queues","u":"reference/http.html#queues","d":0,"x":"Worker or admin token. Every answer is 200 : {\"ok\":true,…} , or {\"ok\":false,\"code\",\"message\"} for refusals such as a lease no longer held, or queue_purging while a removed module's queue is being deleted (retry once the purge ends). Native workers may name any queue; guests use only the queues their module declares. Endpoint Body or parameters Answer POST /v1/queue/poll {db, queue, limit?=10, lease_ms?=30000, scope?=\"\", owner?, wait_ms?} items : claims with id , token , payload , attempts , fence , scope , priority , owner , name , group , expires_ms , available_ms , claimed_ms POST /v1/queue/heartbeat {db, queue, id, token, lease_ms?} renews the lease POST /v1/queue/complete {db, queue, id,","k":"200 {\"ok\":true,…} {\"ok\":false,\"code\",\"message\"} queue_purging /v1/queue/poll items id token payload attempts fence scope priority owner name group expires_ms available_ms claimed_ms /v1/queue/heartbeat {db, queue, id, token, lease_ms?} /v1/queue/complete {db, queue, id, token, result?, next?} result next /v1/queue/fail {db, queue, id, token, delay_ms?, next?} /v1/queue/kill {db, queue, id, token, next?} /v1/queue/release {db, queue, id, token, delay_ms?} /v1/queue/cancel /retry /purge {db, queue, id} {db, queue, id, delay_ms?} {db, queue, older_than_ms?} /v1/queue/stats /ready /item ?db&queue&scope&owner pending claimed dead completed ready next_ms {limit?, lease_ms?, scope?, owner?, wait_ms?}"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Timers","u":"reference/http.html#timers","d":0,"x":"Admin token. GET /v1/timer/stats?db ( pending , dead , held ), GET /v1/timer/get?db&id , GET /v1/timer/list?db&state=pending|dead|held|all&limit ; POST /v1/timer/cancel {db, id} , /retry {db, id, delay_ms?} , /purge {db, older_than_ms?} . A held timer came due while its module was removed. Timers fire from the leader's maintenance loop, not from an endpoint.","k":"GET /v1/timer/stats?db pending dead held GET /v1/timer/get?db&id GET /v1/timer/list?db&state=pending|dead|held|all&limit POST /v1/timer/cancel {db, id} /retry {db, id, delay_ms?} /purge {db, older_than_ms?}"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Operator endpoints","u":"reference/http.html#admin","d":0,"x":"Admin token. The cargo flower commands wrap them. Endpoint Purpose CLI POST /v1/deploy {db, module, wasm_b64, manifest, identity?} : deploy a module. A module or manifest that fails validation answers 500 invalid_module or invalid_manifest . deploy /v1/admin/deployments {\"operation\": \"stage\"|\"advance\"|\"status\"|\"activate\"|\"cancel\"|\"collect\", db, module, …} deployment /v1/admin/modules List modules; {\"operation\":\"remove\", db, module, purge?} . modules /v1/admin/membership View; {\"members\": […], expected_log_id?, retain?} to change. membership /v1/admin/leadership {\"to\": id} . leadership /v1/admin/backup Continuous backup status; POST takes a full backup ( 503 unavailable without backups config","k":"cargo flower /v1/deploy {db, module, wasm_b64, manifest, identity?} 500 invalid_module invalid_manifest deploy /v1/admin/deployments deployment /v1/admin/modules {\"operation\":\"remove\", db, module, purge?} modules /v1/admin/membership {\"members\": […], expected_log_id?, retain?} membership /v1/admin/leadership {\"to\": id} leadership /v1/admin/backup 503 unavailable backup /v1/admin/keys ?db= {db, operation, requestId?, …} KEY_REQUEST KEYS_LOCKED keys /v1/admin/retention rotate advance collect budget retention /v1/admin/groups /partitions /transactions group partition transactions /v1/metrics /v1/admin/raft /v1/admin/activity metrics raft activity /v1/admin/stream /v1/admin/activity/stream --deploy-max-bytes"},{"t":"HTTP API","h":"Errors","u":"reference/http.html#errors","d":0,"x":"Every error answers JSON: {\"ok\":false,\"code\":…,\"message\":…} , with details when the call's own failure carries any, revision / position / duplicate for a recorded write, retry_after_ms for retryable refusals and leader for forward . The status tells a client what to do; only 422 is the call's own answer . Status Codes Retry? 400 bad_request : a body that is not the endpoint's JSON, a malformed header or request ID No 403 unauthorized (the authorization component), ACCESS_DENIED (a query's access rule), forbidden (server token) No 404 UNKNOWN_METHOD , unknown_group No 409 MODULE_CONFLICT , deployment , deployment_in_progress , membership , transfer_failed No 410 partition_retired No 413 trans","k":"{\"ok\":false,\"code\":…,\"message\":…} details revision position duplicate retry_after_ms leader forward bad_request unauthorized ACCESS_DENIED forbidden UNKNOWN_METHOD unknown_group MODULE_CONFLICT deployment deployment_in_progress membership transfer_failed partition_retired transaction_too_large payload_too_large unsupported_media_type UPPER_SNAKE_CASE INVALID_ARGUMENT INVALID_INVOCATION SCHEMA_MISMATCH MODULE_PRIVATE deadline_exceeded budget_exhausted out_of_memory stack_overflow nesting_too_deep init_failed host_trap stale_revision invalid_module invalid_manifest fatal overloaded caller_deadline_exceeded quorum stale_replica recovering rejoining storage_failed host_io leadership_transfer leader_isolated forward_failed instances_exhausted instantiation_failed module_unavailable partition_moving partition_moved unavailable"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"cargo flower","u":"reference/cli.html","d":1,"x":"One binary, cargo-flower , builds and inspects guests, deploys and calls them, operates clusters, and serves cluster nodes. Run it as cargo flower <command> or cargo-flower <command> .","k":"cargo-flower cargo flower <command> cargo-flower <command>"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Conventions","u":"reference/cli.html#conventions","d":0,"x":"Install with cargo install --path crates/cargo-flower from a Flower checkout. --help works on every command and subcommand. Commands that talk to a server take --endpoint URL ( http:// or https:// ), read from FLOWER_ENDPOINT when absent, and default to http://127.0.0.1:8080 . HTTPS trusts the platform's roots. ( backup is the exception: it talks to a server only when given an endpoint.) Credentials: operator commands take --admin-token or FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN ; call , transaction and retry take --token or FLOWER_TOKEN (an end user's token for the module's authorization component, or the admin token as a trusted gateway); queue takes --token or FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN . Flags of a command come bef","k":"cargo install --path crates/cargo-flower --help --endpoint URL http:// https:// FLOWER_ENDPOINT http://127.0.0.1:8080 backup --admin-token FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN call transaction retry --token FLOWER_TOKEN queue FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN cargo flower queue --db app stats --queue mail queue stats --db app … key=value cargo-flower: <message> export https: flower.example.com cargo flower status"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"build","u":"reference/cli.html#build","d":0,"x":"Builds the workspace's cdylib (exactly one, or the one --package names) for wasm32-unknown-unknown , inspects it through the server's Wasm backend, and writes <package>.wasm and <package>.manifest.json . Prints built <name> wasm=… manifest=… sha256=… and counts of collections, queries, mutations, derived values, transactions, triggers, HTTP names, SQL plans and SQL endpoints.","k":"cdylib --package wasm32-unknown-unknown <package>.wasm <package>.manifest.json built <name> wasm=… manifest=… sha256=… cargo flower build package manifest-path Cargo.toml profile release features no-default-features locked out-dir"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"dev","u":"reference/cli.html#dev","d":0,"x":"Watches Rust sources, Cargo manifests and lockfiles beneath the selected Cargo workspace directory, builds with the dev profile and deploys successful builds to a running server. It uses the ordinary deployment preflight and FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN . --once performs one build/deploy cycle and returns its status. Otherwise failures wait for a source edit; Ctrl-C stops the watcher. Symlinks and directories named target , .git and .flower are excluded.","k":"FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN --once target .git .flower cargo flower package module watch endpoint once"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"plan","u":"reference/cli.html#plan","d":0,"x":"Preflights a deployment offline: validates the module and manifest, applies bindings, prints the content identity , diffs against --current (a manifest or Wasm), and with --with runs the cross-module link check. Exits 1 on an incompatible change.","k":"identity --current --with cargo flower plan wasm manifest current DEPLOYED module NAME with MODULE FILE bind SLOT bindings"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"contract","u":"reference/cli.html#contract","d":0,"x":"inspect prints collections, indexes, references, definitions and triggers. diff marks lines + added, - removed, ~ changed and ! incompatible; it always exits 0. Inspection and diff also include named SQL plans and scoped endpoints. typescript generates deterministic Queries and Mutations wire-type maps for public aliases, including argument, result and error contracts. Decimal integers remain strings; optional and nullable fields remain distinct. These static types require server validation for bounds, patterns and authorization.","k":"inspect diff + - ~ ! typescript Queries Mutations cargo flower contract manifest from contracts.ts"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"sql-explain","u":"reference/cli.html#sql-explain","d":0,"x":"Explains a named SQL plan offline, including its access path and budgets. For dynamic SQL, call the module's explain query. See SQL queries .","k":"cargo flower sql-explain --manifest M --plan PLAN cargo flower sql-explain manifest plan PLAN"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"deploy","u":"reference/cli.html#deploy","d":0,"x":"Runs the plan preflight, then deploys through consensus. Prints revision=… position=… . A slot the module imports ( $inventory ) must be bound, or nothing is sent.","k":"plan revision=… position=… $inventory cargo flower deploy module wasm manifest identity bind SLOT MODULE bindings FILE endpoint admin-token"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"deployment","u":"reference/cli.html#deployment","d":0,"x":"Subcommand Flags stage --wasm , --manifest (both required), --identity , --bind , --bindings Stage without activating. advance --max-records N (server default 4096) Build more backfill, then derived roots. status Print the staging. activate Activate a ready staging. cancel Abandon it. collect --max-keys N (server default 4096) Delete structures retired deployments left.","k":"stage --wasm --manifest --identity --bind --bindings advance --max-records N status activate cancel collect --max-keys N cargo flower deployment module endpoint admin-token"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"modules","u":"reference/cli.html#modules","d":0,"x":"list prints one line per module: its identity, definition count, collections, HTTP names ( http= ), whether it has an authorization component and any staging. remove takes the module's code, HTTP names, hooks and derived values away and keeps its data; --purge deletes the data too, a page at a time, and the module cannot deploy again until the purge ends.","k":"list http= remove --purge cargo flower modules module purge"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"call","u":"reference/cli.html#call","d":0,"x":"--args-file - reads JSON from standard input. File input and inline --args are mutually exclusive. --contract checks the public alias, requested operation kind and structural argument schema before delivery. Host-only validations and authorization remain server checks. --name is the method's HTTP name: only methods that declare http answer. Add --mutation for a mutation. Without --contract , a kind mismatch is rejected by the server; with a contract, it is rejected locally. Prints the result as JSON; a mutation also prints revision=… position=… duplicate=… . --request-id makes a retry answer the original outcome while the server retains that receipt.","k":"--args-file - --args --contract --name http --mutation revision=… position=… duplicate=… --request-id cargo flower call module name NAME args JSON args-file FILE contract MANIFEST mutation request-id token"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"transaction","u":"reference/cli.html#transaction","d":0,"x":"","k":"cargo flower transaction steps module method args request-id token"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"retry","u":"reference/cli.html#retry","d":0,"x":"","k":"cargo flower retry token identity JSON intent order:42 request open module session session-id sequence through abandon limit status close"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Queues and timers","u":"reference/cli.html#queues-and-timers","d":0,"x":"queue Flags stats --scope peek --id poll --limit , --lease-ms , --scope , --owner , --wait-ms (needs --owner ) heartbeat , complete , fail , kill , release --id , --token (the claim token), and --lease-ms (heartbeat) or --delay-ms (fail, release) cancel , retry , purge --id ; --id and --delay-ms ; --older-than-ms Timer subcommands: stats , get --id , list [--state pending|dead|held|all] [--limit] , cancel --id , retry --id [--delay-ms] , purge [--older-than-ms] .","k":"stats --scope peek --id poll --limit --lease-ms --owner --wait-ms heartbeat complete fail kill release --token --delay-ms cancel retry purge --older-than-ms get --id list [--state pending|dead|held|all] [--limit] cancel --id retry --id [--delay-ms] purge [--older-than-ms] cargo flower queue token subcommand timer admin-token"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Operating","u":"reference/cli.html#operating","d":0,"x":"Command What it does status Raft state of one member. membership [--members ID=RAFTADDR]… [--expected-log-id T-N-I] [--retain] View, or change to the complete desired voter set. leadership --to ID Hand leadership to another voter (address the leader). keys [--db D] [--request-id ID] <subcommand> A database's managed-key catalog; see below . retention --db D [rotate|advance|collect|budget] Retry retention: epoch length and count, receipt collection and byte budget. group , partition , transactions Groups, partition placement and moves, cross-group transactions (see Operating a cluster ). backup --endpoint URL [status] Take a full backup through a member now, or print its backup status. backup","k":"status leadership --to ID keys [--db D] [--request-id ID] <subcommand> retention --db D [rotate|advance|collect|budget] group partition transactions backup --endpoint URL [status] backup list backup restore metrics raft activity"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"keys","u":"reference/cli.html#keys","d":0,"x":"Every database keeps its own replicated key catalog. Without a subcommand (or with list ), keys prints every database's catalog, or one with --db ; changes need --db (before or after the subcommand, as --request-id ) and run at the leader of the database's group under a request ID (printed when you pass none; pass --request-id to retry one safely). Subcommand Flags generate --name , --algorithm ( Ed25519 , P256 , RSA , HS256 , A256GCM , XSalsa20Poly1305 , X25519 ), --bits (RSA) seal --key-file , --format raw|pem|der , --wrapping-file , optional --out . Works offline without a database, endpoint or token. Writes a new file or prints the sealed envelope. import --name , --algorithm , and eithe","k":"list keys --db --request-id generate --name --algorithm Ed25519 P256 RSA HS256 A256GCM XSalsa20Poly1305 X25519 --bits seal --key-file --format raw|pem|der --wrapping-file --out import --sealed-file bind unbind --alias --key --usages sign,verify,publicKey,encrypt,decrypt,derive rotate retire revoke destroy --version rewrap cache --key-wrapping-file KEYS_LOCKED cargo flower shop name tickets algorithm alias usages sign verify publicKey"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"backup list and restore","u":"reference/cli.html#backup","d":0,"x":"Both run offline, against --backup-url ( s3://… or file:///… , or FLOWER_BACKUP_URL ) or --backup-dir , configured as for serve . Without a target, restore restores the newest state the backups hold (the latest base, then the archive after it); --manifest restores one backup as it was taken. restore replaces a stopped node's group file and removes its Raft log; start the node with --bootstrap afterwards. For an existing cluster, stop every member and restore the same target on each. See Backups .","k":"--backup-url s3://… file:///… FLOWER_BACKUP_URL --backup-dir serve restore --manifest --bootstrap cargo flower backup list backup-url BUCKET PREFIX keystore kek-file data at-ms index history manifest NAME"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"serve","u":"reference/cli.html#serve","d":0,"x":"Serves a cluster node: Raft plus the HTTP API. Only --dir and --id are required. Most flags also read an environment variable, shown in the tables; restarting with the same flags is always safe. Upgrades across a compatibility contract A member's files record the storage contract they were written under (how entries apply, how snapshots and values encode). A build whose contract differs refuses to open them at start ( incompatible_store ), naming both, and refuses peers of another contract. There is no in-place migration: across a contract change, take a backup, then restore it into a fresh directory under the new build. Builds that keep the contract roll one member at a time.","k":"--dir --id incompatible_store cargo flower serve data bootstrap admin-token secret"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Node, listeners and TLS","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-node","d":0,"x":"Flag Default Meaning --dir required Data directory (created if missing). --id required Member ID. --http 127.0.0.1:8080 API address; port 0 picks one and prints it ( listening http=… ). --raft 127.0.0.1:9000 Raft address. --bootstrap ID[=RAFTADDR] Found a new cluster with these members (repeatable; ignored once initialized). --advertise-http · FLOWER_ADVERTISE_HTTP listen address URL clients reach this member at. --listen-fd · FLOWER_LISTEN_FD Serve on a socket inherited from a supervisor. --tls-cert , --tls-key · FLOWER_TLS_CERT_FILE , FLOWER_TLS_KEY_FILE API TLS (PEM). --tls-handshake-timeout-ms 10000 TLS handshake deadline. --http2-max-streams hyper's Concurrent HTTP/2 streams per connect","k":"--dir --id --http 127.0.0.1:8080 listening http=… --raft 127.0.0.1:9000 --bootstrap ID[=RAFTADDR] --advertise-http FLOWER_ADVERTISE_HTTP --listen-fd FLOWER_LISTEN_FD --tls-cert --tls-key FLOWER_TLS_CERT_FILE FLOWER_TLS_KEY_FILE --tls-handshake-timeout-ms --http2-max-streams --admin-ui FLOWER_ADMIN_UI true /admin --shutdown-timeout-ms"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Credentials","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-credentials","d":0,"x":"Flag Environment Meaning --admin-token FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN Operator endpoints, and trusted gateways that may assert principals and timestamps. --worker-token FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN Queue endpoints. --sync-token FLOWER_SYNC_TOKEN Replica sync. --peer-token FLOWER_PEER_TOKEN Raft between members; defaults to the admin token; required off loopback. --raft-tls-cert , --raft-tls-key , --raft-tls-ca FLOWER_RAFT_TLS_*_FILE TLS between members (all three, on every member).","k":"--admin-token FLOWER_ADMIN_TOKEN --worker-token FLOWER_WORKER_TOKEN --sync-token FLOWER_SYNC_TOKEN --peer-token FLOWER_PEER_TOKEN --raft-tls-cert --raft-tls-key --raft-tls-ca FLOWER_RAFT_TLS_*_FILE"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Guest limits and caches","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-guests","d":0,"x":"Flag Default Meaning --guest-timeout-ms 5000 Deadline of a call whose method declares no timeout , nested calls included. Wall-clock only: the instruction budget follows the declaration, the same on every member. --guest-max-timeout-ms 60000 The longest deadline a method may declare; longer declarations run to this. --guest-memory-bytes 128 MiB Linear memory per instance. --guest-invocation-memory-bytes 1 GiB Memory across an invocation's nested instances. --guest-table-elements 65536 Table elements per instance. --max-transaction-bytes 32 MiB Largest encoded effects one mutation or transaction may commit; larger ones fail transaction_too_large (413) before anything is proposed. Four times i","k":"--guest-timeout-ms timeout --guest-max-timeout-ms --guest-memory-bytes --guest-invocation-memory-bytes --guest-table-elements --max-transaction-bytes transaction_too_large --raft-max-frame-bytes --deploy-max-bytes --wasm-pool-slots --wasm-keep-resident-bytes --image-cache-bytes --code-cache-bytes --query-cache-bytes --query-flight-bytes --decision-memo-bytes FLOWER_ FLOWER_GUEST_TIMEOUT_MS FLOWER_MAX_TRANSACTION_BYTES"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Admission and writer","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-admission","d":0,"x":"Flag Default Meaning --query-workers CPU count Concurrent query and watch evaluations. --control-workers 1 Concurrent deployments. --queued-input-bytes 64 MiB Request bytes queued or running. --admission-max-wait-ms 10000 Longest admission wait. --admission-target-ms , --admission-interval-ms 50, 500 Early shedding of requests with deadlines. --admission-refresh-share-percent 25 Watch refreshes' minimum share while queries wait. --evaluation-memory-bytes 256 MiB Memory reserved per running evaluation. --writer-preparation-workers min(CPUs, 8) Threads preparing mutations speculatively. --writer-conflict-prediction 1 Run methods whose speculation keeps conflicting in turn.","k":"--query-workers --control-workers --queued-input-bytes --admission-max-wait-ms --admission-target-ms --admission-interval-ms --admission-refresh-share-percent --evaluation-memory-bytes --writer-preparation-workers --writer-conflict-prediction"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Raft timing and transport","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-raft","d":0,"x":"Flag Default Meaning --raft-rtt-ms 0 Round trip between the farthest members; the rest derive from it. --raft-heartbeat-ms 50 Heartbeat interval. --raft-leader-lease-ms 300 Leader lease. --raft-election-min-ms , --raft-election-max-ms 100, 200 Election timeout range, past the lease. --raft-read-lease-ms 270 Read lease; 0 makes every linearizable read take a quorum round. --raft-rpc-timeout-ms 500 Append and quorum-read deadline. --raft-max-frame-bytes 256 MiB Largest Raft frame. A member whose frames cannot carry a peer's largest entry refuses that peer's dial. --raft-max-connections 512 Inbound peer connections.","k":"--raft-rtt-ms --raft-heartbeat-ms --raft-leader-lease-ms --raft-election-min-ms --raft-election-max-ms --raft-read-lease-ms --raft-rpc-timeout-ms --raft-max-frame-bytes --raft-max-connections"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Storage, snapshots and compaction","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-storage","d":0,"x":"Flag Default Meaning --fsync on off survives process crashes, not power loss. --leader-flush lazy With ≥3 voters, the leader defers its own flush while followers form a majority. --apply-flush-ms 1000 How often applied state is flushed; 0 = every entry. --raft-snapshot-logs , --raft-keep-logs 10000, 10000 Snapshot every N entries; entries kept after a snapshot. --snapshot-after-bytes , --snapshot-max-age-ms 16 MiB, 300000 Extra snapshot triggers. --compact-ratio 2 Compact a data file online at this waste ratio; 0 = never.","k":"--fsync on off --leader-flush lazy --apply-flush-ms --raft-snapshot-logs --raft-keep-logs --snapshot-after-bytes --snapshot-max-age-ms --compact-ratio"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Managed keys","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-keys","d":0,"x":"Flag Meaning --key-wrapping-file · FLOWER_KEY_WRAPPING_FILE ( FLOWER_KEYRING_FILE , its earlier name, is still read) 32 raw bytes of the wrapping key that seals every database's key catalog; every member of every group mounts the same one. Without it the member applies catalogs but serves no catalog key: guests still hash, draw random bytes and verify with keys they hold, but managed-key operations answer KEYS_LOCKED . --key-wrapping-previous Earlier wrapping keys, during rotation (until keys rewrap ). Repeatable. --key-cache-bytes , --key-cache-ttl-ms Prepared-key cache budget (16 MiB) and lifetime (0: never expires).","k":"--key-wrapping-file FLOWER_KEY_WRAPPING_FILE FLOWER_KEYRING_FILE KEYS_LOCKED --key-wrapping-previous keys rewrap --key-cache-bytes --key-cache-ttl-ms"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Backup","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-backup","d":0,"x":"Flag Default Meaning --backup-url · FLOWER_BACKUP_URL Where backups go: s3://BUCKET/PREFIX or file:///PATH . Turns on continuous backup; give every member the same one. --backup-dir · FLOWER_BACKUP_DIR A local directory instead of --backup-url . --keystore , --kek-file · FLOWER_BACKUP_KEYSTORE , FLOWER_BACKUP_KEK_FILE The backup keystore and its 32-byte key-encryption key; required with either destination. --backup-retention-ms 30 days How far back restores reach: every point of this window stays restorable, and older bases thin to one per --backup-base-ms . --backup-base-ms , --backup-base-bytes 24 h, 64 MiB When the leader takes a new base: once the newest is this old, or the archive since","k":"--backup-url FLOWER_BACKUP_URL s3://BUCKET/PREFIX file:///PATH --backup-dir FLOWER_BACKUP_DIR --keystore --kek-file FLOWER_BACKUP_KEYSTORE FLOWER_BACKUP_KEK_FILE --backup-retention-ms --backup-base-ms --backup-base-bytes --backup-archive-ms --backup-part-bytes --backup-retention-interval-ms --backup-hold-max-entries --backup-hold-max-bytes path virtual FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_REGION FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ADDRESSING FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY FLOWER_BACKUP_S3_SESSION_TOKEN AWS_"},{"t":"cargo flower","h":"Maintenance","u":"reference/cli.html#serve-maintenance","d":0,"x":"Flag Meaning --timer-sweep-ms Maintenance tick for timers, tasks and receipt collection (1000). --retry-window-ms · FLOWER_RETRY_WINDOW_MS Default retry window for databases (24 h; 0 = none). --partition-freeze-max-ms Longest a partition move may freeze its partition (30000). --leader-placement on|off Reconcile preferred group leaders (on); FLOWER_LEADER_PLACEMENT . OpenTelemetry is configured by environment only: FLOWER_OTEL_ENABLED=1 and the standard OTEL_* variables. Logging follows RUST_LOG .","k":"--timer-sweep-ms --retry-window-ms FLOWER_RETRY_WINDOW_MS --partition-freeze-max-ms --leader-placement on|off FLOWER_LEADER_PLACEMENT FLOWER_OTEL_ENABLED=1 OTEL_* RUST_LOG"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"flower-sdk","u":"reference/sdk.html","d":1,"x":"The guest SDK: no_std plus alloc , no runtime dependencies. This page lists its public surface by module; the concept pages explain how the pieces fit.","k":"no_std alloc"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"Crate setup","u":"reference/sdk.html#crate-setup","d":0,"x":"A guest is a cdylib built for wasm32-unknown-unknown ; add \"rlib\" so tests and Rust clients can link its types and descriptors ( Testing , typed calls ). The derive and attribute macros generate ::flower_sdk and ::alloc paths: put extern crate alloc; at the crate root. Guests may use std types that only allocate; operating-system APIs (files, sockets, threads, clocks) are not available. End the crate with flower_sdk::export!(APP); .","k":"cdylib wasm32-unknown-unknown \"rlib\" ::flower_sdk ::alloc extern crate alloc; std flower_sdk::export!(APP);"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"Crate root","u":"reference/sdk.html#crate-root","d":0,"x":"Item Kind App struct App::new(&[&component, …]) : the module, one flat list of components; .init(fn() -> Result<()>) . See The App assembly . Component trait Anything an app lists: methods, derived values, transactions, collections, triggers, tasks, retention hooks, queues, key declarations, the authorization component, imports and composites (an interface provider, an external value). Every accessor defaults to nothing; a static array of components is one component. Op<A, T, E = Failure> struct A method with its types, as #[query] and #[mutation] declare it: argument struct A , result T , declared errors E . Dereferences to its Method ; .named(name) and .consistency(c) make twins. Timers an","k":"App App::new(&[&component, …]) .init(fn() -> Result<()>) Component Op<A, T, E = Failure> #[query] #[mutation] A T E Method .named(name) .consistency(c) Method::query(name, cb) Method::mutation(name, cb) fn(&mut Ctx, Value) -> Result<Value> .args .result .errors .consistency .named .http() .access(Access) .export() .export_to(&[\"module\"]) .timeout_ms(ms) MethodKind Export Query Mutation Private All Only(&[…]) Consistency Linearizable ReplicaLocal ReplicaLocalWithin(ms) Derived Aggregation Derived::new(name, cb) .materialize(…) .access(fn() -> Rule) .aggregate(Aggregation) Materialize Never Always Each(&Collection) Transaction Plan Call Target Committed PlannerFn Transaction::new(name, planner) Definition Method(&Method) Derived(&Derived) Transaction(&Transaction) Collection IndexDef Reference Place OnDelete ReferenceTargets Collection::new(name) .key_schema .value_schema .indexes .references .access(fn() -> Policy) Import ImportedCollection ImportedDerived ImportedMethod Contract $slot Trigger Change Task TaskFailure Retention DeletedRow features Value Null Bool Number(f64) String Array Object(BTreeMap) canonical_json() Failure Result<T> fail fail_with Failure { code, message, details } Principal { subject, tenant, claims } FlowerType BusinessError CallOutcome CallError CollectionRef RangeBounds RawPage RawRow HistoryEntry"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"Macros","u":"reference/sdk.html#macros","d":0,"x":"Macro Generates #[derive(FlowerType)] Schema and codec. Named structs (closed objects), unit enums (string enums), single-payload enums ( {\"tag\",\"value\"} ). Container option #[flower(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] ; field options #[flower(default)] , #[flower(default = path)] , #[flower(skip)] . Two fields with one wire name do not compile; r#type is \"type\" . #[derive(FlowerError)] BusinessError for an enum of unit or single-payload variants, each #[flower(code = \"UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\")] with optional message = \"…\" (default: the variant's name) and retryable ; adds is_retryable() . One #[flower(other)] Host(Failure) variant takes every other failure and generates From<Failure> , so ? works. #[collec","k":"#[derive(FlowerType)] {\"tag\",\"value\"} #[flower(rename_all = \"camelCase\")] #[flower(default)] #[flower(default = path)] #[flower(skip)] r#type \"type\" #[derive(FlowerError)] BusinessError #[flower(code = \"UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\")] message = \"…\" retryable is_retryable() #[flower(other)] Host(Failure) From<Failure> ? NAME SOURCE get(ctx, &key) set(ctx, &key, &value) delete(ctx, &key) page(ctx, limit, after) Index raw() export access fn() -> access::Policy #[query(…)] #[mutation(…)] GetTaskArgs get_task Op<Args, T, E> name = \"…\" http consistency = linearizable | replica_local max_staleness_ms = N access = public | authenticated | path callers(\"m\", …) timeout = \"250ms\" rename_all = \"camelCase\" bare_arg Option #[derived(…)] DerivedCtx Derived name materialize = always | never | each(CollectionMarker) access = rule fn() -> access::Rule #[interface(name = \"…\", handle = Api, provider = Name?)] #[query] #[mutation] #[derived] static X: Api = Api::import(\"$slot\") try_ <Trait>Provider<P> COMPONENT COMPONENT.http() export!(APP) index(name = \"by_x\", fields(a, b), types(A, B)) types Value targets(A, B) discriminator = \"field\""},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"Application assembly","u":"reference/sdk.html#application","d":0,"x":"#[interface] also accepts client = ClientName to generate native query methods, prepare_* methods and command builders. See generated workflows . #[flower_sdk::application] on an inline module generates its public APP catalogue from lexical collection and method declarations. Mark additional component statics, such as SQL plans, endpoints or component arrays, with #[component] . Export it explicitly with flower_sdk::export!(module::APP) . HTTP exposure and access rules stay explicit on the methods. App::new remains available for custom initialization and composition. See the compiled SQL example .","k":"#[interface] client = ClientName prepare_* #[flower_sdk::application] APP #[component] flower_sdk::export!(module::APP) App::new"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"Contexts","u":"reference/sdk.html#contexts","d":0,"x":"Trait / type Methods ReadCtx (all contexts) get , raw_get , get_derived , scan , raw_scan , range , now , clock , changes_at MethodCtx (queries, mutations) principal , caller , call , try_call MutationCtx set , set_clearing , delete , delete_permanently , delete_if_match , call_mutation , try_mutation , materialize , unmaterialize QueryCtx , MutationCtx , DerivedCtx .crypto() : the host's cryptography ; .ctx() : the raw Ctx , for raw descriptors. Ctx (raw) All of the above over Value s, plus method_call , settle and history . Bring the traits into scope ( use flower_sdk::{ReadCtx, MethodCtx}; ) to call clock , principal and friends on typed contexts. Raw operations take keys as Value s ( &Va","k":"ReadCtx get raw_get get_derived scan raw_scan range now clock changes_at MethodCtx principal caller call try_call MutationCtx set set_clearing delete delete_permanently delete_if_match call_mutation try_mutation materialize unmaterialize QueryCtx DerivedCtx .crypto() .ctx() Ctx Value method_call settle history use flower_sdk::{ReadCtx, MethodCtx}; &Value::String(id) FlowerType encode()"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"typed","u":"reference/sdk.html#typed","d":0,"x":"Item Field<T> Visible(T) or Redacted : a field an access policy may hide. DecimalI64 , DecimalU64 Full-range integers as decimal strings. Row<K, V> , Page<K, V> , decode_page Typed scan results ( rows of typed key and value , cursor ). TypedCollection A collection's Key , Value and NAME ; implemented by #[collection] markers. call_typed , decode_outcome Typed calls to other modules. MAX_SAFE_INTEGER 2 53 − 1.","k":"Field<T> Visible(T) Redacted DecimalI64 DecimalU64 Row<K, V> Page<K, V> decode_page rows key value cursor TypedCollection Key Value NAME #[collection] call_typed decode_outcome MAX_SAFE_INTEGER"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"index","u":"reference/sdk.html#index","d":0,"x":"Item Index<K, V, F> .query() or .prefix(tuple) , then .gt/.gte/.lt/.lte(&bound) , .after(cursor) , .reverse() , .page(ctx, limit) ; .in_module(name) for another module's export. Source<K, V> .get(ctx, &key) , .page(ctx, limit, after) , .in_module(name) . TypedAggregate Source , Fields , Group , Accumulator , NAME , INDEX , VERSION , PAGE ; initial , add , remove ; provides get(ctx, &group) and the DERIVED descriptor. Reduced , ReduceCall , RowChange Raw reducer types.","k":"Index<K, V, F> .query() .prefix(tuple) .gt/.gte/.lt/.lte(&bound) .after(cursor) .reverse() .page(ctx, limit) .in_module(name) Source<K, V> .get(ctx, &key) .page(ctx, limit, after) TypedAggregate Source Fields Group Accumulator NAME INDEX VERSION PAGE initial add remove get(ctx, &group) DERIVED Reduced ReduceCall RowChange"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"sql","u":"reference/sdk.html#sql","d":0,"x":"SqlQuery<P, R> is a registered, deployment-checked statement with typed parameters and rows. SqlEndpoint is a registered capability for request-time SQL over an explicit collection allowlist. Both use SqlBudget::new(candidates, bytes, work) ( scan remains available), and offer require_index , run , explain and explain_typed . Endpoints additionally provide run_as<R> and accept any FlowerType parameter object. SqlExplain exposes common fields and retains the original response in raw . SQL executes with the enclosing invocation's snapshot, staged writes, authority and policies. Neither declaration creates an HTTP alias. Results have no continuation cursor. See SQL queries for supported syntax,","k":"SqlQuery<P, R> SqlEndpoint SqlBudget::new(candidates, bytes, work) scan require_index run explain explain_typed run_as<R> FlowerType SqlExplain raw"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"features","u":"reference/sdk.html#features","d":0,"x":"Item TypedTrigger Source , NAME , run(ctx, key, before, after) ; list its TRIGGER . TypedRetention Source , NAME , retain(ctx, key, value, deleted_at) ; list its RETENTION . TypedTask NAME , due(ctx) , run(ctx) , optional HANDLES_FAILURES and on_error ; list its TASK . raw_set , raw_delete Write Value s from a typed mutation.","k":"TypedTrigger Source NAME run(ctx, key, before, after) TRIGGER TypedRetention retain(ctx, key, value, deleted_at) RETENTION TypedTask due(ctx) run(ctx) HANDLES_FAILURES on_error TASK raw_set raw_delete Value"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"queue and timer","u":"reference/sdk.html#queue-and-timer","d":0,"x":"Item TypedQueue<P, R = Value> static Q: TypedQueue<P, R> = TypedQueue::new(\"name\") , .turn_ms(ms) : the queue's declaration (contracts from P and R ) and its handle — enqueue , claim , renew , complete , finish , fail , kill , release , cancel , get , result , stats , ready . Guests use only the queues their app lists ( queue_undeclared otherwise). QueueDef A raw declaration: QueueDef::new(name).payload(schema).result(schema).turn_ms(ms) . Queue Raw operations by queue name: enqueue , claim , claim_admitting , wait , renew , complete , finish , fail , kill , release , cancel , retry , purge ; reads get , stats , stats_for , ready take any context. EnqueueOptions , ClaimOptions , Job , Claim ","k":"TypedQueue<P, R = Value> static Q: TypedQueue<P, R> = TypedQueue::new(\"name\") .turn_ms(ms) P R enqueue claim renew complete finish fail kill release cancel get result stats ready queue_undeclared QueueDef Queue claim_admitting wait retry purge stats_for EnqueueOptions ClaimOptions Job Claim Lease Item Stats Timers after(ctx, id, Duration, &OP, &args) at(ctx, id, due_ms, &OP, &args) Op schedule(ctx, id, due_ms, &Method, Value, &ScheduleOptions) retry(ctx, id, Duration) scan ScheduleOptions { max_attempts } Timer TimerState"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"auth and access","u":"reference/sdk.html#auth-and-access","d":0,"x":"Item auth::Authorization { authenticate, default, sessions, methods } , a component (at most one per module). default is the access of methods that declare none; methods gives access, by name, to definitions another macro declares ( external! operations, interface providers). The manifest refuses a name the app does not define, one named twice, and one whose definition declares its own access, so cargo flower build fails on a typo. auth::Authentication Typed(&dyn Authenticator) , Callback(fn) , Gateway , Anonymous . auth::Access Public , Authenticated , Check(fn) . auth::JwtBearer , BearerKey , claims_principal Bearer JWT authentication. auth::Authenticator , Authenticated , AuthRequest Writ","k":"auth::Authorization { authenticate, default, sessions, methods } default methods external! cargo flower build auth::Authentication Typed(&dyn Authenticator) Callback(fn) Gateway Anonymous auth::Access Public Authenticated Check(fn) auth::JwtBearer BearerKey claims_principal auth::Authenticator AuthRequest access::Policy FieldAccess Rule Operand"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"crypto","u":"reference/sdk.html#crypto","d":0,"x":"Item Crypto , from ctx.crypto() sha256 , random_bytes , set_prng , managed_sign , managed_verify , managed_public_key , managed_version , sign , verify , public_key , key_version , jwt() , nacl() , webauthn() . Every server has a crypto host: only managed keys need the operator's catalog. KeyDeclaration KeyDeclaration::new(alias, Algorithm, &[Usage]) , a component; .key() is the ManagedKey operations take. ManagedKey , ManagedKeyRef , Key , Algorithm , Usage Keys and their references. crypto::jwt sign , verify , encrypt , decrypt ; JwtKey , KeyFormat , SignOptions , VerifyOptions , ValidationOptions , EncryptOptions , Verified , ProtectedHeader . crypto::webauthn challenge , registration_opt","k":"Crypto ctx.crypto() sha256 random_bytes set_prng managed_sign managed_verify managed_public_key managed_version sign verify public_key key_version jwt() nacl() webauthn() KeyDeclaration KeyDeclaration::new(alias, Algorithm, &[Usage]) .key() ManagedKey ManagedKeyRef Key Algorithm Usage crypto::jwt encrypt decrypt JwtKey KeyFormat SignOptions VerifyOptions ValidationOptions EncryptOptions Verified ProtectedHeader crypto::webauthn challenge registration_options authentication_options verify_registration verify_authentication base64url_encode decode crypto::nacl hex_encode hex_decode"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"schema","u":"reference/sdk.html#schema","d":0,"x":"schema::v builds schemas for raw descriptors: v::string() , number() , int() , boolean() , null() , json() , array(…) , tuple(…) , object(&[…]) , nullable(…) , str_enum(…) , field(name, schema) , optional(name, schema) , with .min , .max , .pattern and .build() . Schema::parse validates with a path; Schema::is tests.","k":"schema::v v::string() number() int() boolean() null() json() array(…) tuple(…) object(&[…]) nullable(…) str_enum(…) field(name, schema) optional(name, schema) .min .max .pattern .build() Schema::parse Schema::is"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"error::codes","u":"reference/sdk.html#error-codes","d":0,"x":"SQL additionally defines SQL_INVALID and SQL_BUDGET ; see SQL failures . Constants for the SDK's own failure codes: TYPE_MISMATCH , MISSING_FIELD , INVALID_VALUE , DEPTH_LIMIT , REDACTED_VALUE , SCHEMA_MISMATCH , INVALID_ARGUMENT , UNKNOWN_METHOD , INVALID_INVOCATION , UNKNOWN_KEY , FORBIDDEN_USAGE , BAD_SIGNATURE , ACCESS_DENIED , UNAUTHENTICATED , FORBIDDEN , MODULE_PRIVATE , MODULE_READ_ONLY , MODULE_CONFLICT , MODULE_CONTRACT_VIOLATION . See Error codes and limits . API docs Every item is documented in the source: run cargo doc -p flower-sdk --open in the Flower repository for the full rustdoc.","k":"SQL_INVALID SQL_BUDGET TYPE_MISMATCH MISSING_FIELD INVALID_VALUE DEPTH_LIMIT REDACTED_VALUE SCHEMA_MISMATCH INVALID_ARGUMENT UNKNOWN_METHOD INVALID_INVOCATION UNKNOWN_KEY FORBIDDEN_USAGE BAD_SIGNATURE ACCESS_DENIED UNAUTHENTICATED FORBIDDEN MODULE_PRIVATE MODULE_READ_ONLY MODULE_CONFLICT MODULE_CONTRACT_VIOLATION cargo doc -p flower-sdk --open"},{"t":"flower-sdk","h":"Absent and nullable members","u":"reference/sdk.html#explicit-members","d":0,"x":"Nullable<T> is a required member whose visible value may be null. Omittable<T> is either Missing or Present(T) ; Omittable<Nullable<T>> represents absence, explicit null and a value separately. Encoding omits missing fields, and decoding preserves the distinction. Field<T> continues to represent policy redaction. Legacy method Option parameters retain their omission default; ordinary Option struct fields remain required. #[interface(name = \"tasks.v1\", handle = TasksApi, client = TasksClient)] optionally generates a native facade with named query methods and prepare_* mutation methods. Bind it with TasksApi::client(remote) . The facade uses flower_sdk::remote::RemoteModule ; the SDK remains i","k":"Nullable<T> Omittable<T> Missing Present(T) Omittable<Nullable<T>> Field<T> Option prepare_* TasksApi::client(remote) flower_sdk::remote::RemoteModule"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Error codes and limits","u":"reference/errors.html","d":1,"x":"Every failure carries a stable code. UPPER_SNAKE_CASE codes come from guests and the SDK — yours among them; lowercase codes come from the engine and server. The HTTP status tells a client what to do.","k":"UPPER_SNAKE_CASE"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Reading a failure","u":"reference/errors.html#reading-a-failure","d":0,"x":"422 is the call's own answer: a code its code chose, bad arguments, a limit it ran out of, its code trapping, and every failure a mutation or transaction recorded. The body is {\"ok\":false,\"code\",\"message\",\"details\"?} , plus revision , position and duplicate for a recorded write. Final: show it, don't retry it. 503 means nothing ran, or the outcome is unknown and another attempt may get through; retry, writes with the same request ID. 400 , 404 , 413 and 415 refuse the request itself; 403 refuses the caller; 409 refuses an operator change; 500 is any other host failure. The authorization component's refusals are wrapped: code unauthorized , message \"<HOOK CODE>: <message>\" . The status column","k":"{\"ok\":false,\"code\",\"message\",\"details\"?} revision position duplicate unauthorized \"<HOOK CODE>: <message>\""},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Codecs and calls","u":"reference/errors.html#codec-and-call","d":0,"x":"Code HTTP Meaning INVALID_ARGUMENT 422 The arguments do not match the method's argument struct, before your code runs; details.path names the member ( [\"id\"] , or member names and indexes). TYPE_MISMATCH — A value has the wrong JSON kind for its type. MISSING_FIELD — A required member is absent. INVALID_VALUE 422 Right kind, invalid content: a non-integer, an unknown member, an out-of-range option, a cursor from another range, a transaction naming a database twice. DEPTH_LIMIT — Nesting deeper than 128. REDACTED_VALUE — Encoding a Field::Redacted . SCHEMA_MISMATCH 422 A value the engine checks against a contract was refused: arguments another module, a timer, a queue or a transaction plan pa","k":"INVALID_ARGUMENT details.path [\"id\"] TYPE_MISMATCH MISSING_FIELD INVALID_VALUE DEPTH_LIMIT REDACTED_VALUE Field::Redacted SCHEMA_MISMATCH UNKNOWN_METHOD http INVALID_INVOCATION /v1/query stale_revision expected_revision cascade_too_deep transaction_too_large --max-transaction-bytes JOB_EXISTS queue_undeclared queue_purging ok:false bad_request"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Authorization and access","u":"reference/errors.html#access","d":0,"x":"Code HTTP Meaning unauthorized 403 The module's authorization component refused the caller; the message starts with its code: UNAUTHENTICATED (no or bad credentials), FORBIDDEN (an Access::Check said no), or your own. ACCESS_DENIED 422 (writes), 403 (queries) An access policy refused a write, or a caller may not read a derived value. forbidden 403 A server endpoint's token is missing or wrong. watch_scope_changed SSE A watcher's principal changed; reconnect.","k":"unauthorized UNAUTHENTICATED FORBIDDEN Access::Check ACCESS_DENIED forbidden watch_scope_changed"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Modules and deployments","u":"reference/errors.html#modules","d":0,"x":"Code HTTP Meaning MODULE_PRIVATE — Another module's item is not exported, or not to this caller. MODULE_READ_ONLY — Writing another module's exported collection. MODULE_CONFLICT 409 A deployment or removal would break a link between modules. MODULE_CONTRACT_VIOLATION — A provider answered outside its declared contract. invalid_module , invalid_manifest 500 The Wasm or its manifest failed validation at deployment: a field reference without a covering index, for example. cargo flower plan runs the same checks offline. deployment , deployment_in_progress 409 A staging step refused, or another deployment of the module is running. host_trap 422 The guest trapped: a panic or an ABI violation. init","k":"MODULE_PRIVATE MODULE_READ_ONLY MODULE_CONFLICT MODULE_CONTRACT_VIOLATION invalid_module invalid_manifest cargo flower plan deployment deployment_in_progress host_trap init_failed init module_unavailable instantiation_failed instances_exhausted aggregate_context_forbidden"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Deadlines and budgets","u":"reference/errors.html#budgets","d":0,"x":"Code HTTP Meaning deadline_exceeded 422 The call ran past its own deadline: its method's timeout , the operator's default, or its transaction's. Declare a longer timeout , or do less per call. budget_exhausted 422 The instruction or host-operation budget derived from the call's deadline ran out: the same on every member. caller_deadline_exceeded 503 The caller's x-flower-timeout-ms passed first. An attempt with more time may succeed. out_of_memory 422 An instance or the invocation hit its memory limit. stack_overflow 422 The Wasm stack overflowed. nesting_too_deep 422 More than 32 nested calls. These end the invocation: savepoints cannot catch them.","k":"deadline_exceeded timeout budget_exhausted caller_deadline_exceeded x-flower-timeout-ms out_of_memory stack_overflow nesting_too_deep"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"SQL failures","u":"reference/errors.html#sql","d":0,"x":"Code HTTP Meaning SQL_INVALID 422 Invalid syntax, logical binding, plan name, limit or unsupported access path. Parser errors include zero-based UTF-8 start_byte and exclusive end_byte offsets. SQL_BUDGET 422 A configured candidate, byte or work ceiling was exhausted. Evaluator failures identify the dimension in details.budget . No partial result is returned. Parameter contract mismatches use INVALID_ARGUMENT ; reading a collection outside an endpoint's scope uses ACCESS_DENIED . SQL failures are separate from the invocation's host-fuel exhaustion. See SQL budgets and limits .","k":"SQL_INVALID start_byte end_byte SQL_BUDGET details.budget INVALID_ARGUMENT ACCESS_DENIED"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Retries and receipts","u":"reference/errors.html#retries","d":0,"x":"Recorded outcomes of a write, answered 422: identity_conflict , epoch_retired , epoch_not_admitted , wrong_history , receipt_released , receipt_budget_exceeded , retry_session_unknown , retry_session_mismatch , retry_session_closed , retry_session_forbidden , retention_not_initialized , already_acknowledged , retry_ack_gap , retry_ack_budget , retention_epoch_moved . See Retries and receipts .","k":"identity_conflict epoch_retired epoch_not_admitted wrong_history receipt_released receipt_budget_exceeded retry_session_unknown retry_session_mismatch retry_session_closed retry_session_forbidden retention_not_initialized already_acknowledged retry_ack_gap retry_ack_budget retention_epoch_moved"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Cluster and availability","u":"reference/errors.html#cluster","d":0,"x":"Code HTTP Meaning Write outcome overloaded 503 Admission refused, or the member holds its limit of watch arguments; honour Retry-After . not run quorum 503 No quorum to prove a read or commit in time. not run forward 503 No leader reachable from this member ( leader names the last known). not run stale_replica 503 A replica-local read past its bound could not prove freshness. — recovering , rejoining 503 The member is catching up after a restart. not run storage_failed , host_io 503 The member's storage failed. not run leadership_transfer 503 Leadership is moving. not run leader_isolated 503 The leader lost its quorum and gave up the writes it held. unknown forward_failed 503 A forward to th","k":"overloaded Retry-After quorum forward leader stale_replica recovering rejoining storage_failed host_io leadership_transfer leader_isolated forward_failed partition_moving partition_moved partition_retired transaction_prepared transaction_conflict membership transfer_failed incompatible_store unavailable"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Cryptography","u":"reference/errors.html#crypto","d":0,"x":"Code Meaning CRYPTO_ERROR A rejected token or signature, a failed decryption, a malformed key, a failed WebAuthn check. UNKNOWN_KEY , UNKNOWN_VERSION The alias is not bound in this database's catalog; the version does not exist. FORBIDDEN_USAGE The module did not declare the key, or the binding does not grant the usage. BAD_SIGNATURE Malformed signature bytes. KEYS_LOCKED The member has no wrapping key mounted ( --key-wrapping-file ), so it serves no catalog key; operator key changes sent to it answer 503 with this code: retry once a member that has the key leads. KEY_REVOKED , KEY_DESTROYED The key version was revoked or destroyed. WEBAUTHN_COUNTER A passkey's signature counter did not incr","k":"CRYPTO_ERROR UNKNOWN_KEY UNKNOWN_VERSION FORBIDDEN_USAGE BAD_SIGNATURE KEYS_LOCKED --key-wrapping-file KEY_REVOKED KEY_DESTROYED WEBAUTHN_COUNTER"},{"t":"Error codes and limits","h":"Limits","u":"reference/errors.html#limits","d":0,"x":"Limit Value Set by Deadline per call the method's timeout , else 5 s; at most 60 s timeout option; --guest-timeout-ms , --guest-max-timeout-ms Caller's deadline at most one day; only shortens x-flower-timeout-ms Transaction deadline 60 s for client-planned steps a planner's declaration Committed effects per write or transaction 32 MiB --max-transaction-bytes Memory per instance / per invocation 128 MiB / 1 GiB --guest-memory-bytes , --guest-invocation-memory-bytes Nested calls 32 levels Derived evaluation nesting 16 levels Value nesting depth 128 Integers ( i64 , u64 ) ±(2 53 − 1) use DecimalI64 / DecimalU64 beyond Call request body (watches included) 2 MiB Deployment request body 64 MiB --d","k":"timeout --guest-timeout-ms --guest-max-timeout-ms x-flower-timeout-ms --max-transaction-bytes --guest-memory-bytes --guest-invocation-memory-bytes i64 u64 DecimalI64 DecimalU64 --deploy-max-bytes --retry-window-ms cargo flower retention --decision-memo-bytes"}];
